window.LESSONS["netplus"] = {"1|OSI model":"# OSI model\n\nThe OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model is a **reference model**. It describes network communication as **seven layers**, each with one job. It isn't a protocol you install. It's a shared vocabulary, so people can say \"that's a Layer 2 problem\" or \"a router is a Layer 3 device\" and be understood. Network+ uses it everywhere: to classify devices, protocols, attacks, and to structure troubleshooting.\n\n## Why the OSI model matters\n\n- **It splits a complicated process into pieces.** Each layer only has to do its own job and hand data to the layer above or below it.\n- **It lets different vendors' equipment work together.** A switch from one company and a NIC from another interoperate because both follow the same layer standards (e.g. Ethernet at Layer 2).\n- **It gives you a troubleshooting map.** You can test one layer at a time instead of guessing.\n- **It's a model, not an implementation.** Real networks run the TCP/IP suite, which maps onto the OSI layers.\n\n## The seven layers\n\nNumbered from the bottom (closest to the wire) to the top (closest to the user):\n\n| # | Layer | Its job | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | **Application** | Network services that applications use | HTTP, DNS, SMTP, FTP, SSH, DHCP |\n| 6 | **Presentation** | Data format: encoding, **encryption**, compression | TLS/SSL (per CompTIA), ASCII, JPEG |\n| 5 | **Session** | Opens, manages, and closes sessions (dialog control, checkpoints) | RPC, NetBIOS sessions |\n| 4 | **Transport** | End-to-end delivery between hosts, **port numbers**, reliability | TCP, UDP |\n| 3 | **Network** | **Logical (IP) addressing** and **routing** between networks | IP, ICMP, IPsec, routers |\n| 2 | **Data Link** | **Framing**, **MAC addressing**, delivery on the local network | Ethernet, 802.1Q VLAN tags, switches, ARP |\n| 1 | **Physical** | Raw **bits** as electrical, light, or radio signals; cables and connectors | Cables, connectors, hubs, repeaters, transceivers |\n\n> **Memory tip:** from Layer 1 up: **P**lease **D**o **N**ot **T**hrow **S**ausage **P**izza **A**way (Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application). From the top down: **A**ll **P**eople **S**eem **T**o **N**eed **D**ata **P**rocessing.\n\nA few details worth knowing:\n\n- **Layer 4:**\n  - **TCP** gives reliable, ordered delivery: handshake, sequence numbers, acknowledgments, retransmission, and **flow control (windowing)**.\n  - **UDP** is fast and connectionless.\n  - Layer 4 also **segments** large application data into smaller pieces.\n- **Layer 2:** has two sublayers.\n  - **LLC** (Logical Link Control) talks to Layer 3.\n  - **MAC** (Media Access Control) handles hardware addresses and access to the medium.\n- **Layer 1:** covers voltages, pinouts, light, radio signals, and cable specifications. It doesn't understand addresses.\n\n## Encapsulation and PDUs\n\nWhen a host sends data, it travels **down** the stack. Each layer adds its own **header** (Layer 2 also adds a **trailer**). This is **encapsulation**. The receiving host strips them off in reverse order going **up** the stack: **decapsulation**.\n\n| Layer | PDU (protocol data unit) name |\n|---|---|\n| 5–7 | Data |\n| 4 | **Segment** (TCP) / **Datagram** (UDP) |\n| 3 | **Packet** |\n| 2 | **Frame** |\n| 1 | **Bits** |\n\n- **The frame trailer** holds the **FCS (frame check sequence)**, a CRC value the receiver uses to detect corrupted frames. That's why \"CRC errors\" point to physical problems.\n- **Order of wrapping:** application data → wrapped into a segment (ports added) → a packet (IP addresses added) → a frame (MAC addresses and FCS added) → sent as bits.\n\n## Addresses and devices by layer\n\n**Which address lives at which layer:**\n- **Port number** → Layer 4 (identifies the application, e.g. 443).\n- **IP address** → Layer 3 (logical, routable, identifies the host's network location).\n- **MAC address** → Layer 2 (hardware address, used for delivery on the local segment).\n\n**Which device works at which layer:**\n\n| Device | Layer(s) | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hub, repeater, media converter, cable | 1 | Repeat or carry bits; no address awareness |\n| Switch, bridge, wireless access point | 2 | Forward frames by **MAC address** |\n| Router | 3 | Forwards packets by **IP address** between networks |\n| Layer 3 (multilayer) switch | 2 and 3 | Switches within VLANs **and** routes between them |\n| Stateful firewall / packet filter | 3–4 | Filters by IP addresses and ports |\n| Next-generation firewall, Layer 7 load balancer, proxy | Up to 7 | Inspects application content (URLs, apps) |\n\n## The TCP/IP model\n\nThe TCP/IP (Internet) model has **four layers** and is how real networks are built. It maps onto OSI like this:\n\n| TCP/IP layer | OSI layers |\n|---|---|\n| Application | 7, 6, 5 |\n| Transport | 4 |\n| Internet | 3 |\n| Network access (link) | 2, 1 |\n\nExam questions almost always use **OSI** numbering. When someone says \"Layer 3\", they mean the OSI Network layer.\n\n## Troubleshooting with the OSI model\n\n- **Bottom-up (1 → 7):** start with cables, link lights, and power. Best when **connectivity is completely down**. Example: no link light → Layer 1.\n- **Top-down (7 → 1):** start with the application. Best when **one app fails but the network seems fine**. Example: one website shows an HTTP 500 error → Layer 7.\n- **Divide and conquer:** start in the middle, usually with **ping (Layer 3)**.\n  - Ping succeeds → Layers 1–3 work, so look higher.\n  - Ping fails → look lower.\n- **Placing common problems:**\n  - Bad cable or connector → **Layer 1**.\n  - MAC flooding, ARP spoofing, VLAN problems, duplex mismatch symptoms (CRC errors on frames) → **Layer 2**.\n  - Wrong IP, gateway, or routing → **Layer 3**.\n  - A port blocked by a firewall → **Layer 4**.\n  - Expired certificate or encryption mismatch → **Layer 6** (CompTIA's view).\n  - Application error → **Layer 7**.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Packet vs frame vs segment:** **frame = Layer 2**, **packet = Layer 3**, **segment = Layer 4**. Routers move *packets*; switches move *frames*.\n- **Session vs Transport:** Transport (4) delivers data between hosts with ports and reliability. Session (5) manages the *conversation* between applications (opening, checkpoints, closing).\n- **Presentation vs Application:** encryption, compression, and character encoding are **Layer 6**. The protocol the app speaks (HTTP, DNS) is **Layer 7**.\n- **Switch vs router layer:** a normal switch is **Layer 2**. Only a *Layer 3/multilayer switch* routes.\n- **Hub vs switch:** a hub (Layer 1) repeats bits out every port. A switch (Layer 2) learns MACs and forwards only where needed.\n- **Numbering direction:** Layer 1 is Physical (bottom), not Application.\n- **\"Data link\" vs \"network\":** MAC addresses are Data Link (2); IP addresses are Network (3).\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Encapsulation / decapsulation:** adding headers going down the stack / removing them going up.\n- **PDU:** the name for data at a layer: data, segment/datagram, packet, frame, bits.\n- **FCS / CRC:** the error-check value in the frame trailer.\n- **LLC / MAC sublayers:** the two halves of the Data Link layer.\n- **Windowing:** TCP flow control that limits unacknowledged data in flight.\n- **Reference model:** a conceptual framework (OSI) rather than software you run.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Expect to name the layer for a device, protocol, PDU, address type, or symptom. Routers = 3, switches = 2, hubs = 1, encryption = 6, ports = 4.","1|Networking appliances":"# Networking appliances\n\nA networking appliance is a device, physical or virtual, that does one networking job: connecting, forwarding, protecting, distributing, or storing. For each one, know **what it does, which OSI layer it works at, and when you'd choose it**.\n\n## Switches and routers\n\n**Switch (Layer 2):**\n- Connects devices **within** a network.\n- Learns which **MAC address** is on which port by reading the *source* MAC of incoming frames, storing it in its **MAC (CAM) table**.\n- Forwards a frame **only out the port** where its destination lives.\n- **Unknown destinations and broadcasts are flooded** out every port in the VLAN (except the one they came in on).\n- **Each switch port is its own collision domain.** All ports in the same VLAN share **one broadcast domain**.\n\n**Router (Layer 3):**\n- Connects **different IP networks** and forwards **packets** using a **routing table** of destination networks.\n- **Does not forward broadcasts by default**, so **each router interface is a separate broadcast domain**.\n- Often also does NAT and acts as the default gateway for its subnets.\n\n**Layer 3 (multilayer) switch:** switches at Layer 2 *and* routes between VLANs at Layer 3. Common at the distribution and core layers.\n\n**Hub / repeater (Layer 1):** repeats signals out every port with no address awareness. Everything shares one collision domain. Obsolete, but still on the exam.\n\n## Firewalls\n\nA firewall enforces rules about which traffic may pass between **zones** of different trust (e.g. internet ↔ internal LAN).\n\n| Type | How it decides |\n|---|---|\n| **Stateless packet filter** | Checks each packet on its own (IP, port, protocol), with no memory of connections |\n| **Stateful firewall** | Tracks connections in a **state table** and automatically allows the **return traffic** of sessions it saw start |\n| **Next-generation firewall (NGFW)** | Stateful, plus **application awareness** (identifies apps regardless of port), built-in **IPS**, URL filtering, and threat intelligence |\n| **Host-based firewall** | Software on one endpoint protecting only that host |\n\n## IDS and IPS\n\nBoth look for attacks using:\n- **Signature-based detection:** matches known attack patterns. Accurate for known threats; misses brand-new (zero-day) attacks.\n- **Anomaly/behavior-based detection:** alerts on deviations from a learned **baseline**. Can catch unknown attacks but produces more **false positives**.\n\n| | IDS (intrusion detection system) | IPS (intrusion prevention system) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Placement | **Out of band**, watching a copy of traffic from a **SPAN port or TAP** | **Inline**, in the traffic path |\n| Action | **Detects and alerts** | **Detects and blocks/drops** |\n| Risk | Misses being stopped | A false positive **blocks legitimate traffic** |\n\n## Load balancers and proxies\n\n**Load balancer:**\n- Spreads client requests across a **pool of servers** behind one **virtual IP (VIP)**, for performance and availability.\n- **Methods:** **round-robin** (take turns), **least connections** (send to the least busy server), weighted.\n- **Health checks** remove failed servers from the pool automatically.\n- **Session persistence / sticky sessions** keep a user on the same server (e.g. a shopping cart).\n- **TLS/SSL offload:** the load balancer does the encryption work instead of the servers.\n\n**Proxy server:** makes requests *on behalf of* clients.\n- **Forward proxy:** serves **internal users** going out to the internet. Caching, **URL/content filtering**, logging.\n- **Reverse proxy:** sits **in front of servers**, receiving outside requests and passing them in. Protects servers and does caching and TLS termination.\n\n## Storage: NAS and SAN\n\n| | **NAS** (network-attached storage) | **SAN** (storage area network) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Access type | **File-level** (shared folders) | **Block-level** (servers see raw disks) |\n| Protocols | **SMB** (Windows), **NFS** (Linux/Unix) | **Fibre Channel**, **iSCSI**, **FCoE** |\n| Network | The normal LAN | Usually a dedicated, high-speed storage network |\n\n- **iSCSI:** carries SCSI disk commands over normal **TCP/IP**.\n- **FCoE:** carries Fibre Channel frames directly over **Ethernet**. Not routable, and needs lossless Ethernet.\n\n## Wireless devices\n\n- **Wireless access point (AP):** bridges wireless clients onto the wired LAN (Layer 2).\n- **Autonomous (thick) AP:** configured individually and works alone.\n- **Lightweight (thin) AP:** managed centrally by a **wireless LAN controller (WLC)**, which handles configuration, firmware, RF tuning, and roaming.\n- **Wireless bridge:** links two sites point-to-point over the air.\n\n## Other appliances to recognize\n\n- **VPN concentrator / headend:** terminates large numbers of VPN tunnels.\n- **UTM (unified threat management):** one box combining firewall, antivirus, content filtering, and VPN, popular with small businesses.\n- **Content delivery network (CDN):** servers worldwide cache content close to users.\n- **Modem:** converts between the provider's medium (cable, DSL) and your Ethernet.\n- **WAN optimizer:** deduplication, compression, and caching to speed up WAN links.\n- **VoIP gateway:** connects an IP phone system to the traditional phone network.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **IDS vs IPS:** IDS **watches and alerts** (passive, out of band). IPS **blocks** (inline). \"Inline\" or \"drops traffic\" → IPS.\n- **Collision vs broadcast domains:** switches break up **collision** domains (per port). **Routers** (and VLANs) break up **broadcast** domains.\n- **Forward vs reverse proxy:** forward = protects and controls **clients**; reverse = protects and fronts **servers**.\n- **NAS vs SAN:** NAS = **files** over the LAN (SMB/NFS); SAN = **blocks** over a storage network (FC/iSCSI).\n- **Stateful vs stateless:** stateful **remembers connections** and allows replies automatically; stateless needs explicit rules both ways.\n- **Load balancer vs proxy:** a load balancer's main job is **spreading load across servers**; a forward proxy's main job is **acting for clients**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **MAC (CAM) table:** a switch's map of MAC addresses to ports.\n- **Flooding:** sending a frame out all ports when the destination is unknown or it's a broadcast.\n- **State table:** a stateful firewall's list of active connections.\n- **False positive:** legitimate activity wrongly flagged as an attack.\n- **Health check:** a load balancer's probe that tests whether a server is responding.\n- **VIP:** the single virtual address clients use to reach a load-balanced service.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Alert only\" → IDS. \"Inline and blocks\" → IPS. \"Different networks\" → router. \"Spread traffic across web servers\" → load balancer. \"File shares\" → NAS. \"Block storage\" → SAN.","1|Cloud concepts":"# Cloud concepts\n\nCloud computing means renting computing resources (servers, storage, networks, software) from a provider over a network and paying for what you use, instead of buying and running everything yourself.\n\n## Service models\n\nThe service model decides **who manages what**. Think of a stack from the physical data center up to your data: the provider takes over more of it as you move from IaaS to SaaS.\n\n| Layer of the stack | On-premises | **IaaS** | **PaaS** | **SaaS** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Data and users | You | You | You | You |\n| Application | You | You | You | Provider |\n| Runtime and middleware | You | You | Provider | Provider |\n| Operating system | You | **You** | Provider | Provider |\n| Virtualization, servers, storage, networking | You | Provider | Provider | Provider |\n| Facility (power, cooling, physical security) | You | Provider | Provider | Provider |\n\n- **IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service):** you rent **virtual machines, storage, and networks**. **You install and patch the OS** and everything above it. Most control, most work. Example: cloud VMs.\n- **PaaS (Platform as a Service):** you deploy **your code**; the provider runs the OS, runtime, and scaling. Example: app hosting platforms.\n- **SaaS (Software as a Service):** you just **use a finished application**, usually in a browser. Example: hosted email and office suites.\n- **Desktop as a Service (DaaS):** virtual desktops hosted by the provider.\n\n## Deployment models\n\n| Model | Who uses it | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Public** | Anyone; many customers share it | Pay-as-you-go, **multitenant**, run by a provider |\n| **Private** | One organization | On-premises or hosted; more control, higher cost |\n| **Hybrid** | Private + public **working together** | E.g. sensitive data on-premises, web front end in public cloud; **cloud bursting** sends overflow demand to public cloud |\n| **Community** | Several organizations with shared needs | E.g. hospitals sharing infrastructure built for the same regulations |\n\n**Multicloud** means using **two or more public providers**. That's different from hybrid, which is specifically private + public.\n\n## Cloud characteristics\n\n- **Elasticity:** resources **scale up and down automatically** with demand.\n- **Scalability:** the general ability to grow.\n- **Multitenancy:** many customers share the same hardware while staying **logically isolated**.\n- **Metered (pay-as-you-go) service:** turns large up-front purchases (CapEx) into ongoing costs (OpEx).\n- **Regions and availability zones:** zones are separate data centers within a region. Spreading workloads across zones gives **high availability**.\n\n## Shared responsibility model\n\nSecurity duties are split:\n- **The provider always secures** the physical facilities, hardware, and virtualization layer.\n- **The customer always owns** their **data, user accounts and permissions (IAM), and access rules** such as security groups.\n- In **IaaS** the customer also patches the guest OS. In **SaaS** the provider handles almost everything except data and users.\n\nMisconfigured customer settings, such as an open security group or a public storage bucket, are a leading cause of cloud breaches.\n\n## Cloud networking\n\n- **VPC (virtual private cloud):** your **logically isolated private network** in the cloud, with your own IP ranges, subnets, and route tables.\n- **Public vs private subnet:** a subnet is **public** when its route table sends **0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway**. Private subnets have no such route.\n- **Internet gateway:** gives public subnets **two-way** internet access.\n- **NAT gateway:** lets private-subnet instances make **outbound** connections (e.g. downloading patches) while **blocking unsolicited inbound** connections.\n- **Network security group / security group:** a **stateful** virtual firewall attached to instances or network interfaces. Return traffic is allowed automatically.\n- **Network ACL:** a **stateless** filter applied to a whole subnet. Return traffic must be explicitly allowed.\n- **Cloud gateways to on-premises:**\n  - A **site-to-site VPN** to a cloud **VPN gateway** gives an encrypted tunnel over the internet.\n  - A **direct connection** (e.g. AWS Direct Connect, Azure ExpressRoute) is a **private dedicated link**, not over the internet, with consistent performance.\n- **VPC peering:** connects two VPCs privately. Peering is **not transitive** (A↔B and B↔C doesn't give A↔C).\n- **Transit gateway:** a central **hub** that connects many VPCs and on-premises networks.\n- **Cloud load balancer:** spreads traffic across instances in multiple zones.\n\n## NFV\n\n**Network functions virtualization (NFV)** runs network functions such as routers, firewalls, and load balancers as **software (VNFs, virtual network functions)** on standard servers instead of dedicated hardware appliances. In the cloud, the virtual firewall image you deploy into your VPC is a VNF.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **IaaS vs PaaS:** if **you patch the OS**, it's IaaS. If you just push code, it's PaaS.\n- **PaaS vs SaaS:** PaaS is for **building** apps; SaaS is a **finished** app you use.\n- **Hybrid vs multicloud:** hybrid = private **plus** public; multicloud = **several public** providers.\n- **Internet gateway vs NAT gateway:** internet gateway = two-way access for public subnets; NAT gateway = **outbound only** for private subnets.\n- **Security group vs network ACL:** security groups are **stateful** and attach to instances; NACLs are **stateless** and apply to subnets.\n- **VPN gateway vs direct connection:** a VPN goes **encrypted over the internet**; a direct connection is a **private dedicated circuit**.\n- **Elasticity vs scalability:** elasticity is **automatic** scaling up *and down* with demand.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **VPC:** an isolated virtual network in the cloud.\n- **Multitenancy:** many customers sharing infrastructure, isolated from each other.\n- **Cloud bursting:** sending overflow from private to public cloud during peaks.\n- **VNF:** a network function running as software.\n- **Availability zone:** an isolated data center within a cloud region.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Customer patches the guest OS\" → IaaS. \"Private subnet needs outbound internet only\" → NAT gateway. \"Stateful rules on an instance\" → security group. \"Private link, not over the internet\" → direct connection.","1|Ports & protocols":"# Ports & protocols\n\nAn IP address gets traffic to the right **host**. A **port number** (Layer 4) gets it to the right **application** on that host. A server listens on a well-known port (e.g. 443 for HTTPS). A client connects from a temporary high-numbered **ephemeral** port, so one PC can hold many connections at once.\n\n## Port ranges and how connections work\n\n| Range | Name | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0–1023 | **Well-known** | Standard services (22, 53, 80, 443…) |\n| 1024–49151 | **Registered** | Vendor applications (1433 SQL Server, 3389 RDP) |\n| 49152–65535 | **Dynamic / ephemeral** | Temporary client source ports |\n\nA connection is identified by **source IP + source port + destination IP + destination port + protocol**. Example: your laptop 192.168.1.20:**51544** → web server 93.184.216.34:**443**/TCP.\n\n## TCP and UDP\n\n| | **TCP** | **UDP** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Connection | **Connection-oriented**: three-way handshake first | **Connectionless**: just sends |\n| Reliability | Sequence numbers, **acknowledgments**, **retransmission**, in-order delivery, flow control (windowing) | None. Lost packets stay lost |\n| Overhead / speed | Higher overhead | Low overhead, fast |\n| Used by | Web, email, file transfer, SSH, RDP | **Voice/video**, DNS queries, DHCP, TFTP, SNMP, syslog, NTP |\n\n- **Three-way handshake:** client **SYN** → server **SYN-ACK** → client **ACK**.\n- **Ending:** **FIN** closes gracefully; **RST** aborts immediately (often meaning \"nothing is listening on that port\").\n- **Why real-time traffic uses UDP:** a retransmitted voice packet arrives too late to be useful. Small losses are better than delay.\n\n## Ports you must know\n\n| Port | Protocol | TCP/UDP | What it does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **20, 21** | FTP | TCP | File transfer, **unencrypted**. 21 = control; 20 = data in active mode |\n| **22** | SSH | TCP | **Encrypted** remote command line |\n| **22** | SFTP | TCP | Encrypted file transfer **inside SSH** |\n| **23** | Telnet | TCP | **Unencrypted** remote command line (insecure) |\n| **25** | SMTP | TCP | **Sends/relays** email between mail servers |\n| **53** | DNS | UDP + TCP | Name resolution. UDP for queries, TCP for zone transfers |\n| **67, 68** | DHCP | UDP | Address assignment. 67 server, 68 client |\n| **69** | TFTP | UDP | Simple file transfer, **no authentication** (device configs, firmware) |\n| **80** | HTTP | TCP | Web, unencrypted |\n| **123** | NTP | UDP | Clock synchronization |\n| **161, 162** | SNMP | UDP | Monitoring. 161 = manager polls agent; 162 = agent sends **traps** |\n| **389** | LDAP | TCP/UDP | Directory queries (Active Directory) |\n| **443** | HTTPS | TCP | Web over **TLS** |\n| **445** | SMB | TCP | Windows file and printer sharing |\n| **514** | Syslog | UDP | Sends logs to a central server |\n| **587** (also 465) | SMTPS | TCP | **Secure** email submission |\n| **636** | LDAPS | TCP | LDAP over TLS |\n| **1433** | SQL Server | TCP | Microsoft SQL Server database |\n| **3389** | RDP | TCP/UDP | Graphical **remote desktop** |\n| **5060, 5061** | SIP | TCP/UDP | **VoIP call setup**. 5061 = SIP over TLS |\n\nCommonly tested extras: POP3 **110** / POP3S **995**, IMAP **143** / IMAPS **993**, Kerberos **88**, TACACS+ **49**, RADIUS **1812/1813**, BGP **179**, IKE **500**.\n\n## IP protocol types\n\nSome protocols sit directly on IP and have a **protocol number** in the IP header, **not a port**:\n\n| Protocol number | Protocol | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **ICMP** | Ping (echo) and error messages (unreachable, time exceeded) |\n| **6** | **TCP** | Reliable transport |\n| **17** | **UDP** | Connectionless transport |\n| **47** | **GRE** | Generic Routing Encapsulation: **tunnels** other traffic (even multicast) **without encryption** |\n| **50** | **ESP** | IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload: **encrypts** (and authenticates) |\n| **51** | **AH** | IPsec Authentication Header: **authenticates, does not encrypt** |\n\nIPsec's **IKE** negotiates the keys and settings on **UDP 500**, switching to **UDP 4500** (NAT traversal) when a NAT device is in the path.\n\n## Secure vs insecure protocols\n\n| Insecure | Secure replacement |\n|---|---|\n| Telnet (23) | **SSH (22)** |\n| HTTP (80) | **HTTPS (443)** |\n| FTP (20/21) | **SFTP (22)** or FTPS (FTP over TLS) |\n| LDAP (389) | **LDAPS (636)** |\n| SMTP (25) for submission | **SMTPS (587/465)** |\n| SNMPv1/v2c | **SNMPv3** (same ports, adds encryption) |\n| TFTP (69) | SFTP/SCP |\n\nInsecure protocols send **credentials in cleartext**, so anyone capturing traffic can read them.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **SFTP vs FTPS:** **SFTP runs over SSH (port 22)**. **FTPS** is old FTP with TLS added.\n- **SMTP vs IMAP/POP3:** SMTP **sends**; IMAP and POP3 **retrieve**. IMAP keeps mail on the server and syncs devices; POP3 downloads it.\n- **SNMP 161 vs 162:** 161 = the manager **asks** (polls); 162 = the agent **tells** (traps).\n- **DHCP 67 vs 68:** server **67**, client **68**.\n- **SIP vs RTP:** SIP **sets up** the call; **RTP** carries the actual audio and video.\n- **Ports vs protocol numbers:** ICMP, GRE, ESP, and AH have **protocol numbers** (1, 47, 50, 51), not ports.\n- **DNS is both:** queries over UDP 53, zone transfers over TCP 53.\n- **TFTP vs FTP:** TFTP uses **UDP 69 with no login**; FTP uses TCP 20/21 with a login.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Ephemeral port:** a temporary high port the client picks as its source port.\n- **Socket:** an IP address plus a port.\n- **Three-way handshake:** SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK.\n- **Cleartext:** data sent unencrypted and readable if captured.\n- **NAT traversal (NAT-T):** IPsec moving to UDP 4500 to pass through NAT.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Use the **Ports & protocols drill** until you can answer instantly in both directions (port → protocol and protocol → port).","1|Traffic types":"# Traffic types\n\nEvery packet has a destination, and the destination address decides **how many devices receive it**. Network+ tests four delivery types and where each is used.\n\n## The four types\n\n| Type | Delivery | Example addresses | In IPv6? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Unicast** | One sender → **one** receiver | Any normal host address | Yes |\n| **Broadcast** | One → **every** host on the local network | 255.255.255.255; MAC FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF | **No** |\n| **Multicast** | One → a **group** of hosts that asked to receive it | 224.0.0.0 – 239.255.255.255 (Class D) | Yes (FF00::/8) |\n| **Anycast** | One → the **nearest** of several hosts sharing one address | Public DNS resolvers, CDNs | Yes |\n\n## Unicast\n\nOrdinary one-to-one traffic: a web request from your laptop to a server, an SSH session, a file download. Most network traffic is unicast. If 500 people watch the same video by unicast, the server sends **500 separate copies**.\n\n## Broadcast\n\n- **What it is:** a broadcast reaches **every device in the broadcast domain**: all hosts in the same VLAN or subnet.\n- **Where it stops:** **switches forward broadcasts** (within the VLAN); **routers do not**. So a router interface or VLAN boundary marks the edge of a broadcast domain.\n- **Limited broadcast (255.255.255.255):** all hosts on the local segment; never routed.\n- **Directed broadcast** (e.g. 192.168.5.255 for 192.168.5.0/24): all hosts on a *specific remote* subnet. Routers block these by default because they were abused in Smurf DoS attacks.\n- **Who uses broadcast:**\n  - **ARP requests:** \"Who has 192.168.1.1?\" The reply comes back **unicast**.\n  - **DHCP Discover:** a client with no IP sends from 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255.\n- **Why it's a problem at scale:** every host must process every broadcast. Big flat networks waste bandwidth and CPU. Splitting them with **VLANs** shrinks broadcast domains.\n\n## Multicast\n\n- **How it works:** the sender transmits **one stream**; the network copies it only toward receivers that **joined the group**. Efficient for **IPTV, live video to many viewers**, and routing protocols (OSPF uses **224.0.0.5** and 224.0.0.6).\n- **IGMP:** hosts use **IGMP** (Internet Group Management Protocol) to tell their router they want to join a group.\n- **IGMP snooping:** switches watch IGMP messages and send multicast only to ports with interested receivers. **Without it, a switch floods multicast like broadcast.**\n- **Multicast MAC addresses:** IPv4 multicast maps to MACs beginning **01:00:5E**; IPv6 multicast maps to **33:33**.\n\n## Anycast\n\n- **How it works:** the **same IP address is advertised from many locations** (using BGP). Normal routing delivers each user's traffic to the **topologically closest** one.\n- **Benefits:** lower latency and resilience. If one site fails, routing sends users to the next closest.\n- **Examples:** public DNS resolvers like 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8, DNS root servers, and CDN edge networks.\n- **IPv6:** anycast is a formal IPv6 address type.\n\n## IPv6 and broadcast\n\nIPv6 **has no broadcast at all**. Functions that used broadcast in IPv4 use multicast instead:\n- **FF02::1:** all nodes on the link.\n- **FF02::2:** all routers on the link.\n- **Neighbor Discovery** (instead of ARP) uses solicited-node multicast.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Multicast vs broadcast:** broadcast hits **everyone** on the segment; multicast reaches **only group members**.\n- **Anycast vs multicast:** anycast delivers to **one** destination (the nearest); multicast delivers to **many**.\n- **ARP:** the request is **broadcast**; the reply is **unicast**.\n- **Broadcast domains vs routers:** routers **stop** broadcasts; switches **pass** them within a VLAN.\n- **\"Which type isn't in IPv6?\"** → **broadcast**.\n- **Directed vs limited broadcast:** directed targets a **remote subnet's** broadcast address; limited (255.255.255.255) stays **local**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Broadcast domain:** the set of devices that receive each other's broadcasts (a VLAN or subnet).\n- **IGMP:** the protocol hosts use to join IPv4 multicast groups.\n- **IGMP snooping:** switch feature that limits multicast to interested ports.\n- **Class D:** 224.0.0.0/4, the IPv4 multicast range.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Stream one video to many viewers efficiently\" → multicast. \"Same address served from many places; users reach the closest\" → anycast. \"Not supported in IPv6\" → broadcast.","1|Transmission media":"# Transmission media\n\nTransmission media is **what carries the signal**: electricity over copper, light over glass, or radio waves through the air. The right choice depends on distance, speed, interference, cost, and environment.\n\n## Twisted-pair copper\n\nEthernet cable has **four pairs** of copper wires. Each pair is **twisted**, so electromagnetic interference (EMI) hits both wires equally and cancels out, and pairs don't leak into each other (**crosstalk**).\n\n- **Maximum length:** **100 meters** (328 ft) per run, typically 90 m in the wall plus 10 m of patch cords. Beyond that, the signal fades (**attenuation**) and errors appear.\n- **UTP vs STP:** UTP (unshielded) is standard. STP (shielded) adds foil or braid to resist EMI in noisy places and **must be grounded**.\n\n| Category | Speed | Max distance at that speed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cat 5e | 1 Gbps | 100 m |\n| Cat 6 | 10 Gbps | **~55 m** (1 Gbps to 100 m) |\n| **Cat 6a** | 10 Gbps | **100 m** |\n| Cat 8 | 25/40 Gbps | ~30 m (data centers) |\n\n**1000BASE-T** (gigabit) uses **all four pairs** at once; older 100BASE-TX used only two.\n\n## Wiring standards and cable types\n\nThe pin order is set by **T568A** or **T568B** (they swap the orange and green pairs).\n\n| Cable | Wiring | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Straight-through** | Same standard on both ends | Normal device-to-switch links |\n| **Crossover** | T568A on one end, T568B on the other | Historically switch-to-switch or PC-to-PC |\n| **Rollover (console)** | Pins fully reversed (1↔8, 2↔7…) | PC to a device's **console port** |\n\n**Auto-MDIX** on modern ports detects and swaps the transmit and receive pairs automatically, so straight-through cables work almost everywhere.\n\n**Jacket (fire) ratings:**\n- **Plenum (CMP):** low smoke and toxic fumes. **Required in air-handling spaces** such as above drop ceilings.\n- **Riser (CMR):** for vertical runs between floors.\n- **Direct burial:** moisture- and crush-resistant, for running underground.\n\n## Fiber optic\n\nFiber carries **light** through glass, so it's **immune to EMI**, goes much farther and faster than copper, and is hard to tap.\n\n| | **Single-mode (SMF)** | **Multimode (MMF)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core size | ~**9 µm** (tiny) | **50 or 62.5 µm** |\n| Light source | **Laser** (1310/1550 nm) | LED or VCSEL (850 nm) |\n| Distance | **Kilometers** | Hundreds of meters |\n| Cost | Optics cost more | Cheaper optics |\n| Example | **10GBASE-LR** (long reach, 10 km) | **10GBASE-SR** (short reach, ~300 m on OM3) |\n\n- **Why multimode is limited:** light takes **multiple paths (modes)** down the wide core and spreads out (**modal dispersion**). Single-mode's narrow core allows one path, so the signal stays clean over long distances.\n- **Multimode grades:** **OM3/OM4** are laser-optimized multimode for 10G and faster.\n- **Handling:** don't bend fiber tighter than its **bend radius**, and keep connectors clean.\n\n## Coaxial, DAC, and AOC\n\n- **Coaxial cable:** a center conductor inside shielding.\n  - **RG-6 (75 Ω)** with **F-type** screw-on connectors carries **cable TV and cable-modem internet**. RG-59 is older and thinner.\n  - Old 10BASE2 Ethernet used thin coax with BNC connectors.\n- **Direct attach copper (DAC):** **twinax** copper with SFP or QSFP modules **permanently attached** to each end. The **cheapest, lowest-power** option for links of a few meters, such as server to top-of-rack switch.\n- **Active optical cable (AOC):** the same idea with **fiber** in the middle. Lighter and reaches longer distances than DAC.\n\n## Wireless media\n\n- **802.11 (Wi-Fi):** radio in the 2.4, 5, and 6 GHz bands. Details are in *Wireless devices*.\n\n| Marketing name | Standard | Bands |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wi-Fi 4 | 802.11n | 2.4 and 5 GHz |\n| Wi-Fi 5 | 802.11ac | 5 GHz only |\n| Wi-Fi 6 / 6E | 802.11ax | 2.4/5 GHz; **6E adds 6 GHz** |\n| Wi-Fi 7 | 802.11be | 2.4, 5, and 6 GHz |\n\n- **Cellular (4G LTE / 5G):** no cabling, quick to deploy anywhere with coverage. Good for temporary sites and backup links. **5G** gives higher speeds and lower latency.\n- **Satellite:**\n  - **Geostationary** satellites orbit ~35,786 km up, so signals travel far: **round-trip latency around 600 ms**, which hurts VoIP and gaming.\n  - **Low-earth-orbit (LEO)** constellations are much closer, with latency in the tens of milliseconds.\n  - Both can be affected by weather.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Cat 6 vs Cat 6a:** both do 10 Gbps, but **only Cat 6a reaches the full 100 m**. Cat 6 manages about 55 m.\n- **Single-mode vs multimode:** single-mode = **small core, laser, long distance**. Multimode = **bigger core, short distance**. SR optics → multimode; LR optics → single-mode.\n- **Plenum vs riser:** plenum is for **air-handling spaces** and can also be used in risers; riser cable is **not** allowed in plenums.\n- **Crossover vs rollover:** crossover connects two network devices directly; rollover connects to a **console port**.\n- **DAC vs AOC:** DAC is **copper** (shortest, cheapest); AOC is **fiber** (longer).\n- **Attenuation vs crosstalk:** attenuation = signal **weakens over distance**; crosstalk = signal **leaks between pairs**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **EMI:** electromagnetic interference from motors, lights, and power lines.\n- **Attenuation:** loss of signal strength over distance.\n- **Crosstalk:** a signal bleeding into an adjacent pair.\n- **Modal dispersion:** light spreading across many paths in multimode fiber.\n- **Auto-MDIX:** automatic swapping of transmit and receive pairs.\n- **Twinax:** the copper used in DAC cables.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Run past electric motors\" → fiber (immune to EMI). \"10 Gbps for 100 m on copper\" → Cat 6a. \"Several kilometers between buildings\" → single-mode. \"Above the drop ceiling\" → plenum.","1|Transceivers & connectors":"# Transceivers & connectors\n\nConnectors attach cables to devices. Transceivers convert electrical signals to light (or copper signaling) in a switch port. On the exam you'll need to **identify connectors by description** and **pick or troubleshoot the right transceiver**.\n\n## Copper connectors\n\n| Connector | Looks like / used for |\n|---|---|\n| **RJ45** (8P8C) | 8-pin plug for **twisted-pair Ethernet** |\n| **RJ11** (6P2C/6P4C) | Smaller plug for **telephone lines and DSL** |\n| **F-type** | **Screws on** to RG-6 coax: **cable TV and cable modems** |\n| **BNC** | **Bayonet twist-lock** coax connector: old Ethernet, video, test equipment |\n\n**Tools:**\n- A **crimper** attaches RJ45 plugs to cable ends.\n- A **punchdown tool** pushes wires into **patch panels, keystone jacks, and 110 blocks**.\n- A **cable stripper** removes the jacket.\n\n## Fiber connectors\n\n| Connector | How to recognize it |\n|---|---|\n| **LC** (Lucent Connector) | **Small**, **push-pull latching tab**, 1.25 mm ferrule. Usually **duplex** (a pair). The most common connector on **SFP/SFP+** optics |\n| **SC** | **Square** body, **push-pull** snap-in, 2.5 mm ferrule |\n| **ST** | **Round** metal, **bayonet twist-lock** (\"stick and twist\"). Older multimode installations |\n| **MPO/MTP** | **Many fibers (12, 24…) in one rectangular ferrule**, for **40/100G parallel optics** and high-density trunks |\n\n**End-face polish:**\n- **UPC** connectors are usually **blue** (single-mode) or beige/aqua (multimode).\n- **APC** connectors are **green**, polished at an **8° angle** to cut back-reflection.\n- **Never mate APC with UPC**: the faces don't meet properly, causing high loss and possible damage.\n\n**MPO polarity** (types A, B, C) must match the system so each transmit fiber lines up with the right receive fiber.\n\n## Transceivers (form factors)\n\nTransceivers are **hot-swappable modules** that slot into switch ports, so one port can use different media and distances.\n\n| Form factor | Speed |\n|---|---|\n| GBIC | 1 Gbps (old, large; replaced by SFP) |\n| **SFP** | 1 Gbps |\n| **SFP+** | 10 Gbps |\n| **SFP28** | 25 Gbps |\n| **QSFP+** | 40 Gbps (4 × 10G lanes) |\n| **QSFP28** | 100 Gbps (4 × 25G lanes) |\n\nEach transceiver also has a **standard** that defines fiber type and reach:\n\n| Standard | Fiber | Wavelength | Reach |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1000BASE-SX / 10GBASE-**SR** | Multimode | 850 nm | Short (hundreds of m) |\n| 1000BASE-LX / 10GBASE-**LR** | Single-mode | 1310 nm | Long (~5–10 km) |\n| 10GBASE-ER | Single-mode | 1550 nm | Extended (~40 km) |\n\n## Making links work\n\nFor a fiber link to come up, **both ends must match**:\n- **Same standard and wavelength:** an SR optic won't talk to an LR optic.\n- **Same fiber type:** multimode with multimode, single-mode with single-mode.\n- **Compatible speed:** a 1G SFP in a 10G link may only come up at 1G, or not at all.\n- **Transmit to receive:** TX on one end must connect to RX on the other (correct **polarity**).\n- **Supported module:** some switches reject third-party optics (\"unsupported transceiver\").\n\nHelpful features:\n- **BiDi (bidirectional) optics** send and receive over **one strand**, using two different wavelengths. Buy them as matched pairs.\n- **DOM/DDM (digital optical monitoring)** reports transmit and receive light levels and temperature. It's great for spotting dirty or failing links.\n- **Media converters** join different media, e.g. copper Ethernet to fiber, to extend a run.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **LC vs SC:** both push-pull. **LC is small** (half-size ferrule); **SC is square and larger**.\n- **ST vs BNC:** both twist-lock bayonets. **ST is fiber**; **BNC is coax**.\n- **RJ11 vs RJ45:** RJ11 is the **smaller phone/DSL** plug; RJ45 is **Ethernet**.\n- **F-type vs BNC:** F-type **screws on** (cable TV/modems); BNC **twists to lock**.\n- **SFP vs SFP+ vs QSFP:** SFP = 1G, SFP+ = 10G, QSFP+ = 40G, QSFP28 = 100G.\n- **SR vs LR:** SR = **multimode, short**; LR = **single-mode, long**. Mixing them = **transceiver mismatch**.\n- **APC vs UPC:** green angled (APC) vs flat (UPC). Don't mix them.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Ferrule:** the precise tube that holds and aligns the fiber in a connector.\n- **Hot-swappable:** can be replaced while the device is powered on.\n- **Duplex connector:** a pair of fibers (one transmit, one receive) joined together.\n- **DOM:** digital optical monitoring of transceiver light levels.\n- **Media converter:** a device that joins two different media types.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Small push-pull fiber → **LC**. Square → **SC**. Round twist-lock fiber → **ST**. Many fibers in one → **MPO**. Screw-on coax → **F-type**. Phone line → **RJ11**.","1|Network topologies":"# Network topologies\n\nA topology is the **shape of a network**: how devices and sites connect. The **physical** topology is how cables actually run. The **logical** topology is how traffic flows. The exam asks you to recognize each topology from a description and pick the right one for a scenario (cost, redundancy, scale).\n\n## Star and hub-and-spoke\n\n- **Star:** every device connects to **one central device**, usually a switch. It's how almost every LAN is built.\n  - **Pros:** easy to add devices and troubleshoot; one bad cable affects only one device.\n  - **Con:** the **central switch is a single point of failure**.\n- **Hub and spoke:** the WAN version of a star. **Branch sites (spokes) connect to a central site (hub)**, usually headquarters, and **not to each other**.\n  - **Pros:** cheap and simple, with central control.\n  - **Cons:** **branch-to-branch traffic must pass through the hub**, adding latency and load. If the hub fails, branches lose contact with each other.\n\n## Mesh\n\n- **Full mesh:** **every node connects directly to every other node**. **Maximum redundancy**: there's always another path and no single point of failure. But the number of links grows fast.\n\n**Full mesh links = n × (n − 1) ÷ 2**\n\n| Sites | Links |\n|---|---|\n| 4 | 6 |\n| 6 | 15 |\n| 10 | 45 |\n\n- **Partial mesh:** only **critical sites** get redundant interconnections; smaller sites connect to one or two others. A balance of cost and redundancy.\n- **Wireless mesh:** access points relay traffic for each other over the air and reroute around failures. Useful where running cable to every AP is hard.\n\n## Point-to-point and point-to-multipoint\n\n- **Point-to-point:** a **dedicated link between exactly two endpoints**, e.g. a leased line between two offices or a wireless bridge between two buildings.\n- **Point-to-multipoint:** **one central site communicates with several remote sites**, common in wireless (one central antenna serving many buildings).\n\n## Hybrid and legacy topologies\n\n- **Hybrid:** a **combination** of topologies, e.g. a star in each building plus a mesh between building switches. Most real networks are hybrids.\n- **Bus** (legacy): all devices on **one shared cable** with terminators at each end. One break or missing terminator takes the whole segment down.\n- **Ring** (legacy): devices form a loop. A break can disrupt the ring unless it's dual-ring.\n\n## Three-tier and collapsed core\n\nLarge campuses use a **hierarchical, three-tier** design so each layer has one clear job:\n\n| Layer | Role | Typical devices and features |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Core** | **High-speed backbone** connecting distribution blocks. Keep it fast, redundant, and **simple**, with no heavy filtering | Fast Layer 3 switches/routers |\n| **Distribution** | **Aggregates access switches**. Applies **policy**: inter-VLAN routing, ACLs, QoS, route summarization | Layer 3 switches |\n| **Access** | Where **end devices connect** (PCs, phones, APs). PoE, port security, VLAN assignment | Access switches |\n\nAccess switches are usually **dual-homed** (uplinked to two distribution switches) for redundancy.\n\n**Collapsed core:** **core and distribution merged into one layer**. It suits **small and medium networks** (e.g. a single building) where a separate core isn't needed, saving cost and complexity.\n\n**Why use a hierarchy:** it scales in modules, isolates faults, and makes troubleshooting predictable.\n\n## Spine and leaf\n\nThe modern **data center** design:\n\n- **Leaf switches** (often **top-of-rack**) connect servers and storage.\n- **Spine switches** form the backbone.\n- **Every leaf connects to every spine.** **Spines don't connect to each other**, and leaves don't connect to each other.\n- Any server reaches any other in the same number of hops (leaf → spine → leaf), so **latency is predictable**. Links are used together with **ECMP** (equal-cost multipath).\n- **Scales out:** add **leaves** for more server ports and **spines** for more bandwidth. With 4 spines, each leaf has **4 uplinks**.\n\n**Traffic directions:**\n- **East-west:** server-to-server traffic **inside** the data center. Spine and leaf is built for this.\n- **North-south:** traffic **entering or leaving** the data center (users ↔ servers).\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Star vs hub and spoke:** same shape. \"Star\" usually means a **LAN around a switch**; \"hub and spoke\" means **WAN sites around HQ**.\n- **Full vs partial mesh:** full = **every** node to every node; partial = only some.\n- **Three-tier vs spine and leaf:** three-tier is a **campus** design (north-south heavy). Spine and leaf is a **data center** design (east-west heavy).\n- **Collapsed core:** **core + distribution** combined, not access + distribution.\n- **Spine and leaf links:** leaf↔spine only, never spine↔spine.\n- **Physical vs logical topology:** a network can be cabled as a star but behave logically differently.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Single point of failure:** one component whose failure takes everything down.\n- **Dual-homed:** connected to two upstream devices for redundancy.\n- **Top-of-rack (ToR):** a switch in each rack that servers connect to.\n- **ECMP:** spreading traffic over several equal-cost paths.\n- **East-west / north-south:** inside the data center / in and out of it.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Highest redundancy\" → full mesh. \"Branches connect only through HQ\" → hub and spoke. \"Data center with predictable server-to-server latency\" → spine and leaf. \"Small campus, fewer layers\" → collapsed core.","1|IPv4 addressing":"# IPv4 addressing\n\nAn IPv4 address is **32 bits**, written as four **octets** (8 bits each) in decimal, e.g. **192.168.10.25**. Each octet ranges from 0 to 255. Every address has two parts:\n\n- **Network portion:** which network the host is on (all hosts on a subnet share it).\n- **Host portion:** which device on that network.\n\nThe **subnet mask** (e.g. 255.255.255.0) or **CIDR prefix** (e.g. /24) says how many bits are network bits.\n\n## Masks and CIDR notation\n\nA mask is a run of **1 bits (network)** followed by **0 bits (host)**. The prefix length counts the 1s:\n\n| Prefix | Mask | Host bits | Usable hosts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| /8 | 255.0.0.0 | 24 | 16,777,214 |\n| /16 | 255.255.0.0 | 16 | 65,534 |\n| /24 | 255.255.255.0 | 8 | 254 |\n| /25 | 255.255.255.128 | 7 | 126 |\n| /26 | 255.255.255.192 | 6 | 62 |\n| /27 | 255.255.255.224 | 5 | 30 |\n| /28 | 255.255.255.240 | 4 | 14 |\n| /29 | 255.255.255.248 | 3 | 6 |\n| /30 | 255.255.255.252 | 2 | 2 |\n\nMemorize the mask octet values for 1–8 bits: **128, 192, 224, 240, 248, 252, 254, 255**.\n\n## Address classes\n\nThe original **classful** system divides addresses by the **first octet**:\n\n| Class | First octet | Leading bits | Default mask | Used for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** | 1–126 | 0 | 255.0.0.0 (/8) | Unicast, very large networks |\n| **B** | 128–191 | 10 | 255.255.0.0 (/16) | Unicast, medium networks |\n| **C** | 192–223 | 110 | 255.255.255.0 (/24) | Unicast, small networks |\n| **D** | 224–239 | 1110 | none | **Multicast** |\n| **E** | 240–255 | 1111 | none | **Experimental** |\n\n- 127 is reserved for loopback, so Class A is usually listed as 1–126.\n- Today networks use **CIDR**, not classes. Classes still matter for exam questions about ranges and default masks.\n\n## Public, private, and special addresses\n\n**Private (RFC 1918) ranges** aren't routed on the internet. Hosts using them need **NAT/PAT** at the edge to get online:\n- **10.0.0.0/8:** 10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255 (Class A)\n- **172.16.0.0/12:** 172.16.0.0 – **172.31**.255.255 (Class B)\n- **192.168.0.0/16:** 192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255 (Class C)\n\nEverything else in Classes A–C is **public** (internet-routable). Watch the edges: **172.15.x.x** and **172.32.x.x** are public, and so is **192.169.x.x**.\n\n**Special addresses:**\n- **Loopback, 127.0.0.0/8** (usually 127.0.0.1): tests the local TCP/IP stack; never leaves the host.\n- **APIPA (link-local), 169.254.0.0/16:** Windows **assigns itself** one of these when it **can't reach a DHCP server**. It only works on the local link, so seeing 169.254.x.x is a classic DHCP-failure clue.\n- **0.0.0.0:** \"no address yet\" (the source of a DHCP Discover). **0.0.0.0/0** means the **default route**.\n- **255.255.255.255:** limited broadcast to the local network.\n\n## Subnetting step by step\n\n**Subnetting** splits one network into smaller ones by **borrowing host bits** for network bits. These formulas cover most questions:\n\n- **Usable hosts per subnet = 2^h − 2**, where h = host bits. Subtract 2 because the **network ID** (first address) and **broadcast** (last address) can't be assigned to hosts.\n- **Number of subnets = 2^b**, where b = borrowed bits.\n- **Block size = 256 − the mask value in the interesting octet** (the last octet with network bits). Subnets start at multiples of the block size.\n\n**Worked example: 192.168.10.77/26**\n1. **Mask:** /26 = 255.255.255.**192**. The interesting octet is the 4th.\n2. **Block size:** 256 − 192 = **64**. Subnets start at .0, .64, .128, .192.\n3. **Find the subnet:** 77 falls between 64 and 127, so it's in the **.64** subnet.\n4. **Network ID:** 192.168.10.**64**. **Broadcast:** the next block minus 1 = 192.168.10.**127**.\n5. **Usable hosts:** .65 – .126 (6 host bits → 2^6 − 2 = **62** hosts).\n\n**Sizing a subnet:**\n- **To fit N hosts:** pick the smallest h where 2^h − 2 ≥ N. For **50 hosts**: 2^6 − 2 = 62 works and 2^5 − 2 = 30 doesn't, so 6 host bits → **/26**.\n- **To get N subnets:** pick the smallest b where 2^b ≥ N. For **6 subnets** from a /24: 2^3 = 8 works → borrow 3 bits → **/27**.\n\n## VLSM and CIDR\n\n- **CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing):** prefixes of any length (/22, /27…) replace fixed class boundaries. CIDR also allows **route summarization (supernetting)**: advertising one route for many contiguous networks. Example: 192.168.0.0/24 through 192.168.3.0/24 (four /24s) summarize to **192.168.0.0/22**.\n- **VLSM (Variable Length Subnet Masking):** using **different mask lengths inside the same network**, sizing each subnet to fit. Example: **/30** for point-to-point router links (exactly 2 usable addresses, no waste) and **/24** for user VLANs. VLSM saves address space.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Usable vs total addresses:** a /26 has **64 addresses** but **62 usable**. Always subtract 2.\n- **Network ID vs first usable host:** the network ID (e.g. .64) can't be assigned. The **first usable** is .65.\n- **Broadcast vs last usable host:** broadcast (.127) can't be assigned. The **last usable** is .126.\n- **172.16/12 boundaries:** private is **172.16 through 172.31** only.\n- **APIPA vs private:** 169.254.x.x is **self-assigned link-local** (a DHCP failure), not an RFC 1918 private range.\n- **Loopback:** the **whole 127.0.0.0/8** is loopback, not just 127.0.0.1.\n- **Class D vs E:** D (224–239) = **multicast**; E (240–255) = **experimental**.\n- **Subnets vs hosts formulas:** subnets = 2^b (no −2); hosts = 2^h − 2.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Octet:** 8 bits of an IPv4 address.\n- **Network ID:** the first address of a subnet; identifies the subnet itself.\n- **Broadcast address:** the last address of a subnet; reaches all its hosts.\n- **Block size:** the increment between subnets in the interesting octet.\n- **Route summarization:** one route covering several contiguous networks.\n- **RFC 1918:** the standard defining private IPv4 ranges.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Practice in the **Subnetting** and **IP classes** drills until the block-size method is automatic. Subnetting appears across several exam objectives.","1|Modern network environments":"# Modern network environments\n\nThis objective covers newer ways of building and securing networks: software-defined control, overlays, zero trust, cloud-delivered security, automation, and IPv6.\n\n## SDN\n\n**Software-defined networking** separates the decision-making from the forwarding hardware and centralizes the decisions in software. Every network device has three logical \"planes\":\n\n| Plane | Job | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Control plane** | **Decides** where traffic should go | Routing protocols, routing tables, STP |\n| **Data (forwarding) plane** | **Actually forwards** packets and frames | Switching ASICs forwarding traffic |\n| **Management plane** | **Configures and monitors** the device | SSH, SNMP, web GUI, APIs |\n\nIn SDN, a central **SDN controller** runs the control plane for many devices and **programs** their forwarding through APIs:\n- **Southbound APIs** (e.g. OpenFlow): controller → network devices.\n- **Northbound APIs** (often REST): applications and orchestration tools → controller.\n\n**Benefits:** centralized, network-wide view; automation; consistent policy.\n\n## SD-WAN\n\n**Software-defined WAN** manages several WAN links at once (MPLS, broadband internet, LTE/5G) from a **central controller**:\n- **Application-aware routing:** steers each application over the best link (e.g. voice over the lowest-latency path, bulk backups over cheap broadband).\n- **Cheaper links:** can replace or supplement expensive MPLS circuits with broadband while protecting critical apps.\n- **Automatic overlays:** builds **encrypted tunnels** between sites and provides zero-touch branch deployment.\n\n## VXLAN\n\n**Virtual Extensible LAN** extends **Layer 2 networks across a Layer 3 network** by **encapsulating Ethernet frames inside UDP** (port **4789**).\n- **Scale:** a 24-bit **VNI (VXLAN Network Identifier)** allows about **16 million** segments, vs **4,094** usable 802.1Q VLANs. That matters for large multi-tenant data centers.\n- **VTEPs (VXLAN tunnel endpoints)**, on leaf switches or hypervisors, wrap and unwrap the traffic.\n- **MTU:** encapsulation adds about **50 bytes**, so the **underlay MTU must be raised** (e.g. 1550+ or jumbo) to avoid fragmentation.\n\n## Zero trust\n\nTraditional networks trusted anything **inside** the perimeter. **Zero trust architecture (ZTA)** assumes the network is already compromised: **\"never trust, always verify.\"**\n- **Every request** is authenticated and authorized using **identity, device health (posture), and context** (location, time).\n- **Least privilege:** access only to the specific resources needed.\n- **Micro-segmentation** limits **lateral movement** if something is compromised.\n- **ZTNA (zero trust network access)** grants access **per application** after verification. A traditional **VPN** places the user **on the network**.\n\n## SASE and SSE\n\n- **SSE (Security Service Edge):** **cloud-delivered security**, including:\n  - **Secure web gateway (SWG):** web filtering.\n  - **CASB (cloud access security broker):** controls use of SaaS apps.\n  - **ZTNA.**\n  - **Firewall as a service.**\n- **SASE (Secure Access Service Edge):** **SSE + SD-WAN networking**, delivered together from the cloud. In short: **SASE = SD-WAN + SSE**.\n\nThese suit organizations whose users and apps are spread out (remote work, SaaS) instead of sitting behind one headquarters firewall.\n\n## Infrastructure as code\n\n**IaC** defines network and system configuration in **machine-readable files** (templates, playbooks) that tools like **Ansible** or **Terraform** apply automatically.\n- **Benefits:** **repeatable, consistent deployments**, **less configuration drift** (re-applying code restores the intended state), faster changes.\n- **Version control (Git)** keeps every change in history, enables **peer review**, and allows easy **rollback**.\n\n## IPv6\n\n**Format:** **128 bits**, written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits. Two shortening rules:\n1. Drop **leading zeros** in each group (0db8 → db8).\n2. Replace **one** run of all-zero groups with **::** (only once per address).\n\nExample: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329 → **2001:db8::ff00:42:8329**\n\n**Address types:**\n\n| Type | Prefix | Like IPv4… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Global unicast** | 2000::/3 | Public addresses |\n| **Unique local** | FC00::/7 (FD00::/8 in practice) | RFC 1918 private |\n| **Link-local** | **FE80::/10** | APIPA; every interface has one |\n| **Multicast** | FF00::/8 | Class D (and replaces broadcast) |\n| **Loopback** | **::1** | 127.0.0.1 |\n| **Unspecified** | :: | 0.0.0.0 |\n\n**How IPv6 works differently:**\n- **No broadcast:** uses **multicast** and **anycast** instead.\n- **NDP (Neighbor Discovery Protocol)**, using ICMPv6, **replaces ARP** and discovers routers through router solicitations and advertisements.\n- **SLAAC:** hosts **configure their own address** from the router advertisement prefix plus an interface ID.\n  - **EUI-64** builds the 64-bit interface ID from the 48-bit MAC by inserting **FFFE** in the middle and flipping the 7th bit.\n  - Many systems use random interface IDs for privacy instead.\n\n**Transition methods:**\n- **Dual stack:** devices run **IPv4 and IPv6 at the same time**. The most common approach.\n- **Tunneling** (6to4, GRE…): carries **IPv6 inside IPv4** across IPv4-only networks.\n- **NAT64** (with DNS64): translates so **IPv6-only clients can reach IPv4-only servers**.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Control vs data vs management plane:** control **decides**, data **forwards**, management **configures**.\n- **Northbound vs southbound:** northbound = **apps ↔ controller**; southbound = **controller ↔ devices**.\n- **SD-WAN vs SDN:** SDN is the general concept (often in the data center); SD-WAN applies it to **branch WAN links**.\n- **SASE vs SSE:** SSE is **security only**; SASE adds **SD-WAN** networking.\n- **ZTNA vs VPN:** ZTNA grants **per-app** access after verification; a VPN typically gives **network-level** access.\n- **VLAN vs VXLAN:** VLAN = **12-bit ID** (4,094), Layer 2 only. VXLAN = **24-bit VNI** (~16 million), Layer 2 **over** Layer 3.\n- **IPv6 link-local vs unique local:** FE80 = link-only (like APIPA); FC00/FD00 = private, routable within the organization.\n- **\"::\" rule:** it can appear **only once** in an address.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Overlay / underlay:** the virtual network (e.g. VXLAN) built on top of the physical routed network.\n- **VTEP:** the VXLAN tunnel endpoint.\n- **Micro-segmentation:** fine-grained isolation down to individual workloads.\n- **Configuration drift:** devices slowly diverging from their intended configuration.\n- **Dual stack:** running IPv4 and IPv6 together.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Never trust, always verify\" → zero trust. \"Steer apps across multiple WAN links\" → SD-WAN. \"Layer 2 over Layer 3, 16 million segments\" → VXLAN. \"SD-WAN + cloud security\" → SASE.","2|Routing technologies":"# Routing technologies\n\nRouters forward **packets between different IP networks**. For every packet, a router looks up the destination IP in its **routing table** and sends the packet toward the best match. This lesson covers how routes get into that table, how the router chooses between them, and the related technologies NAT, FHRP, and subinterfaces.\n\n## The routing table\n\nEach route entry contains:\n- **Destination network and prefix**, e.g. 10.20.0.0/16.\n- **Next hop** (the neighbor router's IP) and/or **exit interface**.\n- **Administrative distance:** how trustworthy the route's source is.\n- **Metric:** the cost of this path, as measured by its routing protocol.\n\n**Where routes come from:**\n- **Connected:** networks on the router's own interfaces.\n- **Static:** typed in by an admin.\n- **Dynamic:** learned from routing protocols.\n\n**Default route:** **0.0.0.0/0**, the \"gateway of last resort\", matches every destination and is used when nothing more specific matches.\n\n**No match and no default route:** the router **drops** the packet and usually sends an **ICMP Destination Unreachable** back to the source.\n\n**TTL:** each router decreases the packet's **TTL** by 1. At 0, the packet is discarded and an ICMP **Time Exceeded** is sent back. This stops routing loops from circulating packets forever, and **traceroute** uses it to discover each hop.\n\n## Static routing\n\nAn admin manually enters routes, e.g. \"to reach 10.50.0.0/16, send to 192.168.1.2\".\n- **Pros:** predictable, no protocol overhead, secure (nothing to spoof).\n- **Cons:** doesn't adapt to failures, and becomes unmanageable in big networks.\n- **Good uses:** small networks, **stub networks** (one way in and out), and a branch's **default route to its ISP**.\n- **Floating static route:** a static route with a **higher administrative distance** than the primary. It stays out of the table until the primary route disappears, then takes over as a **backup**.\n\n## Dynamic routing protocols\n\nRouters share route information automatically and **reconverge** around failures. **Convergence** is when all routers agree on the updated topology.\n\n| | **RIP** | **OSPF** | **EIGRP** | **BGP** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Type | Distance vector | **Link state** | Advanced distance vector (\"hybrid\") | **Path vector** |\n| Metric | **Hop count** (max 15; 16 = unreachable) | **Cost** (from bandwidth) | **Bandwidth + delay** | Path attributes (**AS_PATH**, local preference…) |\n| Scope | Interior (IGP) | Interior (IGP) | Interior (IGP), Cisco origin | **Exterior** (EGP): between autonomous systems |\n| Notes | Slow, legacy | Areas; **Area 0 = backbone**; SPF (Dijkstra) algorithm; fast convergence | DUAL algorithm; keeps **feasible successors** (pre-computed backups) | Runs the internet; **TCP 179** |\n\n**How the types differ:**\n- **Distance vector:** routers tell neighbors their routes and distances (\"routing by rumor\"). Loop prevention includes **split horizon** (don't advertise a route back out the interface it came from).\n- **Link state:** every router learns the **full topology map** and calculates best paths itself, so it **converges faster**.\n- **Path vector (BGP):** chooses routes by path attributes. It rejects any route whose **AS_PATH already contains its own AS number**, which prevents loops between ASes.\n\n**OSPF details:**\n- Routers discover neighbors with **hello** packets (multicast 224.0.0.5).\n- Neighbors must agree on area, subnet, hello/dead timers, authentication, and MTU.\n- Multi-area OSPF connects every area to **Area 0** through **area border routers (ABRs)**.\n- On Ethernet segments a **designated router (DR)** reduces adjacencies.\n\n**BGP details:**\n- An **autonomous system (AS)** is a network under one routing policy, identified by an **ASN**.\n- **eBGP** runs **between** ASes; **iBGP** runs **inside** one AS.\n- You need BGP when **multihoming** to several ISPs with your own public address space. A single-homed office just needs a static default route.\n\n## Route selection\n\nWhen several routes could match a packet, the router decides **in this order**:\n\n**1. Longest prefix match.** The **most specific** route wins, regardless of protocol. For 10.1.1.50, a route to 10.1.1.0/24 beats 10.1.0.0/16, which beats 10.0.0.0/8, which beats 0.0.0.0/0.\n\n**2. Administrative distance (AD).** Only when routes to the **same prefix** come from **different sources**. **Lower AD wins.**\n\n| Route source | Default AD |\n|---|---|\n| Directly connected | **0** |\n| Static | **1** |\n| eBGP | **20** |\n| EIGRP (internal) | **90** |\n| OSPF | **110** |\n| RIP | **120** |\n| iBGP | **200** |\n\n**3. Metric.** Among routes from the **same protocol** to the same prefix, the **lowest metric** wins. If metrics tie, the router can **load-share** across them: **ECMP**, equal-cost multipath.\n\n## NAT and PAT\n\n**Network address translation** rewrites addresses as packets cross the edge, usually translating **private (RFC 1918) addresses to public ones**. It conserves IPv4 addresses and hides internal addressing (but it isn't a firewall).\n\n| Type | Mapping | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Static NAT** | **One-to-one, permanent** | A server that must always be reachable at the same public IP |\n| **Dynamic NAT** | One-to-one, from a **pool** of public IPs, as needed | When the pool runs out, new hosts can't translate |\n| **PAT** (NAT overload) | **Many-to-one**: many private hosts share **one public IP**, told apart by **unique source ports** | Almost every home and office internet connection |\n| **Port forwarding** (static PAT) | Public IP:port → a specific inside host:port | Publishing an internal web server on 443 |\n\n**Terminology:**\n- **Inside local:** the host's real private address.\n- **Inside global:** the public address it's translated to.\n\nA full translation table (port exhaustion) blocks new outbound connections.\n\n## FHRP and virtual IPs\n\nHosts have **one default gateway**. If that router fails, they're cut off. **First hop redundancy protocols** fix this by having two or more routers share a **virtual IP (VIP)** and virtual MAC. **Hosts use the VIP as their gateway**, and whichever router is currently active answers for it.\n\n| Protocol | Roles | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **HSRP** | Active / standby | Cisco-proprietary |\n| **VRRP** | Master / backup | **Open standard** |\n| **GLBP** | Several forwarders | Cisco; adds load balancing |\n\n- **Priority** decides which router is active (highest wins).\n- **Preemption** lets a higher-priority router **take back** the active role when it returns.\n- **Failover** is invisible to hosts because the gateway IP never changes.\n\n**VIP** also appears in **load balancing**: clients connect to one virtual IP that fronts a pool of servers.\n\n## Subinterfaces\n\nA **subinterface** is a logical interface carved out of one physical interface, e.g. **Gi0/0.10** and **Gi0/0.20**.\n- **Router-on-a-stick:** one router port connects to a switch **trunk**. Each subinterface handles one VLAN (`encapsulation dot1Q 10`) and has an IP that serves as that VLAN's gateway. The router routes **between VLANs** over the single link.\n- **On a Layer 3 switch:** the same job is done with **SVIs** (switched virtual interfaces, e.g. `interface vlan 10`).\n- **If one VLAN doesn't route:** check that subinterface's **VLAN tag and IP**, and that the VLAN is **allowed on the trunk**.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Prefix length vs AD:** prefix length is checked **first**. A /24 learned by RIP beats a /16 from OSPF, because they're different prefixes. AD only compares **identical** prefixes.\n- **AD vs metric:** AD compares **different sources**; metric compares paths **within one protocol**.\n- **Lower is better:** for both AD and metric.\n- **Static NAT vs PAT:** static = **one-to-one**; PAT = **many-to-one using ports**.\n- **HSRP vs VRRP:** HSRP is **Cisco** (active/standby); VRRP is the **open standard** (master/backup).\n- **Interior vs exterior:** OSPF/EIGRP/RIP route **inside** an organization; BGP routes **between** organizations.\n- **Link state vs distance vector:** link state knows the **whole map**; distance vector trusts **neighbors' summaries**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Gateway of last resort:** the default route.\n- **Convergence:** all routers agreeing on the topology after a change.\n- **Autonomous system (AS):** a network under one routing policy, with an ASN.\n- **Floating static route:** a backup static route with a higher AD.\n- **ECMP:** load-sharing across equal-cost routes.\n- **SVI:** a routed interface for a VLAN on a Layer 3 switch.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Route selection = **longest prefix → lowest AD → lowest metric**. Memorize the AD table. \"Many hosts, one public IP\" → PAT. \"Gateway survives a router failure\" → FHRP with a VIP.","2|Switching technologies":"# Switching technologies\n\nSwitches connect devices **within** a network. This lesson covers how switches forward frames, how VLANs and trunks divide them, how ports are configured, how spanning tree prevents loops, and what MTU and jumbo frames are.\n\n## How a switch forwards frames\n\n1. **Learn:** when a frame arrives, the switch records its **source MAC** and the **port** it came in on, in the **MAC address (CAM) table**. Entries age out after about 5 minutes without traffic.\n2. **Forward:** if the **destination MAC** is in the table, the switch sends the frame **only out that port**.\n3. **Flood:** if the destination is **unknown**, or the frame is a **broadcast**, the switch sends it **out every port in the VLAN** except the incoming one.\n\n**Switching methods:**\n- **Store-and-forward:** receives the whole frame and checks its FCS before forwarding. Corrupted frames are dropped.\n- **Cut-through:** starts forwarding after reading the destination MAC. Lower latency, but can pass on bad frames.\n\n## VLANs\n\nA **VLAN (virtual LAN)** splits one physical switch into separate **broadcast domains**. Devices in different VLANs can't talk to each other **without a Layer 3 device** (a router or Layer 3 switch) routing between them.\n\n- **Why use them:** security (separating departments, guests, voice, management), smaller broadcast domains, and flexible grouping regardless of physical location.\n- **VLAN IDs:** **1–4094** are usable (0 and 4095 are reserved). **VLAN 1** is the default VLAN, and all ports start there. Best practice is to move users and the native VLAN **off VLAN 1**.\n- **Voice VLAN:** an IP phone and a PC can share one port. The phone **tags** its traffic into the voice VLAN (learned via **CDP or LLDP-MED**) and the PC's traffic stays untagged in the data VLAN. This allows separate QoS and security.\n\n## Access ports and trunks\n\n| | **Access port** | **Trunk port** |\n|---|---|---|\n| VLANs | **One** data VLAN (plus an optional voice VLAN) | **Many** VLANs |\n| Tagging | Sends **untagged** frames to the end device | Adds **802.1Q tags** |\n| Connects to | PCs, printers, phones, APs | Other switches, routers, hypervisors, some APs |\n\n**The 802.1Q tag** is **4 bytes** inserted into the Ethernet frame. It contains a **12-bit VLAN ID** and a **3-bit priority** (802.1p / CoS).\n\n**Native VLAN:**\n- Frames in the native VLAN cross a trunk **untagged**.\n- The native VLAN **must match on both ends**. A **mismatch** leaks traffic between VLANs and logs errors.\n- Set it to an **unused VLAN** (not 1), or **tag it too**, to block **double-tagging VLAN hopping**.\n\n**Other trunk settings:**\n- **Allowed VLAN list:** only carry the VLANs that are needed. A new VLAN must be **allowed on the trunk** or it won't reach other switches.\n- **Disable DTP:** hard-code ports as access or trunk so attackers can't negotiate a trunk (\"switch spoofing\").\n\n## Interface configuration\n\n- **Speed and duplex:**\n  - Set **both ends to auto-negotiate**, or **hard-code both identically**.\n  - If one side is fixed (e.g. 100/full) and the other is on auto, auto-negotiation fails and the auto side falls back to **half duplex**. This is a **duplex mismatch**: **late collisions**, CRC errors, and very slow throughput.\n  - Two ports hard-coded to **different speeds** won't link at all.\n- **Link aggregation (802.3ad / LACP):** bundles several physical links into **one logical port channel** for more bandwidth and redundancy. STP treats it as one link, so all members forward.\n  - **LACP modes:** **active** sends negotiation messages; **passive** only responds. Active–active or active–passive forms a bundle; **passive–passive never does**.\n  - All members must match in **speed, duplex, and VLAN/trunk settings**.\n- **Port security:** limits which or how many **MAC addresses** may use a port. On a violation the port can **shutdown** (err-disable), **restrict** (drop and log), or **protect** (drop silently).\n- **Descriptions:** label every port (e.g. \"Uplink to Core-SW1\").\n- **Unused ports:** **shut them down** and put them in an unused \"parking\" VLAN.\n- **Port status:** **administratively down** = someone disabled it; **err-disabled** = the switch disabled it because of a violation.\n\n## Spanning tree\n\nRedundant links between switches create **Layer 2 loops**. Ethernet frames have **no TTL**, so a loop causes:\n- **Broadcast storms:** broadcasts multiply until links and CPUs are saturated.\n- **MAC address table instability (flapping):** the same MAC appears on different ports.\n- **Duplicate frames.**\n\n**Spanning Tree Protocol (STP, 802.1D)** blocks redundant paths so there's exactly one active path, and **unblocks a backup** if the active one fails.\n\n**How STP builds the tree:**\n1. **Elect the root bridge:** the switch with the **lowest bridge ID** wins. The bridge ID is **priority** (default **32768**) followed by the **MAC address**.\n   - If priorities tie, the **lowest MAC** wins, which is often the oldest, slowest switch.\n   - So **set the priority deliberately**, lower and in steps of 4096, on a core or distribution switch.\n2. **Each non-root switch picks one root port:** its lowest-cost path to the root. Port **cost** depends on link speed; faster = lower.\n3. **Each segment gets one designated port** (forwarding). **All other ports block.**\n\n**Port states:**\n- Classic STP moves ports through **blocking → listening → learning → forwarding**, which takes about **30–50 seconds**.\n- **RSTP (802.1w)** converges in a few seconds.\n- **PVST+ / Rapid PVST+** run a tree **per VLAN**. **MSTP (802.1s)** maps groups of VLANs to instances.\n\n**Protecting STP:**\n- **PortFast** skips straight to forwarding on **edge (host) ports**, so PCs get DHCP quickly.\n- **BPDU guard** **err-disables** an edge port that receives a BPDU, which stops someone plugging in a rogue switch.\n- **Root guard** stops a port from becoming the path to a **new root bridge**.\n\n## MTU and jumbo frames\n\n- **MTU (maximum transmission unit):** the largest payload a frame can carry. Standard Ethernet MTU = **1500 bytes**. The full frame is 1518 bytes, or 1522 with an 802.1Q tag.\n- **Jumbo frames:** frames with an MTU around **9000 bytes**. Fewer, larger frames mean **less overhead and CPU load**, so they're used for **storage (iSCSI/NFS)**, backups, and VM migration.\n- **Every device in the path** (servers, switches, routers) **must support the same larger MTU**. Switches **don't fragment**; they **drop** oversized frames, which show up as **giants**.\n- **Tunnels and overlays** (VXLAN, GRE, IPsec) add headers, so either raise the underlay MTU or lower the MTU/MSS inside the tunnel.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Access vs trunk:** access = **one VLAN, untagged**; trunk = **many VLANs, tagged**.\n- **Native VLAN:** it's the **untagged** VLAN on a **trunk**, not the access VLAN.\n- **Collision vs broadcast domain:** each **switch port** is its own collision domain. Each **VLAN** is one broadcast domain.\n- **Root bridge election:** the **lowest** bridge ID wins, not the highest.\n- **STP vs LACP:** STP **blocks** redundant links to prevent loops; LACP **combines** links so they all forward as one.\n- **Duplex mismatch symptoms:** **late collisions** on the half-duplex side; CRC/runt errors on the full-duplex side.\n- **Admin down vs err-disabled:** a human did it vs the switch did it.\n- **Jumbo frames:** must be enabled **end to end**, not just on servers.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **CAM / MAC address table:** the switch's map of MAC → port.\n- **802.1Q:** the VLAN tagging standard.\n- **BPDU:** bridge protocol data unit, the message STP uses.\n- **Root bridge / root port / designated port:** STP roles.\n- **Port channel:** a logical bundle of aggregated links.\n- **Giant:** a frame larger than the allowed MTU.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Prevent loops\" → STP. \"Untagged traffic on a trunk\" → native VLAN. \"Combine links for bandwidth\" → LACP. \"Storage network performance\" → jumbo frames end to end. \"Late collisions\" → duplex mismatch.","2|Wireless devices":"# Wireless devices\n\nWi-Fi (IEEE 802.11) sends data over radio. Designing and securing it means choosing **frequency bands and channels**, the **network type**, **encryption and authentication**, **antennas**, and the right **access points**.\n\n## Frequency bands\n\n| Band | Range and wall penetration | Non-overlapping channels | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **2.4 GHz** | **Longest** range, best penetration | Only **3** (channels **1, 6, 11** in North America) | Crowded; **microwave ovens, Bluetooth, cordless phones** interfere |\n| **5 GHz** | Medium | **Many** (about 20+ at 20 MHz) | **DFS** channels must move if **radar** is detected |\n| **6 GHz** | **Shortest** range | Lots of clean spectrum | Only **Wi-Fi 6E and Wi-Fi 7** devices can use it |\n\n**Rule of thumb:** higher frequency = more speed and channels, but **less range** and worse penetration through walls.\n\nKeep 2.4 GHz enabled for older and IoT devices that only support it.\n\n## Channels and channel width\n\n- **2.4 GHz:** channels are 5 MHz apart but about 20–22 MHz wide, so **neighbors overlap**. Only **1, 6, and 11** are clear of each other. Use 20 MHz channels there.\n- **Channel bonding:** combining channels into **40, 80, 160 (or 320) MHz** widths gives **more speed per client** but leaves **fewer non-overlapping channels**. Dense offices often use 20/40 MHz on 5 GHz for more total capacity.\n- **Interference types:**\n  - **Co-channel interference:** nearby APs on the **same** channel must take turns (share airtime).\n  - **Adjacent-channel interference:** APs on **overlapping, different** channels (e.g. 3 and 5) corrupt each other's signals.\n- **Band steering:** nudges dual-band clients onto 5/6 GHz.\n- **Regulatory domain:** allowed channels and power levels **vary by country**; set the AP's country correctly.\n\n## 802.11 standards\n\n| Name | Standard | Bands | Key features |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| — | 802.11b / g | 2.4 GHz | Legacy (11 / 54 Mbps) |\n| — | 802.11a | 5 GHz | Legacy (54 Mbps) |\n| **Wi-Fi 4** | **802.11n** | 2.4 + 5 GHz | **MIMO** (multiple antennas/streams), 40 MHz channels |\n| **Wi-Fi 5** | **802.11ac** | **5 GHz only** | Wider channels, downlink **MU-MIMO** |\n| **Wi-Fi 6 / 6E** | **802.11ax** | 2.4/5 GHz; **6E adds 6 GHz** | **OFDMA** (serves many clients per transmission), uplink and downlink MU-MIMO, **Target Wake Time** (battery saving) |\n| **Wi-Fi 7** | **802.11be** | 2.4/5/6 GHz | 320 MHz channels, **multi-link operation** |\n\n- **MIMO:** multiple antennas send several data streams at once.\n- **MU-MIMO:** the AP serves **multiple clients at the same time**.\n- **Access method:** Wi-Fi uses **CSMA/CA** (collision *avoidance*), because radios can't detect collisions while transmitting. **RTS/CTS** helps with \"hidden nodes\".\n\n## Network types and identifiers\n\n- **Infrastructure mode:** clients connect **through an access point**. The normal setup.\n  - **BSS:** one AP and its clients.\n  - **ESS:** **multiple APs sharing one SSID** so clients can **roam** between them.\n- **Ad hoc (IBSS):** clients connect **directly to each other**, with no AP.\n- **Mesh:** some APs connect to other APs **wirelessly** (wireless backhaul). Useful where cabling is hard; each hop costs throughput.\n- **Point-to-point / point-to-multipoint bridges:** link buildings with directional antennas.\n\n**Identifiers:**\n- **SSID:** the network **name** users see.\n- **BSSID:** the **MAC address of an AP's radio** for that SSID. It distinguishes APs that broadcast the same SSID.\n- **Hiding the SSID is not security:** the name still appears in client probes and analyzers.\n\n**SSID planning:** you can map **several SSIDs to different VLANs** (Corp, Guest, IoT) over the AP's trunk uplink. Keep it to a few per radio, because each SSID sends its own beacons and wastes airtime.\n\n## Encryption\n\n| Option | Status |\n|---|---|\n| **WEP** | **Broken**; crackable in minutes. Never use |\n| **WPA (TKIP)** | **Deprecated** |\n| **WPA2** | **AES-CCMP** encryption. Still widely used |\n| **WPA3** | Current best. **Personal mode uses SAE**; **Protected Management Frames** are required |\n| **Enhanced Open (OWE)** | Encrypts **open** networks (e.g. a café) **without a password** |\n\n- **SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals)** replaces WPA2's pre-shared-key handshake. Attackers **can't capture a handshake and guess passwords offline**, and it provides **forward secrecy**.\n- **802.11w (Protected Management Frames)** stops spoofed **deauthentication** attacks.\n- **Disable WPS PIN mode:** the PIN can be brute-forced, revealing the passphrase.\n\n## Authentication and guest networks\n\n| Mode | How users authenticate | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Personal (PSK)** | **One shared passphrase** for everyone | Homes, small offices |\n| **Enterprise (802.1X/EAP)** | **Individual credentials** checked by a **RADIUS** server (often backed by Active Directory) | Businesses. Disable one user without changing everyone's key |\n\n**EAP methods:**\n- **EAP-TLS:** certificates on **both** client and server. The **most secure**.\n- **PEAP:** a TLS tunnel using only a **server** certificate; the user then logs in with a password (MSCHAPv2) inside it.\n\n**Other controls:**\n- **MAC filtering is weak:** MAC addresses are easy to spoof.\n- **Captive portal:** a web page where users must accept terms or log in before getting access.\n\n**Guest network best practice:**\n- A **separate SSID and VLAN**.\n- **Internet-only** firewall rules: no access to internal systems.\n- **Client isolation**, so guests can't reach each other.\n- Optionally a **captive portal**.\n\n## Antennas\n\n| Antenna | Pattern | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Omnidirectional** | **360°** horizontally | General coverage from a ceiling AP |\n| **Directional (Yagi, parabolic dish)** | Narrow, focused beam | **Long-distance links**, building-to-building, long hallways |\n| **Patch / panel** | Semi-directional | Wall-mounted, covering an area in front |\n\n- **Gain (dBi):** how much an antenna focuses energy. Higher gain = longer range in one direction but narrower coverage.\n- **EIRP** = transmit power + antenna gain − cable loss. Regulations cap it.\n- **Polarization** must match on both ends of a point-to-point link.\n\n## Access points and controllers\n\n- **Autonomous (thick) AP:** configured individually and works standalone.\n- **Lightweight (thin) AP:** managed by a **wireless LAN controller (WLC)**, on-premises or cloud, using **CAPWAP**. The controller pushes configuration and firmware and handles **RF management** (channels and power).\n- **Roaming:**\n  - **802.11k** gives neighbor reports.\n  - **802.11v** gives network-assisted roaming.\n  - **802.11r** gives **fast BSS transition**, which matters for voice.\n- **Placement:** ceiling-mounted near the center of the coverage area, away from metal.\n- **Site surveys:** **predictive** (modeled before install), **passive** (listen), **active** (connect and test throughput). Validate after installation.\n- **Transmit power:** more APs at **lower power** usually beats a few APs at maximum power.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Co-channel vs adjacent-channel:** **same** channel = airtime sharing; **overlapping** channels = corruption.\n- **SSID vs BSSID:** the **name** vs the **AP radio's MAC**.\n- **WPA2-Personal vs WPA3-Personal:** PSK handshake vs **SAE** (resists offline cracking).\n- **Personal vs Enterprise:** **one shared password** vs **per-user 802.1X/RADIUS**.\n- **EAP-TLS vs PEAP:** **client + server certificates** vs **server certificate + password**.\n- **Omni vs directional:** **everywhere around** vs **one focused direction**.\n- **Thick vs thin AP:** **standalone** vs **controller-managed**.\n- **Wi-Fi 5 vs Wi-Fi 6:** 802.11ac (**5 GHz only**) vs 802.11ax (**OFDMA**, 2.4 and 5 GHz, and 6 GHz for 6E).\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **DFS:** dynamic frequency selection; avoiding radar on certain 5 GHz channels.\n- **OFDMA:** splitting a channel into resource units to serve many clients at once.\n- **CAPWAP:** the protocol between lightweight APs and their controller.\n- **Captive portal:** a login or terms page before network access.\n- **Client isolation:** blocking wireless clients from talking to each other.\n\n> **Exam tip:** 2.4 GHz → channels 1/6/11. \"Strongest personal security\" → WPA3 (SAE). \"Each employee logs in with their own credentials\" → Enterprise / 802.1X with RADIUS. \"Link two buildings\" → directional antennas.","2|Physical installations":"# Physical installations\n\nNetwork equipment has to live somewhere: closets, racks, and data centers. This objective covers **where** equipment goes, **how it's powered**, and the **environment** it needs.\n\n## Installation locations\n\n- **Demarc (demarcation point):** where the **service provider's responsibility ends and yours begins**, usually where the carrier's line enters the building. Problems on the provider side of the demarc are theirs to fix. A **demarc extension** carries the handoff to your equipment room if it's far away.\n- **MDF (main distribution frame):** the **central wiring location**. It typically holds the **core equipment**, the **demarc connection**, and links to every IDF.\n- **IDF (intermediate distribution frame):** a wiring **closet serving a floor or area**. It holds **access switches and patch panels** and **uplinks to the MDF**. Place IDFs so **horizontal cable runs stay within 90 meters** (100 m including patch cords).\n- **Patch panel:** horizontal cables from wall jacks **punch down** to the back of the panel; short **patch cables** connect the front to switch ports.\n\n## Racks and cabling practice\n\n- **Size:** standard network racks are **19 inches wide**. Height is measured in **rack units**: **1U = 1.75 inches**, and a full rack is often 42U.\n- **Rack types:**\n  - **2-post or 4-post open-frame** racks give easy access and good airflow, but **no physical security**. Use them in locked rooms.\n  - **Enclosed, locking cabinets** **protect equipment and console ports** in shared or open spaces.\n- **Good practice:**\n  - **Heaviest equipment** (UPS units, batteries) goes at the **bottom** for stability.\n  - Install **blanking panels** in empty spaces so hot exhaust doesn't recirculate to the front.\n  - Use **horizontal and vertical cable managers** and **hook-and-loop (Velcro) straps**, not tight zip ties that crush cables.\n  - Respect the **bend radius** (about 4× the cable diameter for UTP; fiber has its own minimum).\n  - **Label both ends of every cable**, matching your cable map.\n  - **Separate data cables from power cables** (EMI), or cross them at 90°.\n  - **Ground and bond** racks for safety and to protect shielded cabling.\n  - Check **floor load ratings** for heavy racks.\n\n## Power\n\n| Device | What it does | How long |\n|---|---|---|\n| **UPS** (uninterruptible power supply) | **Immediate battery power** during an outage, plus **power conditioning** for sags and surges | **Minutes**: enough to ride through a blip, start a generator, or shut down gracefully |\n| **Generator** | Powers the site during **extended** outages | **Hours to days** (starts after a delay, so a UPS bridges the gap) |\n| **PDU** (power distribution unit) | Distributes power to devices in a rack. **Managed/switched PDUs** report per-outlet use and can remotely power-cycle devices | — |\n| **Surge protector** | Clamps **voltage spikes** only | **No runtime** at all |\n\n**UPS types:**\n- **Online (double-conversion):** always runs the load from its inverter. **Cleanest power, zero transfer time.**\n- **Line-interactive / standby:** switch to battery only when power fails.\n\n**Planning:**\n- **Size** UPSs and circuits in **VA and watts**, with growth headroom. Keep **continuous load at or below about 80%** of a circuit's rating.\n- **Redundant power:** connect a device's **two power supplies to different PDUs on different circuits** (A and B feeds), so one failure doesn't take it down.\n- **Voltage:** about **120 V** in North America and **230 V** in Europe. Most network power supplies auto-switch. Telecom gear often uses **−48 V DC**.\n- **Don't daisy-chain** power strips. Use **locking power cords** in racks.\n\n**PoE (Power over Ethernet):**\n\n| Standard | Name | Power at the switch port |\n|---|---|---|\n| 802.3af | PoE | ~15.4 W |\n| 802.3at | PoE+ | ~30 W |\n| 802.3bt | PoE++ | ~60 W (Type 3) / ~90–100 W (Type 4) |\n\n- **Power budget:** a switch has a **total PoE budget** shared by all ports. Adding devices beyond it leaves some unpowered.\n- **PoE injector:** adds power to a single link when the switch has no PoE.\n\n## Environmental factors\n\n- **Temperature:** about **18–27 °C (64–81 °F)** at equipment air intakes (ASHRAE guidance). Overcooling wastes energy; overheating causes failures.\n- **Humidity:** about **40–60% relative humidity**.\n  - **Too low** → **electrostatic discharge (ESD)** risk.\n  - **Too high** → **condensation and corrosion**.\n- **Airflow and hot aisle / cold aisle:** racks face each other so equipment **intakes share a cold aisle** and **exhausts share a hot aisle**. **Containment** keeps the two from mixing. Check each device's **airflow direction** (front-to-back vs back-to-front) matches the layout.\n- **Fire suppression:**\n  - **Clean-agent gas** systems (e.g. FM-200, inert gases) put out fires **without water or residue**, so electronics survive.\n  - **Pre-action sprinklers** keep pipes **dry until a detector triggers**, so a broken sprinkler head alone won't flood the room.\n- **Monitoring sensors:** temperature, humidity, **water/leak**, and smoke sensors that alert staff before damage occurs.\n- **Outdoor equipment:** weatherproof (IP-rated) enclosures, wide temperature tolerance, and **surge/lightning protection**.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **UPS vs generator:** UPS = **instant, short-term** battery. Generator = **long-term**, starts after a delay. They work together.\n- **UPS vs surge protector:** a surge protector gives **no backup power**.\n- **MDF vs IDF:** MDF = **main/central** (core, demarc). IDF = **per floor or area** (access switches).\n- **Demarc:** the **provider/customer boundary**, not the MDF itself.\n- **Low vs high humidity:** low = **static (ESD)**; high = **condensation/corrosion**.\n- **Hot aisle vs cold aisle:** intakes face the **cold** aisle; exhausts face the **hot** aisle.\n- **PoE vs PoE+:** 802.3af ~15.4 W vs **802.3at ~30 W**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Rack unit (U):** 1.75 inches of rack height.\n- **Blanking panel:** a cover for empty rack space that preserves airflow.\n- **Online UPS:** double-conversion UPS with zero transfer time.\n- **PoE budget:** the total power a switch can supply across its ports.\n- **Clean agent:** a gas fire suppressant safe for electronics.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Short outage / graceful shutdown\" → UPS. \"Hours-long outage\" → generator. \"Low humidity risk\" → ESD. \"Above the drop ceiling\" → plenum cable. \"Where provider responsibility ends\" → demarc.","3|Documentation":"# Documentation\n\nDocumentation is how a network is understood by people who didn't build it, and by the people who did, six months later. Good documentation makes **troubleshooting, onboarding, audits, and change planning** faster. It's only useful if it's **accurate**, so **update it as part of every change** and store it centrally with version history.\n\n## Network diagrams\n\n| Diagram | What it shows | Use it when… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Physical diagram** | Actual **devices, ports, cable runs, rooms, and locations** | Tracing a cable, planning a move, replacing hardware |\n| **Logical diagram** | **IP subnets, VLANs, routing domains**, and how traffic **flows** | Adding a VLAN, designing firewall rules, understanding paths |\n| **Rack diagram (rack elevation)** | What sits in **each rack unit**, front and back, including PDUs and patch panels | Installing gear, finding free space, planning power |\n| **Data flow diagram** | How data moves between systems, zones, and apps | Security design and application troubleshooting |\n\nGood diagram habits: **consistent symbols, a legend, device names, and dates or version numbers**.\n\n## Cable maps and port documentation\n\nA **cable map** (port map) records **where every cable goes**: which **wall jack** connects to which **patch panel port** and which **switch port**. Combined with **labels on both ends of every cable**, it lets a technician find a user's switch port in seconds.\n\nWhen a user moves desks and their jack doesn't work, first check that the new jack is **patched to an active switch port**.\n\nDevice records should include **hostname, management IP, role, location, and firmware version**. **Never** store passwords in plain text in documentation; use a password vault.\n\n## Asset inventory and IPAM\n\n**Asset inventory:** a record of every hardware and software asset:\n- **Model and serial number.**\n- **Firmware/software version.**\n- **Location and owner.**\n- **Purchase date, warranty and support contract dates.**\n- **Licenses.**\n\nIt supports **lifecycle planning** (knowing when support ends), **audits**, support calls, and **security**: you can't protect what you don't know about.\n\n**IPAM (IP address management):** one place to plan and track:\n- Subnets and their **utilization**.\n- **Static assignments** (servers, printers, network devices).\n- **DHCP scopes** and **DNS records**.\n\nIPAM prevents **overlapping subnets and duplicate IP addresses**, and utilization reports warn when a scope is close to running out.\n\n## Agreements and policies\n\n| Document | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **SLA** (service level agreement) | **Measurable commitments** between a provider and customer: **uptime %**, response and repair times, and **penalties** if missed |\n| **OLA** (operational level agreement) | An internal agreement between teams that supports an SLA |\n| **MOU** (memorandum of understanding) | A **non-binding** statement of shared goals and responsibilities |\n| **NDA** (non-disclosure agreement) | A **legally binding** promise to keep shared information confidential |\n| **SOW** (statement of work) | The **specific tasks, deliverables, and timeline** for a contracted project |\n| **AUP** (acceptable use policy) | What users **may and may not do** on company systems; users usually sign it |\n| **SOP** (standard operating procedure) | **Step-by-step instructions** for doing a routine task the same way every time |\n\n**Uptime to downtime per year:**\n\n| SLA uptime | Allowed downtime per year |\n|---|---|\n| 99% | ~3.65 days |\n| 99.9% | ~**8.76 hours** |\n| 99.99% | ~**52.6 minutes** |\n| 99.999% (\"five nines\") | ~**5.26 minutes** |\n\n## Baselines\n\nA **baseline** records what **normal** looks like: typical utilization, latency, error rates, CPU and memory, and traffic patterns at different times of day.\n- Without a baseline, you can't tell whether \"the network is slow\" is unusual.\n- With one, **anomalies stand out** and you can plan capacity from trends.\n\n## Wireless surveys\n\n| Survey | How | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Predictive** | **Software models** coverage from floor plans and building materials | **Before** installing, to plan AP count and placement |\n| **Passive** | Walk the site **listening** to all APs: signal strength, noise, channel use | Checking existing coverage and interference |\n| **Active** | **Connect** to the network and measure real **throughput, latency, packet loss, roaming** | Confirming user experience |\n| **Validation** | Post-installation survey | Proving the design works as built |\n\nSurveys produce **heat maps**: color-coded maps of signal strength or SNR over the floor plan. **Re-survey after renovations** or big changes, because new walls, furniture, and neighbors change RF conditions.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Physical vs logical diagram:** physical = **where things are and how they're cabled**; logical = **subnets, VLANs, and traffic flow**.\n- **Rack diagram vs physical diagram:** a rack diagram zooms in on **one rack's U positions**.\n- **SLA vs MOU:** SLA = **measurable, enforceable** commitments; MOU = **non-binding** intent.\n- **NDA vs AUP:** NDA protects **confidential information** between parties; AUP governs **user behavior** on systems.\n- **SOW vs SOP:** SOW = **a project's deliverables**; SOP = **how to do a routine task**.\n- **Passive vs active survey:** passive **listens**; active **connects and tests**.\n- **Asset inventory vs IPAM:** inventory tracks **equipment and licenses**; IPAM tracks **addresses**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Heat map:** a visual map of signal strength across a floor plan.\n- **Baseline:** recorded normal performance used for comparison.\n- **Cable map:** documentation of jack → patch panel → switch port connections.\n- **Five nines:** 99.999% availability (~5 minutes of downtime a year).\n- **Legend:** the key explaining a diagram's symbols.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Shows VLANs and subnets\" → logical diagram. \"What's in each U\" → rack diagram. \"Uptime guarantee with penalties\" → SLA. \"Predict AP placement before install\" → predictive survey.","3|Life-cycle management":"# Life-cycle management\n\nEvery piece of network equipment and software has a life: you **buy it, deploy it, run and maintain it, and eventually retire it**. Life-cycle management keeps everything **supported, patched, licensed, and budgeted**, and makes sure retired gear doesn't leak data or leave holes behind.\n\n## The hardware life cycle\n\n1. **Procure:** choose and buy (consider support terms and expected lifespan).\n2. **Deploy:** install, configure from the baseline, document, and monitor.\n3. **Operate and maintain:** patch, monitor, renew support, replace failed parts.\n4. **Decommission and dispose:** remove securely and update every record.\n\nTracking each asset's stage and key dates in the **asset inventory** makes it possible to **budget refreshes ahead of time** instead of replacing things only after they fail.\n\n## EOL and EOS\n\nVendors publish milestones as a product ages:\n\n| Milestone | What it means | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| **End of sale / EOL (end of life)** | The vendor **stops selling** the product and begins phasing it out | Plan the replacement; spare parts get harder to find |\n| **End of software maintenance** | No more bug-fix releases | Problems you hit won't be fixed |\n| **EOS (end of support / end of service life)** | **No more patches, security fixes, repairs, or technical support** | **Security risk**: newly discovered vulnerabilities **will never be patched** |\n\nIf an EOS device has a serious vulnerability, **compensating controls** (e.g. ACLs limiting who can reach it) reduce risk **short-term**, but the real fix is **replacing it** with a supported model.\n\n## Software management\n\nUpdating firmware and operating systems on network devices safely:\n\n1. **Read the release notes:** known issues, **required upgrade paths** (sometimes you must go through an intermediate version), and feature changes.\n2. **Back up** the current **configuration and software image** so you can roll back.\n3. **Verify the file's integrity:** compare its **hash (e.g. SHA-256)** with the vendor's published value to prove it wasn't corrupted or tampered with.\n4. **Test in a lab** or non-production environment first.\n5. **Schedule through change management** in a **maintenance window**, with a **rollback plan**.\n6. **Verify** after upgrading, and roll back if something breaks.\n\n**Also part of software management:**\n- **Version standardization:** run consistent, approved versions across devices of the same role. This simplifies support and vulnerability assessment. Vendors' **\"recommended\" or long-term releases** are usually more stable than the newest one.\n- **Licensing:** track licenses and subscriptions (e.g. security feature subscriptions). **Expired licenses** can disable features, updates, or support access.\n\n## Decommissioning\n\nRetiring a device safely:\n\n1. **Remove the data:**\n   - **Wipe configurations and storage.** Configs contain credentials, SNMP strings, VPN keys, and network details attackers would love.\n   - For media that held sensitive data, **sanitize** (overwrite, degauss) or **physically destroy** it, and obtain a **certificate of destruction**.\n2. **Remove leftovers:** delete the device's **accounts, certificates, firewall rules, VPN tunnels, DNS entries, and monitoring entries**. Orphaned rules and credentials widen the attack surface.\n3. **Update records:** asset inventory, network diagrams, IPAM, and documentation, so nobody wastes time chasing a device that no longer exists.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **EOL vs EOS:** **EOL = can't buy it anymore. EOS = no more patches or support.** EOS is the security risk.\n- **Compensating controls vs replacement:** ACLs only buy time; **replacing EOS gear** is the fix.\n- **Hash verification vs version check:** a hash proves the file is **authentic and uncorrupted**, not that it's the newest version.\n- **Newest vs recommended release:** the newest isn't always the most stable; vendors mark recommended versions.\n- **Formatting vs sanitizing:** a quick format may leave recoverable data. **Sanitize or destroy** sensitive media.\n- **Decommissioning isn't just unplugging:** remove the rules, accounts, and records too.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **EOL / EOS:** end of life (sale) / end of support.\n- **Release notes:** vendor documentation of changes and known issues in a version.\n- **Hash:** a fingerprint (e.g. SHA-256) used to verify file integrity.\n- **Compensating control:** a temporary measure that reduces risk when the ideal fix isn't possible yet.\n- **Certificate of destruction:** proof that media was destroyed.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Vendor will no longer release patches\" → EOS, so replace it. Before any upgrade: **read release notes, back up, verify the hash, test, and have a rollback plan**.","3|Change management":"# Change management\n\nMost outages are caused by **changes**: a config tweak, a firmware upgrade, a moved cable. **Change management** is the process that controls **how changes are requested, reviewed, approved, scheduled, carried out, verified, and documented**, so they happen safely and leave a clear record.\n\n## Why change management exists\n\n- **Fewer unplanned outages:** changes are reviewed and tested first.\n- **Coordination:** people affected are warned and changes don't collide.\n- **A rollback plan** exists if something goes wrong.\n- **An audit trail:** who changed what, when, why, and who approved it. This is essential for troubleshooting (\"**what changed?**\") and for **compliance audits**.\n\n## The request process\n\n1. **Submit a change request** (usually a ticket) that includes:\n   - **What** will change and **why** (justification).\n   - **Affected systems and users.**\n   - **Risk and impact assessment:** what could go wrong and who would notice.\n   - **Implementation steps and schedule.**\n   - **Test plan:** how you'll prove it worked.\n   - **Rollback (backout) plan:** how to undo it if it fails.\n2. **Review and approval:** significant changes go to the **change advisory board (CAB)**, a group that weighs risk, timing, and readiness.\n3. **Schedule** in an agreed **maintenance window** (a low-impact time), and **notify** affected users and stakeholders.\n4. **Implement**, ideally after **testing in a lab or staging environment**.\n5. **Verify** the change worked as intended. If it didn't, **execute the rollback plan**.\n6. **Document and close:** record the outcome, update documentation and configuration backups, and hold a **post-implementation review** for lessons learned.\n\n## Types of change\n\n| Type | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Standard** | **Low-risk, routine, pre-approved**, with a documented procedure. No CAB review each time | Assigning a user's port to a VLAN using the SOP |\n| **Normal** | Goes through the **full review and CAB approval** | Upgrading core switch firmware |\n| **Emergency** | **Urgent** fix for an outage or critical vulnerability. **Expedited approval**, but **still documented and reviewed afterward** | Patching an actively exploited firewall flaw |\n\n## Freezes, unauthorized changes, and tracking\n\n- **Change freeze:** a period (e.g. year-end, a product launch) when **non-emergency changes aren't allowed** because the business can't risk disruption.\n- **Unauthorized change:** any change made **outside the process**. It's a risk in itself and may point to a policy violation or even **compromise**. Configuration comparison tools help spot them.\n- **Tracking:**\n  - **Ticketing systems** track requests through each stage.\n  - **Version-controlled configurations** show exactly what changed between versions and allow restoring earlier ones.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Standard vs normal change:** standard = **pre-approved routine**; normal = **needs review each time**.\n- **Emergency change ≠ no process:** it's **faster approval**, not skipping documentation.\n- **Rollback plan vs test plan:** the test plan **proves success**; the rollback plan **undoes failure**.\n- **Maintenance window vs change freeze:** a window is **when changes are allowed**; a freeze is **when they aren't**.\n- **CAB:** approves changes; it doesn't implement them.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **CAB:** change advisory board.\n- **Rollback / backout plan:** steps to restore the previous state.\n- **Maintenance window:** an agreed low-impact time for changes.\n- **Post-implementation review:** a look back at a change's outcome and lessons.\n- **Audit trail:** the recorded history of changes and approvals.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Every change request needs a **rollback plan**. One of the first troubleshooting questions is **\"What changed?\"**, and change records answer it.","3|Configuration management":"# Configuration management\n\nConfiguration management keeps every device's settings **known, consistent, backed up, and recoverable**. Network+ focuses on three configurations: **production**, **backup**, and **baseline**.\n\n## Production, backup, and baseline\n\n| Configuration | What it is | Key practices |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Production** | What's **running on the device and serving live traffic** right now | Change it **only through change management**; protect access to it |\n| **Backup** | **Saved copies** of configurations used to **restore** after a failure or bad change | **Automatic, scheduled** backups; **multiple versions**; stored **off the device**; **test restores** |\n| **Baseline (\"golden\")** | The **approved standard configuration** for a device role (e.g. \"access switch\") | Build new devices from it; **compare running configs against it**; review it periodically |\n\n## Running vs startup configuration\n\nOn most network devices, such as Cisco IOS:\n- **Running configuration:** in **memory (RAM)**, active right now. Your changes take effect here immediately.\n- **Startup configuration:** saved in **NVRAM** and **loaded when the device boots**.\n\nIf you make changes and **don't save them**, they're **lost on reboot**: the device comes back with the old startup config. After verifying a change, save it (e.g. `copy running-config startup-config`).\n\n**Commit-based systems:** some platforms stage changes in a **candidate configuration** that only becomes active when you **commit** it. **Commit confirmed** / rollback timers **automatically revert** a change if you don't confirm it in time, which protects you from locking yourself out.\n\n## Configuration drift\n\n**Drift** happens when devices gradually **diverge from their baseline**: one-off tweaks, emergency fixes that were never documented, forgotten test settings.\n- **Problems it causes:** unpredictable behavior, security gaps, and harder troubleshooting.\n- **Detection:** **configuration management/compliance tools** compare (**diff**) running configs against the baseline across hundreds of devices and report differences.\n- **Investigation:** a difference with **no matching change ticket** could be an **unauthorized change**, or even a compromise.\n- **Automation:** templates, infrastructure as code, and tools like Ansible deploy and re-apply the baseline consistently.\n\n## Backup and restore practices\n\n- **Schedule** automatic backups (e.g. nightly) so a recent copy always exists.\n- **Keep several versions** so you can restore to a point **before** a problem started, even if it's noticed days later.\n- **Store backups off the device**, in an **access-controlled** location with **versioning** (e.g. a Git repository).\n- **Transfer securely** with **SCP or SFTP**, not TFTP or FTP.\n- **Protect secrets:** configs contain password hashes, SNMP strings, and keys. Use hashed or encrypted secrets and restrict who can read backups.\n- **Test restores** in a lab periodically. An untested backup may be incomplete when you need it.\n- **Troubleshooting:** **diff** the current config against the **last known-good backup**. It often reveals the cause immediately.\n\n## Supporting tools and settings\n\n- **Templates and zero-touch provisioning (ZTP):** new devices **download their configuration automatically** on first boot, so large deployments are fast and consistent.\n- **CMDB (configuration management database):** records configuration items and their **relationships and dependencies**, which helps assess the impact of a change.\n- **NTP:** synchronized time gives accurate timestamps for logs and change records.\n- **After an emergency change:** **document it, back up the new config**, and decide through change management whether to update the baseline.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Running vs startup:** running = **active in RAM**; startup = **saved, loaded at boot**. Unsaved changes vanish after a reboot.\n- **Backup vs baseline:** a backup is **a copy of what a device had**; a baseline is **what it should have**.\n- **Production vs baseline:** production is **what's actually running**; drift is the gap between production and baseline.\n- **TFTP vs SCP/SFTP for backups:** TFTP is **unencrypted and unauthenticated**; use SCP/SFTP.\n- **Golden config vs golden image:** config = **settings**; image = **approved OS/firmware version**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Golden configuration:** the approved baseline config.\n- **Configuration drift:** divergence from the baseline over time.\n- **Diff:** a comparison showing what changed between two configs.\n- **Candidate configuration:** staged changes awaiting commit.\n- **ZTP:** zero-touch provisioning.\n- **CMDB:** configuration management database.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Changes disappeared after a reboot\" → running config wasn't saved. \"Devices no longer match the standard\" → configuration drift; compare against the baseline. Back up with SCP/SFTP and keep multiple versions.","3|Network monitoring":"# Network monitoring\n\nMonitoring tells you what the network is doing **right now** and what is **normal**, so you can spot problems before users call. This lesson covers the monitoring solutions Network+ names, and the methods behind them: **SNMP**, **flow data**, **packet capture** with **port mirroring**, **baselines and alerts**, **log aggregation** (syslog and SIEM), and **API integration**.\n\n## Monitoring solutions\n\nNetwork+ groups monitoring into five jobs. Each answers a different question:\n\n| Solution | Question it answers | Typical method |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Network discovery** | What devices are on the network, and how are they connected? | Scans, SNMP, LLDP/CDP; run **ad hoc** (one-off) or **scheduled** (regular) |\n| **Traffic analysis** | Who is talking to whom, using which apps, and how much? | **Flow data** (NetFlow/IPFIX/sFlow), packet capture |\n| **Performance monitoring** | Is the network fast and healthy? | SNMP counters: utilization, latency, jitter, loss, errors, CPU |\n| **Availability monitoring** | Is it up? | **ICMP ping**, SNMP up/down status, service checks, **synthetic** transactions |\n| **Configuration monitoring** | Has a device's config changed or drifted? | Config backups and **diffs** against the baseline |\n\n- **Scheduled discovery** catches devices that appear later, such as a rogue switch or an unauthorized AP. **Ad hoc discovery** is a one-time scan, for example when you inherit an undocumented network.\n- **Synthetic monitoring** simulates a user (logs in, loads a page) on a schedule, so you learn a service is broken even if the server still answers ping.\n- A **network management system (NMS)** pulls these together on one dashboard with topology maps, status, and alerts.\n- Put monitoring and management traffic on a **dedicated management network or VLAN** to protect it from user traffic.\n\n## SNMP components and operations\n\n**SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol)** is the classic way an NMS reads device health.\n\n- **Manager (NMS):** the monitoring server that asks for data and receives alerts.\n- **Agent:** software on the managed device (switch, router, printer, UPS) that answers requests and sends alerts.\n- **MIB (Management Information Base):** the \"dictionary\" that defines what a device can report or set.\n- **OID (Object Identifier):** the unique numeric address of one value in the MIB tree, e.g. `1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10` is an interface's input octets.\n\n**Operations:**\n\n| Operation | Direction | Port | What it does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Get / GetNext / GetBulk** | Manager → agent | UDP **161** | Read one value, the next value, or many values at once |\n| **Walk** | Manager → agent | UDP 161 | Repeated GetNext/GetBulk to read a whole subtree; useful to see what a device exposes |\n| **Set** | Manager → agent | UDP 161 | **Change** a writable value (needs read-write access) |\n| **Trap** | Agent → manager | UDP **162** | **Unsolicited** alert (e.g. link down); fire-and-forget |\n| **Inform** | Agent → manager | UDP 162 | Like a trap but **acknowledged**, and resent until it is |\n\n**Polling vs traps:** polling every few minutes builds history (graphs), but you only learn of a failure at the next poll. Traps tell you **immediately** when something happens. Most NMSs use both.\n\n## SNMP versions and security\n\n| Version | Authentication | Encryption | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **v1** | Community string, sent in **cleartext** | None | Original version |\n| **v2c** | Community string, **cleartext** | None | Added **GetBulk** and **Informs**; still the most common |\n| **v3** | **Usernames** with hashed authentication (e.g. SHA) | Yes (e.g. **AES**) | The **secure** version |\n\n**Community strings** are shared passwords: a **read-only (RO)** string lets the manager read, and a **read-write (RW)** string also allows Set. **\"public\"** (RO) and **\"private\"** (RW) are well-known defaults that attackers try first. Anyone sniffing v1/v2c traffic can read the string.\n\n**SNMPv3 security levels:**\n- **noAuthNoPriv:** username only, no authentication or encryption.\n- **authNoPriv:** authenticated, but data isn't encrypted.\n- **authPriv:** **authenticated and encrypted**. This is what \"secure SNMP\" means.\n\n**Hardening:** use SNMPv3 authPriv; if v2c is unavoidable, change the default strings, use **read-only** where possible, and restrict which manager IPs may query (ACLs).\n\n## Flow data and traffic analysis\n\n**Flow data** summarizes **conversations** instead of saving every packet. A **flow record** typically holds source and destination IP, source and destination port, protocol, **byte and packet counts**, timestamps, and the interface.\n\n- **NetFlow** (Cisco origin): the router or switch (**exporter**) tracks flows and sends records to a **collector**; an **analyzer** turns them into reports.\n- **IPFIX:** the IETF **standard** version, based on NetFlow v9.\n- **sFlow:** **samples** packets statistically (e.g. 1 in 1,000) instead of tracking every flow, so it has **low overhead** on very fast links but is less precise.\n\n**What it's good for:**\n- Finding **top talkers** (\"Who is eating the WAN link?\").\n- **Capacity planning** using historical trends.\n- Spotting **anomalies**, such as a host suddenly sending large amounts of data at night (possible exfiltration) or scanning many ports.\n\n**Limits:** flow data shows **who, when, and how much**, but **not the payload**. Port numbers alone can't always identify an app (most traffic is on 443), so **application-aware** analysis (deep packet inspection, Cisco NBAR) can classify traffic by app.\n\n## Packet capture and port mirroring\n\n**Packet capture** records **full packets**, headers and payload, for deep analysis in a **protocol analyzer** such as **Wireshark** or **tcpdump**. It lets you see TCP handshakes, retransmissions, DNS queries, and exact error messages that counters and flows can't show.\n\n**Limits:** captures use **a lot of storage**, and **encrypted payloads (TLS)** can't be read without keys. Capture only what you need, using filters.\n\n**Getting the traffic to the analyzer:** a switch only sends a frame out the port where the destination lives, so plugging a laptop into a random port sees almost nothing. You need a copy of the traffic:\n\n| Method | How it works | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Port mirroring (SPAN)** | The switch **copies** traffic from chosen ports or VLANs to a monitor port | Free, configured in software | Can **drop frames** if the monitor port is **oversubscribed**; usually drops errored frames; uses switch CPU |\n| **RSPAN / ERSPAN** | Sends the mirrored copy to a monitor port on **another switch** (RSPAN over a VLAN, ERSPAN in a GRE tunnel across routed networks) | Analyzer can be elsewhere | Same oversubscription risk |\n| **Network TAP** | A **hardware** device inserted in the link that **passively copies everything** | Sees **all** traffic, **including errors**; no switch config needed | Costs money; installing it briefly breaks the link |\n\nScenario: a security team needs a permanent, lossless copy of the internet uplink for an IDS → **TAP**. A tech needs a quick capture of one user's port today → **SPAN**.\n\n## Baselines, metrics and alerting\n\nA **baseline** is a record of **normal** performance over time (e.g. two weeks, including business hours and backups). Without a baseline you can't tell whether 70% utilization is normal or a problem.\n\n**Key metrics:**\n- **Bandwidth vs throughput:** bandwidth is the **theoretical capacity** of the link (e.g. 1 Gbps); **throughput** is the **actual data rate achieved**. Utilization is throughput as a percentage of bandwidth.\n- **Latency:** delay from source to destination (ms).\n- **Jitter:** **variation** in latency between packets. Hurts voice and video.\n- **Packet loss:** percentage of packets that never arrive.\n- **Interface errors:** **CRC errors** and runts usually mean a **Layer 1** problem (bad cable, interference, duplex mismatch).\n- **Discards (drops):** packets the device threw away even though they were valid, usually because of **congestion** (buffers full).\n- **Device health:** **CPU, memory, temperature**, and **up/down status**. Sustained high CPU can point to a loop, an attack, or misconfiguration.\n\n**Thresholds and alerting:** set thresholds (e.g. utilization over 80% for 10 minutes, any interface down) so the NMS **notifies** staff by email, text, or ticket. **Anomaly alerting** compares live data to the baseline and alerts on unusual behavior even when no fixed threshold is crossed. Tune alerts so staff don't suffer **alert fatigue** and start ignoring them.\n\n**Trending:** months of utilization history show when a WAN link will need an upgrade (**capacity planning**).\n\n## Logs, syslog and SIEM\n\n**Syslog** sends log messages from devices to a central **syslog collector (server)**, traditionally on **UDP 514** (TCP or TLS can be used for reliability and security). Each message has a **facility** (source type) and a **severity**:\n\n| Level | Name | Level | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Emergency | 4 | Warning |\n| 1 | Alert | 5 | Notice |\n| 2 | Critical | 6 | Informational |\n| 3 | Error | 7 | Debug |\n\n- **Lower number = more severe.** Setting a logging level sends **that level and everything more severe** (lower numbers). Level 3 sends Error, Critical, Alert, and Emergency.\n- **Debug (7)** produces huge volumes and raises CPU; use it briefly on production devices.\n- Memory aid: \"**E**very **A**wesome **C**isco **E**ngineer **W**ill **N**eed **I**ce cream **D**aily.\"\n\n**Log aggregation** collects logs from many devices into one place for **searching, correlation, and retention**. Logs stored only on a device are lost when it reboots or is compromised.\n\nA **SIEM (security information and event management)** goes further: it **correlates** events from many sources (firewalls, servers, switches, authentication) to detect threats, e.g. many failed logins followed by one success from a new country. It also provides dashboards, alerts, and long-term retention for **compliance**.\n\n**Time matters:** all devices must use **NTP** so timestamps line up. Without synchronized clocks, you can't put events from different devices in the right order.\n\n## API integration and automation\n\nModern monitoring tools talk to other systems through **APIs**:\n- **REST APIs** (usually **JSON** over HTTPS) let tools pull data or push changes. Example: the NMS calls the ticketing system's API to **open a ticket** automatically when a link goes down.\n- **Webhooks** are \"reverse APIs\": when an event happens, the tool **sends an HTTP POST** to a URL you configured, e.g. posting an alert into a chat channel.\n- **Enrichment:** a SIEM can call a threat-intelligence API to look up an IP address in an alert.\n- **Automation:** an alert can trigger a script or playbook, such as gathering diagnostics or disabling a compromised port.\n\n**Streaming telemetry** reverses SNMP polling: devices **push** structured data continuously (every few seconds) to a collector. It gives far more detail with less load than frequent polling.\n\nSecure API access with **tokens or keys over HTTPS** and **least-privilege** permissions.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Trap vs inform:** both are agent-initiated alerts on UDP 162; a **trap** is fire-and-forget, an **inform** is **acknowledged**.\n- **Poll vs trap ports:** the manager polls agents on **UDP 161**; agents send traps to the manager on **UDP 162**.\n- **MIB vs OID:** the **MIB** is the whole dictionary; an **OID** is one entry's address in it.\n- **Flow data vs packet capture:** flows give **summaries** (who, how much) with little storage; captures give **full packets** (what exactly) with lots of storage.\n- **SPAN vs TAP:** SPAN is a **switch feature** that can drop frames under load; a TAP is **hardware** that copies everything, even errors.\n- **Bandwidth vs throughput:** **capacity** vs **actual achieved rate**.\n- **Latency vs jitter:** **delay** vs **variation in delay**.\n- **CRC errors vs discards:** CRC errors suggest **physical** problems; discards suggest **congestion**.\n- **Syslog vs SIEM:** syslog **collects** logs; a SIEM **correlates and analyzes** them for security.\n- **Syslog levels:** **0 is the most severe**, 7 the least. A higher number is not \"worse\".\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **NMS:** network management system; the central monitoring platform (SNMP manager).\n- **Agent:** SNMP software on a managed device.\n- **MIB / OID:** the SNMP object dictionary / one object's identifier.\n- **Community string:** SNMPv1/v2c shared password, sent in cleartext.\n- **authPriv:** SNMPv3 level with authentication and encryption.\n- **Flow record:** a summary of one conversation (IPs, ports, protocol, bytes, time).\n- **Top talkers:** the hosts or apps using the most bandwidth.\n- **SPAN:** switch port mirroring. **RSPAN/ERSPAN:** mirroring to another switch.\n- **TAP:** hardware that passively copies all traffic on a link.\n- **Baseline:** recorded normal performance used for comparison.\n- **Jitter:** variation in packet delay.\n- **Syslog:** standard for sending logs to a central collector (UDP 514).\n- **SIEM:** security information and event management; correlates logs to detect threats.\n- **Webhook:** an HTTP callback a tool sends when an event happens.\n- **Streaming telemetry:** devices pushing data continuously instead of being polled.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Who's using the bandwidth?\" → **NetFlow/flow data**. \"See the actual packets\" → **packet capture** via **SPAN or a TAP** (TAP if nothing can be missed). \"Secure SNMP\" → **SNMPv3 authPriv**. \"Alert immediately when a link goes down\" → **SNMP trap**. \"Correlate logs from many devices to find attacks\" → **SIEM**. Syslog level **0 = Emergency**, the most severe.","3|Disaster recovery":"# Disaster recovery\n\nDisaster recovery (DR) is how an organization **gets IT services back** after a major disruption such as a fire, flood, ransomware attack, or long power outage. Network+ tests the **metrics** that set recovery goals (RPO, RTO, MTTR, MTBF), the **recovery site** options, **high-availability** designs, and how plans are **tested**.\n\n## DR, business continuity and impact analysis\n\n- **Disaster recovery plan (DRP):** documents how **IT systems** and data are **restored** after a disruption: who does what, in what order, using which backups and sites.\n- **Business continuity plan (BCP):** broader. It covers keeping the **whole business** running during and after a disruption (people, facilities, manual workarounds, communications). The DRP is one part of business continuity.\n- **Business impact analysis (BIA):** the analysis done **first**. It identifies **critical business functions**, what losing each one costs over time, and therefore the **recovery priorities and targets** (RTO and RPO) for each system.\n\n**Why it matters:** not every system needs the same protection. Payroll might tolerate a day of downtime; online ordering might not tolerate ten minutes. The BIA puts numbers on that so money goes where it matters.\n\n**High availability vs disaster recovery:** **HA** keeps a service running through **small failures** (a failed power supply, switch, or server) with redundancy and automatic failover. **DR** handles **large events** that take out a whole site or system. You usually need both.\n\n## RPO and RTO\n\nThese two targets come out of the BIA and drive the whole design.\n\n| Metric | Question it answers | Measured | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **RPO** (recovery point objective) | How much **data** can we afford to **lose**? | Time **backward** from the disaster to the last good copy | **Backup or replication frequency** |\n| **RTO** (recovery time objective) | How long can we be **down**? | Time **forward** from the disaster until service is restored | **Recovery method and site type** |\n\n**Worked examples:**\n- Backups run every **6 hours**. If disaster strikes just before the next backup, up to **6 hours of data** is lost, so the achievable RPO is 6 hours.\n- \"We can lose **15 minutes** of data and must be back online within **1 hour**\" → **RPO = 15 minutes, RTO = 1 hour**.\n- A bank needs **near-zero data loss** for transactions → **synchronous (continuous) replication** to a second site. Nightly backups could never meet that RPO.\n- A company needs service back in **minutes** → a **hot site** or active-active design. A cold site can't meet that RTO.\n\nLower RPO and RTO cost more: more frequent replication, more standby hardware, faster links.\n\n## MTTR and MTBF\n\nThese measure **reliability and repair** of components and systems, rather than recovery targets.\n\n| Metric | Meaning | Better when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **MTBF** (mean time between failures) | **Average operating time between failures** of a repairable component | **Higher**: it fails less often |\n| **MTTR** (mean time to repair or recover) | **Average time to detect, diagnose, and fix** a failure and restore service | **Lower**: it's fixed faster |\n\n**Example:** a switch has an MTBF of 10,000 hours and an MTTR of 2 hours. You want MTBF to go **up** and MTTR to go **down**.\n\n**How to reduce MTTR:**\n- Keep **spare parts on site** (or a support contract with fast, e.g. 4-hour, replacement).\n- Have **up-to-date documentation** and **configuration backups** ready to load.\n- Good **monitoring** so failures are detected immediately.\n\n**How to raise MTBF:** buy quality hardware, keep it cool and on clean power (UPS), and replace components before end of life.\n\n## Recovery sites\n\nIf the main site is lost, where do you run?\n\n| Site | What's there | Recovery time | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Hot site** | Fully equipped, running systems with **current (replicated) data** | **Minutes** to hours | **Highest** |\n| **Warm site** | Hardware and connectivity in place, but **data must be restored** and systems configured | **Hours to days** | Medium |\n| **Cold site** | **Space, power, and cooling only**: no equipment, no data | **Days to weeks** | **Lowest** |\n\n- **Choosing:** match the site to the **RTO**. An RTO of 30 minutes needs a hot site; an RTO of two weeks can use a cold site.\n- **Cloud site / DRaaS (disaster recovery as a service):** a cloud provider hosts **replicated systems** that can be **started during a disaster**. This gives hot or warm capability without building a second data center, and you pay mostly for storage until you fail over.\n- **Geographic diversity:** put the recovery site **far enough away** that one regional disaster (hurricane, earthquake, regional power failure) can't hit both sites.\n- **ISP diversity:** use **different providers and physical paths** so one carrier outage or cable cut doesn't take the site offline.\n- **Restoring network configs at a DR site:** if the hardware differs, **interface names and hardware-specific settings** may need adjusting before a backup config will work.\n\n## High availability designs\n\n**High availability (HA)** minimizes downtime through **redundancy** and **automatic failover**. The two classic layouts:\n\n| | **Active-active** | **Active-passive** |\n|---|---|---|\n| How it works | **All** nodes (or sites) carry traffic **at the same time** and share the load | One node is **active**; a **standby** node waits and takes over if it fails |\n| Benefit | Uses all capacity; often better performance | **Simpler**; guaranteed capacity on failover because the standby was idle |\n| Drawback | More complex; capacity planning is critical | **Idle hardware** you paid for |\n\n**The active-active capacity trap:** two firewalls each running at **70%** load share traffic. If one fails, the survivor would need **140%** of its capacity and becomes overloaded. In a two-node active-active pair, keep each node **under about 50%** so either one can carry the whole load alone.\n\n**Failover** protocols you may see: **FHRP** (VRRP, HSRP) for gateways, firewall HA pairs, and server **clustering**.\n\n## Redundancy and single points of failure\n\nA **single point of failure (SPOF)** is any component whose failure **takes down the whole service**: one internet link, one core switch, one power feed, one DHCP server. HA design removes them.\n\n- **N+1 redundancy:** **one more component than needed**. If a room needs 3 cooling units, install 4 so one can fail without losing capacity. (**2N** means a full duplicate of everything.)\n- **Redundant power:** **dual, hot-swappable power supplies** that share the load, fed from **different circuits** and UPSs; generators for long outages.\n- **Redundant links and devices:** two uplinks, two core switches, link aggregation, and **diverse paths** so both cables don't run through the same trench.\n- **Clustering:** several servers **appear as one system** and **fail over automatically** if a member fails.\n- **Load balancing:** spreads traffic across several servers and stops sending to one that fails health checks.\n\nScenario: \"If the one router at the branch fails, the branch is offline\" → that router is a SPOF; add a second router with an FHRP, or a backup cellular link.\n\n## Backups and snapshots\n\n**Backup types:**\n\n| Type | What it copies | Backup speed | Restore needs | Archive bit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Full** | **Everything** | Slowest | Just the full | Cleared |\n| **Incremental** | Changes since the **last backup of any type** | **Fastest** | The full **plus every incremental** since | Cleared |\n| **Differential** | Changes since the **last full** | Grows each day | The full **plus only the latest differential** | **Not cleared** |\n\nExample: full on Sunday, failure on Thursday. With incrementals you restore Sunday + Mon + Tue + Wed. With differentials you restore Sunday + Wednesday's differential.\n\n**3-2-1 rule:** keep **3 copies** of data, on **2 different media** types, with **1 copy offsite**. Many add a copy that is **offline (air-gapped) or immutable**, because **ransomware** encrypts or deletes any backup it can reach.\n\n**Snapshots:** a **point-in-time copy of a VM or volume** used for **quick rollback** (e.g. before a risky upgrade). Snapshots usually live on the **same storage**, so if that storage fails they're gone too. They are **not a replacement for backups**.\n\n**Test restores:** a backup you have never restored is a hope, not a plan.\n\n## Testing the plan\n\nA DR plan that has never been tested will fail in surprising ways (missing passwords, wrong phone numbers, a backup nobody can restore). Network+ names **tabletop exercises** and **validation tests**.\n\n| Test | What happens | Risk to production |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tabletop exercise** | Key staff **talk through** a disaster scenario around a table, step by step. **No systems are touched** | None |\n| **Walkthrough / checklist review** | Teams review the documented steps, contact lists, and responsibilities | None |\n| **Validation tests** | **Actually perform recovery steps** to prove they work: restore a backup, bring up DR systems, check data | Low to medium |\n| **Parallel test** | DR systems are brought up and **run alongside production**; users stay on production | Low |\n| **Full interruption (failover) test** | Production is **actually shut down** and everything fails over to DR | **Highest**, but most realistic |\n\n- **Test regularly:** at least **annually** and **after major changes** (new systems, new site, staff turnover).\n- **After-action report:** after every test or real event, record **lessons learned** and **update the plan**.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **RPO vs RTO:** RP**O** = **data loss** (how far back you go); RT**O** = **downtime** (how long until you're back). Think \"**P**oint in the past\" vs \"**T**ime to recover\".\n- **MTBF vs MTTR:** MTBF is time **between** failures (want it **high**); MTTR is time **to repair** (want it **low**).\n- **RTO vs MTTR:** RTO is a **target** the business sets; MTTR is a **measured average** of actual repairs.\n- **Warm vs cold site:** warm has **hardware** but needs data restored; cold has **only space, power, and cooling**.\n- **Hot site vs active-active:** a hot site is a **standby location** ready to take over; active-active means both locations **carry traffic now**.\n- **Incremental vs differential:** incremental = since **last backup of any kind** (many pieces to restore); differential = since **last full** (two pieces to restore).\n- **Snapshot vs backup:** a snapshot is a quick rollback point, usually on the same storage; a backup is a separate copy.\n- **Tabletop vs validation test:** tabletop **talks**; validation **does**.\n- **BCP vs DRP:** BCP = keep the **business** running; DRP = restore **IT**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **BIA:** business impact analysis; identifies critical functions and sets RTO/RPO.\n- **RPO:** maximum acceptable data loss, measured in time.\n- **RTO:** maximum acceptable downtime.\n- **MTBF:** average operating time between failures.\n- **MTTR:** average time to repair or recover.\n- **Hot / warm / cold site:** recovery sites from fully ready to empty space.\n- **DRaaS:** cloud-hosted disaster recovery.\n- **Active-active / active-passive:** HA designs that share load or keep a standby.\n- **N+1:** one more component than required. **2N:** a full duplicate of every component.\n- **SPOF:** a single point of failure.\n- **3-2-1 rule:** 3 copies, 2 media, 1 offsite.\n- **Tabletop exercise:** a discussion-based walk through a disaster scenario.\n- **After-action report:** lessons learned recorded after a test or incident.\n\n> **Exam tip:** RP**O** = how much data you can lose; RT**O** = how long you can be down. Lower MTTR and higher MTBF are better. \"Fastest recovery, highest cost\" → **hot site**; \"just power and space\" → **cold site**. \"Talk through the plan, no systems touched\" → **tabletop**. Two active-active nodes at 70% each will overload when one fails.","3|Network services":"# Network services\n\nEvery network depends on a few background services: **DHCP** hands out addresses, **SLAAC** lets IPv6 hosts address themselves, **DNS** turns names into addresses, and **NTP/PTP** keep clocks in sync. When one of these breaks, users usually just say \"the internet is down\", so knowing how each works is the key to troubleshooting it.\n\n## DHCP and the DORA process\n\n**DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)** automatically gives clients an **IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers**, and other settings, so nobody has to type them in by hand. It uses **UDP 67** (server) and **UDP 68** (client).\n\n**DORA**, the four-step lease process:\n1. **Discover:** the client has no address, so it **broadcasts** from **0.0.0.0** to **255.255.255.255**: \"Is there a DHCP server?\"\n2. **Offer:** a server offers an address and settings.\n3. **Request:** the client requests the offered address (still broadcast, so other servers know their offers weren't chosen).\n4. **Acknowledge (ACK):** the server confirms, and the client starts using the address.\n\n**Lease and renewal:**\n- A **lease** is how long the client may use the address.\n- At **50% of the lease (T1)** the client **unicasts a DHCPREQUEST** to the server that issued it to **renew**.\n- If that fails, at **87.5% (T2)** it **broadcasts** to **rebind** with any server.\n- If the lease expires, the client must stop using the address and start DORA again.\n- A **DHCPNAK** is the server **refusing** a request, e.g. a laptop that moved to another subnet asks to keep its old address. The client then starts over with Discover.\n- **DHCPRELEASE:** the client gives the address back (e.g. `ipconfig /release`).\n\n## DHCP scopes, reservations and options\n\nA **scope** is the **pool of addresses and settings** a server hands out for **one subnet**, e.g. 192.168.10.100 to 192.168.10.200 /24.\n\n- **Exclusions:** addresses **inside** the scope range that the server **won't hand out**, usually because statically configured devices (servers, printers, the router) already use them.\n- **Reservations:** the server **always gives the same IP** to a specific client, identified by its **MAC address** (or client ID). The device still uses DHCP, so you manage it centrally, but its address never changes. Good for printers and cameras.\n- **Lease time:** short leases (hours) for busy **guest Wi-Fi** free addresses quickly; long leases (days) for stable office desktops reduce DHCP traffic.\n\n**Common DHCP options:**\n\n| Option | Provides |\n|---|---|\n| **3** | **Default gateway** (router) |\n| **6** | **DNS servers** |\n| **15** | **Domain name** (DNS suffix) |\n| **51** | Lease time |\n| **66 / 150** | **TFTP server** address, used by **VoIP phones** to download their configuration |\n\nOptions can be set **server-wide** (apply to every scope) or **per scope** (e.g. each subnet's own gateway).\n\nScenario: clients get an IP address and can reach local hosts by IP but nothing off the subnet → check **option 3** (gateway). Clients reach sites by IP but not by name → check **option 6** (DNS).\n\n## DHCP relay, redundancy and failures\n\n**DHCP relay (IP helper):** Discover is a **broadcast**, and **routers don't forward broadcasts**. If the DHCP server sits on a different subnet, a **relay agent**, usually configured on the router interface that is the clients' **default gateway** (`ip helper-address` on Cisco), receives the broadcast and forwards it as **unicast** to the server. The server uses the relay's interface address to pick the right scope.\n\nScenario: a new VLAN is created and its clients get no address, while other VLANs work → the new VLAN's gateway interface is missing its **IP helper**.\n\n**Redundancy:**\n- **DHCP failover:** two servers share the lease database and can each serve the full scope.\n- **Split scope:** each server holds part of the range, often **80/20**.\n\n**When things go wrong:**\n- **No server reachable:** Windows clients give themselves an **APIPA** address in **169.254.0.0/16**. They can talk only to other APIPA hosts on the local link. A 169.254.x.x address means **\"DHCP failed\"**.\n- **Scope (pool) exhaustion:** every address is leased, so new clients get nothing. Fix it by **shortening the lease**, **enlarging the scope** (or subnet), or removing stale leases.\n- **Rogue DHCP server:** an unauthorized server (a home router plugged in, or an attacker) hands out wrong gateways or DNS. **DHCP snooping** on switches only allows server replies from **trusted ports**.\n\n## SLAAC and DHCPv6\n\nIPv6 hosts have more ways to get an address.\n\n**SLAAC (stateless address autoconfiguration):**\n1. The host creates a **link-local** address (**fe80::/10**).\n2. It receives a **Router Advertisement (RA)** from the local router, containing the network **prefix** (usually a /64).\n3. It builds its own address: **prefix + interface ID**. The interface ID comes from **EUI-64** (the MAC split in half with **FFFE** inserted and the 7th bit flipped) or is **randomly generated** for privacy.\n4. It checks for duplicates with **DAD** (duplicate address detection).\n\nNo server tracks who has which address, which is why it's \"stateless\". The router's link-local address becomes the default gateway.\n\n**DHCPv6** uses **UDP 546** (client) and **547** (server). Clients send a **Solicit** to the multicast address **FF02::1:2** (all DHCP relay agents and servers); the exchange is **Solicit, Advertise, Request, Reply**.\n- **Stateless DHCPv6:** SLAAC provides the address; DHCPv6 provides **extra settings** such as DNS servers.\n- **Stateful DHCPv6:** the DHCPv6 server **assigns the addresses** and tracks them, like DHCP for IPv4.\n\n**Router Advertisement flags tell hosts what to do:**\n\n| Flag | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **M (managed)** = 1 | Get your **address** from **stateful DHCPv6** |\n| **O (other)** = 1 | Get **other settings** (like DNS) from DHCPv6; use SLAAC for the address |\n| Both 0 | SLAAC only (DNS can come from the RA itself via RDNSS) |\n\n## How DNS resolution works\n\n**DNS (Domain Name System)** resolves names to IP addresses on **port 53**: **UDP** for normal queries, **TCP** for zone transfers and large responses.\n\n**What a client does when you type www.example.com:**\n1. Checks its own **DNS cache** and the **hosts file** (a local text file of name-to-IP mappings that is checked before DNS; malware sometimes edits it to redirect sites).\n2. Sends a **recursive query** to its configured **DNS resolver** (from DHCP option 6), meaning \"give me the final answer\".\n3. If the resolver doesn't have it cached, it performs **iterative queries**: it asks a **root server** (which refers it to the **.com TLD servers**), then the **TLD server** (which refers it to example.com's **authoritative servers**), then the **authoritative server**, which returns the record.\n4. The resolver **caches** the answer for the record's **TTL** and returns it to the client.\n\n**Server roles:**\n- **Authoritative server:** holds the **actual records** for a zone and gives the official answer.\n- **Recursive resolver (caching server):** finds answers **for clients** by querying other servers, and caches them.\n- **Non-authoritative answer:** an answer that came from a resolver's **cache**, not straight from the zone's authoritative server. `nslookup` labels these.\n- **Forwarder:** a DNS server configured to send queries it can't answer to a **specific upstream resolver** (e.g. an ISP or public resolver) instead of walking the tree itself.\n\n**TTL (time to live):** how long resolvers may **cache** a record. Before migrating a server to a new IP, **lower the TTL** in advance so clients pick up the change quickly.\n\n**Clearing caches:** `ipconfig /flushdns` on Windows clears the local cache when a stale record causes trouble.\n\nScenario: a user reaches a website **by IP but not by name** → **DNS** is the problem.\n\n## DNS record types\n\n| Record | Purpose | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | Name → **IPv4** address | www → 203.0.113.10 |\n| **AAAA** | Name → **IPv6** address | www → 2001:db8::10 |\n| **CNAME** | **Alias**: points one name to **another name** | shop → www.example.com |\n| **MX** | **Mail server** for the domain, with a **preference**; **lower number = tried first** | 10 mail1, 20 mail2 |\n| **PTR** | **IP → name** (reverse lookup) | 10.113.0.203.in-addr.arpa → www |\n| **NS** | The **authoritative name servers** for a zone | example.com → ns1, ns2 |\n| **SOA** | **Start of authority**: primary server, admin email, **serial number**, refresh and expiry timers | One per zone |\n| **SRV** | **Host and port** for a service (e.g. **LDAP, Kerberos, SIP**); Active Directory clients use them to find domain controllers | _ldap._tcp |\n| **TXT** | Free text: **SPF, DKIM, DMARC** email records and **domain verification** codes for SaaS providers | v=spf1 ... |\n\n- A **CNAME** must point to a **name**, never an IP address.\n- **PTR** records also help **mail servers** check that a sending server's IP resolves back to a sensible name (anti-spam).\n- The **SOA serial number** must increase when the zone changes so secondary servers know to update.\n\n## DNS zones, servers and security\n\n**Zone types:**\n- **Forward lookup zone:** **names → IPs** (A, AAAA, CNAME, MX...).\n- **Reverse lookup zone:** **IPs → names** (PTR records), under **in-addr.arpa** (IPv4) or **ip6.arpa** (IPv6).\n\n**Primary vs secondary servers:**\n- The **primary** server holds the **editable master copy** of the zone.\n- **Secondary** servers hold **read-only copies** received by **zone transfer** (AXFR full, IXFR incremental, over **TCP 53**). They add redundancy and spread the load.\n- **Restrict zone transfers** to known secondary servers. An open transfer hands an attacker a **complete list of hostnames and IPs**.\n\n**Other features:**\n- **Split-horizon (split-brain) DNS:** the same name gets **different answers** for **internal** and **external** clients, e.g. an internal IP for staff and a public IP for everyone else.\n- **Dynamic DNS (DDNS):** DHCP clients or the DHCP server **automatically update** DNS records when addresses change.\n\n**DNS security:**\n\n| Feature | What it protects | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| **DNSSEC** | **Integrity and authenticity**: prevents spoofed answers and **cache poisoning** | **Digitally signs** records; a **chain of trust** runs up to the signed **root zone**. It does **not encrypt** |\n| **DoT** (DNS over TLS) | **Privacy**: hides queries from eavesdroppers | Encrypts DNS in TLS on **TCP 853** (easy to identify and block) |\n| **DoH** (DNS over HTTPS) | **Privacy** | Encrypts DNS inside HTTPS on **TCP 443**, blending with web traffic |\n\n## Time synchronization with NTP, NTS and PTP\n\n**Why time matters:**\n- **Log correlation:** you can't reconstruct an incident across devices if their clocks disagree.\n- **Kerberos authentication** fails if client and server clocks differ by more than about **5 minutes** (the default).\n- **Certificates** have validity dates; a wrong clock makes valid certificates look expired or not yet valid.\n- Scheduled jobs, backups, and change records all rely on accurate timestamps.\n\n**NTP (Network Time Protocol)** uses **UDP 123** and a hierarchy of **strata**:\n- **Stratum 0:** the reference clock itself (**GPS**, atomic clock). Not on the network.\n- **Stratum 1:** a server **directly connected** to a stratum 0 clock.\n- **Stratum 2, 3...:** each level syncs from the one above. **Stratum 16** means **unsynchronized**.\n- Configure **several NTP servers** so the client can detect and ignore one that gives **false time**.\n- NTP typically achieves **millisecond** accuracy.\n\n**NTS (Network Time Security)** adds **cryptographic authentication** to NTP so clients know the time really came from the trusted server. This prevents attackers from **spoofing time**.\n\n**PTP (Precision Time Protocol, IEEE 1588)** uses **hardware timestamping** in network interfaces and switches to reach **sub-microsecond (nanosecond-level)** accuracy. The most accurate clock in the domain is the **grandmaster clock**. Used in **financial trading**, **telecom/5G**, power grids, and industrial control.\n\nScenario: users suddenly fail Kerberos logins and certificate warnings appear → check **time synchronization**.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Exclusion vs reservation:** an exclusion is **never handed out**; a reservation is **always handed to the same MAC**.\n- **Relay vs failover:** a relay **forwards DHCP across subnets**; failover provides a **second server**.\n- **APIPA vs link-local IPv6:** both are self-assigned link-only addresses; **APIPA 169.254.x.x** signals **DHCP failure** on IPv4, while an IPv6 **fe80::** address is **always present** and normal.\n- **SLAAC vs stateless DHCPv6 vs stateful DHCPv6:** SLAAC = host builds its own address; stateless DHCPv6 = SLAAC address **plus** DNS from a server; stateful = server **assigns the address**.\n- **M vs O flag:** **M**anaged = address from DHCPv6; **O**ther = only other settings from DHCPv6.\n- **A vs PTR:** A is **forward** (name → IP); PTR is **reverse** (IP → name).\n- **CNAME vs A:** CNAME points to **another name**; A points to an **IP**.\n- **Authoritative vs recursive:** authoritative **owns the records**; recursive **looks them up for clients**.\n- **DNSSEC vs DoH/DoT:** DNSSEC **signs** (authenticity, no encryption); DoH/DoT **encrypt** (privacy).\n- **NTP vs NTS vs PTP:** NTP syncs time; NTS **secures** NTP; PTP is **far more precise**.\n- **Stratum numbers:** **lower is closer to the reference**, not \"worse\".\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **DORA:** Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge.\n- **Scope:** a DHCP address pool and options for one subnet.\n- **Reservation:** a fixed DHCP address tied to a MAC address.\n- **IP helper / relay agent:** forwards DHCP broadcasts to a server on another subnet.\n- **APIPA:** 169.254.0.0/16 self-assigned address after DHCP fails.\n- **SLAAC:** IPv6 hosts building their own address from the RA prefix.\n- **Router Advertisement (RA):** IPv6 router message carrying the prefix and M/O flags.\n- **EUI-64:** method of building an IPv6 interface ID from the MAC address.\n- **Hosts file:** local name-to-IP file checked before DNS.\n- **TTL:** how long a DNS answer may be cached.\n- **Forwarder:** a DNS server that sends unresolved queries to a chosen upstream resolver.\n- **Zone transfer:** copying a zone from primary to secondary servers.\n- **Split-horizon DNS:** different answers for internal and external clients.\n- **DDNS:** automatic DNS updates when addresses change.\n- **Stratum:** an NTP server's distance from the reference clock.\n- **Grandmaster clock:** the most accurate clock in a PTP domain.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Clients on another subnet get no DHCP address\" → missing **relay/IP helper**. \"169.254.x.x\" → **DHCP unreachable**. \"Mail server record\" → **MX**; \"reverse lookup\" → **PTR**; \"service location for AD/SIP\" → **SRV**; \"domain verification/SPF\" → **TXT**. \"Signed DNS\" → **DNSSEC**; \"encrypted DNS\" → **DoH (443) / DoT (853)**. \"Secure, authenticated time\" → **NTS**; \"nanosecond accuracy\" → **PTP**.","3|Access & management":"# Access & management\n\nNetwork staff and remote users need secure ways **in**: users need **VPNs** to reach corporate resources, and administrators need **SSH, GUIs, APIs, and console access** to manage devices, ideally through **jump boxes** and **out-of-band** paths that still work when the network doesn't. This lesson covers each method, when to use it, and how to secure it.\n\n## VPN types\n\nA **VPN (virtual private network)** builds an **encrypted tunnel** across an untrusted network (usually the internet) so traffic is private and authenticated.\n\n| Type | Who connects | How | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Site-to-site** | **Two networks**, via their **gateways** (routers, firewalls, VPN concentrators) | A **permanent, always-on** tunnel; users need **no client** and don't know it's there | Branch office ↔ headquarters, office ↔ cloud VPC |\n| **Client-to-site (remote access)** | **One device** to the corporate network | **VPN client software** on the laptop or phone connects on demand | Home workers and travelers |\n| **Clientless** | One user, via a **web browser** | A **TLS (HTTPS) portal** that publishes **specific internal web apps**; nothing to install | Contractors or personal devices needing one or two apps |\n\n- A **VPN concentrator** (or firewall feature) terminates many remote-access tunnels and usually authenticates users against a central directory with **RADIUS or LDAP**, plus **MFA**.\n- Scenario: \"A new branch must reach HQ servers at all times, and users shouldn't run any software\" → **site-to-site**. \"A contractor on a personal laptop needs only the intranet web app\" → **clientless**.\n\n## Full tunnel vs split tunnel\n\nOnce a remote-access VPN is up, which traffic goes through it?\n\n| | **Full tunnel** | **Split tunnel** |\n|---|---|---|\n| What uses the VPN | **All** traffic, including internet browsing | **Only corporate-bound** traffic; internet traffic goes **directly** out the user's local connection |\n| Security | Corporate firewalls, proxies, and DLP **inspect everything** | Internet traffic **bypasses corporate security controls** |\n| Performance | Uses more corporate bandwidth; can add latency to internet traffic | **Saves** VPN and HQ bandwidth; faster internet for the user |\n\n- **Choose full tunnel** when security and visibility matter most (e.g. regulated industries, or users must be protected by the corporate web filter).\n- **Choose split tunnel** when bandwidth is limited and cloud apps (video calls, SaaS) should go straight out.\n- Scenario: \"Remote users' video calls saturate the HQ internet link\" → split tunnel would relieve it. \"Security wants to filter all remote users' web traffic\" → full tunnel.\n\n## VPN protocols\n\n**IPsec** is the standard suite for site-to-site VPNs (and many remote-access VPNs).\n- **IKE (Internet Key Exchange)** negotiates keys and settings on **UDP 500**, or **UDP 4500** with **NAT traversal** when a device sits behind NAT.\n  - **IKE Phase 1** authenticates the peers and builds a secure **management channel** (the IKE SA).\n  - **IKE Phase 2** uses that channel to negotiate the **IPsec SAs** that protect actual data.\n- **ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload, IP protocol 50)** provides **encryption**, integrity, and authentication. **AH (Authentication Header, protocol 51)** provides integrity and authentication **but no encryption**, and breaks with NAT.\n- **Tunnel mode** encrypts the **entire original packet** and adds a new IP header: used **site-to-site** between gateways.\n- **Transport mode** protects **only the payload**, keeping the original IP header: used **host-to-host**.\n- **Must match on both ends** of a site-to-site tunnel: **encryption and hash algorithms**, the **authentication method** (pre-shared key or certificates), the **Diffie-Hellman (DH) group**, and lifetimes. A mismatch means the tunnel **won't come up**.\n\n**Other VPN protocols:**\n\n| Protocol | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **SSL/TLS VPN** | Runs over **TCP 443**, so it **passes through almost any firewall** or hotel network; used for remote access and clientless portals |\n| **WireGuard** | Modern, lightweight, fast, **UDP-based**, with state-of-the-art cryptography |\n| **L2TP** | A tunneling protocol with **no encryption of its own**; paired with **IPsec** (L2TP/IPsec) |\n| **PPTP** | Old and **insecure** (MS-CHAPv2/MPPE weaknesses); don't use it |\n| **GRE** | Tunnels any protocol (including multicast and routing updates) but **doesn't encrypt**; often run inside IPsec |\n\n## SSH and console connections\n\n**SSH (Secure Shell, TCP 22)** is the standard way to reach a device's **command line (CLI)** remotely. It **encrypts the whole session**, including passwords.\n- **Never use Telnet (TCP 23)**: it sends everything, including passwords, in **cleartext**.\n- Use **SSH version 2 only**; SSHv1 has known cryptographic weaknesses.\n- **Public key authentication** is stronger than passwords: the admin holds a private key and the device stores the public key.\n- Restrict who can connect by applying an **ACL to the VTY lines** (the virtual terminal lines that accept remote sessions), allowing only management subnets.\n- Set idle timeouts and log sessions.\n\n**Console connection:** a **direct, physical serial connection** to a device's **console port**.\n- Needs a **rollover (console) cable**, usually with a **USB-to-serial adapter**, or a USB console cable; then a terminal program (PuTTY, screen).\n- Classic Cisco settings: **9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit (8-N-1)**, no flow control.\n- **Works with no IP configuration at all**, so it's used for **initial setup**, **password recovery**, and recovering a device whose network config is broken.\n- Because anyone at the console can take control, **physical security** of devices and console servers matters.\n\n## GUI and API management\n\n**GUI (graphical user interface):** a **web interface** or management application for configuring and monitoring devices visually. Easier for occasional tasks and dashboards.\n- Use **HTTPS only**, never plain HTTP.\n- Make it reachable **only from management networks**, never the internet.\n- Change default credentials and use strong authentication or MFA.\n\n**API (application programming interface):** lets **software and automation tools** read and change device configuration and state **programmatically**. This is what makes it practical to change **200 switches consistently** instead of typing into each one.\n- **REST APIs** run over **HTTPS** and exchange data as **JSON** (or XML). HTTP methods map to actions:\n\n| Method | Action |\n|---|---|\n| **GET** | **Read** data without changing anything |\n| **POST** | **Create** something new |\n| **PUT / PATCH** | **Replace / update** existing data |\n| **DELETE** | Remove something |\n\n- **NETCONF** configures devices over **SSH (TCP 830)** using structured **YANG data models**, with transaction-style changes. **RESTCONF** offers the same YANG models over HTTPS.\n- **Securing APIs:** authenticate with **tokens or API keys** over **HTTPS**, give each integration **least privilege**, rotate keys, and log API calls.\n- **Automation tools** (Ansible, Python scripts) use APIs or SSH to push the same change to many devices quickly and consistently.\n\n**Which method?** One quick change on one device → SSH. Visual monitoring or occasional configuration → GUI. Repeatable changes across many devices → API and automation. Device unreachable over the network → console.\n\n## In-band vs out-of-band management\n\n| | **In-band** | **Out-of-band (OOB)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Path | The **same production network** that carries user traffic | A **separate, dedicated path** |\n| Examples | SSH or HTTPS to a switch's IP over the LAN or WAN | **Console server** (terminal server), dedicated management network, **cellular (LTE/5G) modem**, dial-up modem |\n| Pro | Convenient, no extra hardware | **Works when the production network is down** |\n| Con | **Unreachable during the outages** when you need it most; management traffic shares the network with users | Extra cost and equipment to maintain |\n\n- A **console server** connects to the **console ports of many devices** in a rack and gives admins remote access to them over its own separate link.\n- Scenario: \"A remote router's WAN link failed after a bad config change; how do you fix it without driving there?\" → **out-of-band** through a console server with a **cellular** connection.\n- Many devices have a dedicated **management port** that can attach to an OOB management network.\n\n## Jump boxes and securing administrative access\n\n**Jump box (jump host / bastion host):** a **hardened, closely monitored** server that admins must log into **first**, and from which they reach devices in secure zones. Benefits:\n- **One controlled entry point** instead of management access from every desktop.\n- Strong authentication (**MFA**) and **session logging/recording** in one place.\n- Device ACLs only need to allow the jump box's IP.\n\n**Other key practices:**\n- **Dedicated management VLAN or network:** separates management interfaces from user traffic and limits who can even reach them.\n- **Central AAA:** use **TACACS+** (separates authentication, **per-command authorization**, and **accounting** of every command) or RADIUS so admin accounts are managed centrally and every action is logged.\n- **Local break-glass account:** a vaulted local account for **emergency use only, when AAA servers are unreachable**.\n- **MFA** on VPN, jump box, and admin logins.\n- **Least privilege:** give each admin only the access their job requires (e.g. read-only for help desk).\n- **Never expose RDP (TCP 3389)** directly to the internet. Use a VPN or an **RD gateway**, which carries RDP inside **HTTPS** with authentication.\n- **Disable insecure protocols:** Telnet, HTTP, SNMPv1/v2c, and SSHv1.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Site-to-site vs client-to-site:** site-to-site joins **networks** through gateways with no user client; client-to-site connects **one device** using client software.\n- **Client-to-site vs clientless:** clientless needs **only a browser** and exposes **specific web apps**; client-based gives full network access.\n- **Full vs split tunnel:** full = **all traffic** through the VPN (more security); split = **only corporate** traffic (saves bandwidth, loses visibility).\n- **Tunnel vs transport mode:** tunnel encrypts the **whole packet** (site-to-site); transport encrypts **only the payload** (host-to-host).\n- **ESP vs AH:** ESP **encrypts**; AH only authenticates.\n- **IKE Phase 1 vs Phase 2:** Phase 1 builds the **secure management channel**; Phase 2 builds the **data tunnels**.\n- **SSH vs Telnet:** both give a CLI; only **SSH encrypts**.\n- **Console vs in-band SSH:** console is a **direct physical** connection that needs no IP; SSH needs a working network.\n- **In-band vs out-of-band:** in-band uses the **production network**; OOB uses a **separate path** that survives outages.\n- **Jump box vs VPN:** a VPN gets you **onto the network**; a jump box is the **controlled hop** into secure management zones.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **VPN concentrator:** device that terminates many VPN tunnels.\n- **Split tunnel / full tunnel:** only corporate traffic vs all traffic through the VPN.\n- **IKE:** IPsec key exchange (UDP 500, or 4500 with NAT traversal).\n- **ESP:** IPsec protocol that encrypts data.\n- **NAT traversal (NAT-T):** wrapping IPsec in UDP 4500 so it passes through NAT.\n- **VTY lines:** virtual terminal lines that accept remote SSH sessions.\n- **Rollover cable:** console cable used to reach a device's serial console port.\n- **REST API:** HTTPS-based interface using methods like GET and POST with JSON data.\n- **NETCONF / YANG:** device configuration protocol over SSH (830) / the data models it uses.\n- **Console server:** gives remote access to many devices' console ports.\n- **Out-of-band management:** device access over a path separate from production.\n- **Jump box (bastion host):** hardened, monitored entry point into secure zones.\n- **TACACS+:** AAA protocol with per-command authorization and accounting.\n- **Break-glass account:** emergency local account for when AAA is down.\n- **RD gateway:** carries RDP inside HTTPS so RDP isn't exposed directly.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Reach the router when the WAN is down\" → **out-of-band** (console server or cellular modem). \"Only corporate traffic in the tunnel\" → **split tunnel**. \"Browser only, no client\" → **clientless**. \"Replace Telnet\" → **SSH**. \"Tunnel won't form\" → check that **IKE/IPsec settings match** on both ends. \"Single monitored entry point for admins\" → **jump box**.","4|Logical security":"# Logical security\n\nLogical security is everything that protects a network with software, cryptography, and identity rather than locks and walls. For N10-009 it covers four ideas: **encryption** (keeping data secret and unaltered), **certificates and PKI** (proving who a key belongs to), **identity and access management** (who you are and what you may do), and **geofencing** (where you are allowed to be when you connect).\n\n## Data in transit and data at rest\n\nData is exposed in two very different situations, and each needs its own protection.\n\n| State | What it means | Threat it stops | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Data in transit** (in motion) | Data **moving across a network** | Eavesdropping, sniffing, on-path (man-in-the-middle) tampering | **TLS/HTTPS**, **IPsec** VPNs, **SSH**, **SFTP/SCP**, WPA3 on Wi-Fi |\n| **Data at rest** | Data **stored** on a disk, database, backup, or USB drive | A stolen laptop, drive, or backup tape being read | **Full-disk encryption** (BitLocker, FileVault), encrypted databases, encrypted backups, self-encrypting drives |\n\n- The two are independent. A website using HTTPS protects the data on the wire, but if the database behind it is unencrypted, a stolen backup still exposes everything.\n- **Data in use** (in memory while being processed) is a third state, but Network+ focuses on transit and rest.\n- Scenario: \"A laptop with customer data was stolen from a car\" → the missing control is **encryption at rest**. \"An attacker captured passwords with a packet sniffer\" → the missing control is **encryption in transit** (e.g. Telnet or HTTP used instead of SSH or HTTPS).\n\n## Symmetric, asymmetric, and hashing\n\nThere are three building blocks. Knowing which one does what explains almost every crypto question.\n\n| Building block | Keys | Strengths | Used for | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Symmetric encryption** | **One shared secret key** encrypts and decrypts | **Fast**, efficient for large amounts of data | Bulk data: the actual session, disk encryption | **AES** (current standard), 3DES (legacy) |\n| **Asymmetric encryption** | A **key pair**: public key (shared with anyone) and private key (never shared) | Solves the **key-exchange problem**; enables signatures | Key exchange, digital signatures, certificates | **RSA**, **ECC**, Diffie-Hellman (key agreement) |\n| **Hashing** | **No key**; one-way function | Fixed-length digest; any change to the input changes the hash | **Integrity** checks, password storage | **SHA-256**; **MD5** and **SHA-1** are broken (collisions) |\n\n**Why TLS uses both:** asymmetric crypto is slow, but it lets two strangers agree on a secret safely. Symmetric crypto is fast, but both sides need the same key. So TLS uses asymmetric crypto (or Diffie-Hellman) to **agree on a session key**, then **symmetric** encryption (AES) protects the bulk data.\n\n**Hashing is not encryption.** You can't \"decrypt\" a hash. You recompute the hash and compare: if the values match, the data wasn't changed. A **collision** is two different inputs producing the same hash, which is why MD5 and SHA-1 are no longer trusted.\n\n**Digital signatures** combine hashing and asymmetric keys:\n1. The sender hashes the message and encrypts the hash with **their private key**. That's the signature.\n2. The receiver decrypts it with the **sender's public key** and compares it to their own hash of the message.\n3. A match proves **integrity** (not altered), **authenticity** (only the private-key holder could sign it), and **non-repudiation** (the sender can't deny signing).\n\n**Perfect forward secrecy (PFS):** each session uses fresh **ephemeral** keys (e.g. ECDHE). If a server's long-term private key is stolen later, previously recorded sessions still can't be decrypted. **TLS 1.3** requires PFS, removes outdated ciphers, and has a faster handshake.\n\n## Certificates, PKI, and key management\n\nA public key is just a number. A **certificate** answers the question \"whose public key is this?\"\n\n- A **digital certificate (X.509)** binds a **public key to an identity** (e.g. `www.example.com`). It contains the **subject**, **subject alternative names (SAN)**, the **public key**, the **issuer**, **validity dates**, a serial number, and the **CA's signature**.\n- A **certificate authority (CA)** verifies the requester and signs the certificate. Clients trust it because the CA's root certificate is in their **trust store**.\n- **Chain of trust:** **root CA** → **intermediate CA** → **server (leaf) certificate**. The **root CA** is the top **trust anchor** and is kept **offline**; intermediates do the day-to-day issuing, so a compromised intermediate can be revoked without replacing the root.\n- **PKI (public key infrastructure)** is the whole system: CAs, certificates, policies, registration, and revocation.\n\n**Revocation:** a certificate can be cancelled before it expires, for example if its private key leaked.\n- **CRL (certificate revocation list):** a published list of revoked serial numbers that clients download.\n- **OCSP (Online Certificate Status Protocol):** the client asks a responder about **one certificate** in real time. **OCSP stapling** lets the server attach a fresh OCSP response itself.\n\n**Self-signed certificates** are signed by their own key instead of a trusted CA. They encrypt traffic just as well, but **clients don't trust them by default** (browser warnings), because nobody vouches for the identity. They're fine for labs and internal device management if you add them to the trust store; public services should use CA-issued certificates.\n\n**Certificate pinning:** an application trusts only a **specific certificate or public key** for a service, so even a certificate from a compromised or rogue CA is rejected.\n\n**Expired certificates** are a common outage cause: \"Everyone suddenly gets certificate warnings on the VPN portal\" → check the expiration date.\n\n### Key management\n\nEncryption is only as strong as the protection of its keys. **Key management** is the life cycle of cryptographic keys:\n1. **Generate** keys with strong randomness and adequate length.\n2. **Store** them securely: a **hardware security module (HSM)**, a tamper-resistant device that generates and stores keys so they never leave it in plain form, or a software key vault.\n3. **Distribute** them safely (never by email in plaintext).\n4. **Rotate** them on a schedule so a leaked key has limited value.\n5. **Revoke and destroy** them when compromised or retired.\n\nScenario: \"A private key for the web server was found on a public code repository\" → **revoke** the certificate, **generate a new key pair**, and get a new certificate issued.\n\n## Authentication and MFA\n\n**IAM (identity and access management)** manages identities, how they **authenticate**, what they're **authorized** to do, and the account **life cycle**: provisioning when someone joins, changing access when they move roles, and prompt **deprovisioning** (disabling) when they leave. Forgotten accounts of former employees are a classic risk.\n\n**AAA** frames the whole process:\n- **Authentication:** proving **who you are** (password, certificate, token).\n- **Authorization:** deciding **what you may do** once identified.\n- **Accounting:** recording **what you did**, when, and for how long (logs for auditing and billing).\n\n**Multifactor authentication (MFA)** requires factors from **different categories**:\n\n| Factor | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Something you know** | Password, PIN, security question |\n| **Something you have** | Smart card, hardware token, phone authenticator app, badge |\n| **Something you are** | Fingerprint, face, iris, voice (biometrics) |\n| **Somewhere you are** | GPS or network location |\n| **Something you do** | Typing rhythm, signature gesture |\n\n- **Smart card + PIN** = MFA (have + know). **Password + security question** = still single-factor (two things you know).\n- **Time-based authentication:**\n   - **TOTP (time-based one-time password):** a code generated from a **shared secret plus the current time**, changing about **every 30 seconds**. The token and server clocks must be roughly in sync.\n   - **HOTP (HMAC-based one-time password):** the code changes with a **counter** each time one is used, not with time.\n   - **Time-of-day restrictions:** an account can log in only during set hours, e.g. contractors 8:00–18:00 on weekdays.\n- **Strengthening device logins:** long passphrases, **account lockout** after failed attempts, MFA, and **centralized AAA** (RADIUS/TACACS+) instead of shared local accounts.\n\n## SSO, SAML, and federation\n\n**Single sign-on (SSO)** lets a user **authenticate once** and reach many applications without logging in again. Benefits: fewer passwords to reuse or write down, one place to enforce MFA, and one place to disable an account. Risk: the SSO account becomes a high-value target, so protect it with MFA.\n\n| Technology | What it does | Key words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SAML** | Web SSO using **XML assertions**. The **identity provider (IdP)** authenticates the user and sends a signed assertion to the **service provider (SP)**, the application | XML, IdP, SP, assertion |\n| **OAuth 2.0** | **Delegated authorization**: lets an app access resources **on the user's behalf** without getting their password (\"Allow this app to read your calendar\") | Access tokens, authorization (not authentication) |\n| **OpenID Connect (OIDC)** | Adds **authentication** (identity) on top of OAuth 2.0 | \"Sign in with...\" buttons |\n| **Kerberos** | **Ticket-based** authentication in **Active Directory**. A **key distribution center (KDC)** issues tickets so passwords aren't sent across the network | TGT, tickets, KDC, time-sensitive |\n\n- **Federation** is a trust relationship between organizations: users from one organization access another's resources using their **home credentials** (e.g. a partner logs in to your portal with their own company account, via SAML).\n- Kerberos depends on synchronized clocks. If a client's clock is several minutes off, ticket authentication fails.\n\n## RADIUS, TACACS+, LDAP, and 802.1X\n\nThese protocols carry authentication between network devices and a central server, so you manage accounts in one place instead of on every switch and AP.\n\n| Protocol | Transport and port | Encryption | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **RADIUS** | **UDP 1812** (authentication), **UDP 1813** (accounting) | Encrypts **only the password** | **Network access** for users: Wi-Fi (WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise), VPN, 802.1X. Open standard |\n| **TACACS+** | **TCP 49** | Encrypts the **entire payload** | **Device administration**: admins logging in to routers and switches. **Separates** authentication, authorization, and accounting, so it can authorize **each command** |\n| **LDAP** | **TCP 389** | None by default (simple bind sends cleartext) | **Querying a directory** such as Active Directory: verify users, look up group memberships |\n| **LDAPS** | **TCP 636** | LDAP over **TLS** | Secure directory queries |\n\n- RADIUS **combines** authentication and authorization in one step. TACACS+ keeps the three A's separate.\n- RADIUS and TACACS+ often **use LDAP/Active Directory behind them** as the actual user database.\n- Scenario: \"Admins must be limited to show commands, and every command they type must be logged\" → **TACACS+**. \"Employees authenticate to corporate Wi-Fi with their domain credentials\" → **RADIUS** with 802.1X.\n\n**802.1X (port-based network access control)** authenticates a device **before** a switch port or Wi-Fi network grants access:\n- **Supplicant:** the client device requesting access.\n- **Authenticator:** the **switch or access point** that relays the request and opens or blocks the port.\n- **Authentication server:** usually a **RADIUS** server that makes the decision.\n- Until authentication succeeds, the port passes only authentication traffic (EAP). The authenticator never decides on its own; it asks the server.\n\n## Authorization and least privilege\n\nAuthentication proves identity; **authorization** decides what that identity may access.\n\n- **Least privilege:** give each user, admin, or service **only the access its job requires**, and no more. A help-desk tech can reset passwords but can't change firewall rules. This limits the damage from a mistake or a compromised account.\n\n| Model | Who decides | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RBAC (role-based)** | Permissions go to **roles**; users are placed in roles | \"Network Operators\" role can view configs; move a user to a new role when they change jobs |\n| **MAC (mandatory)** | The **system**, based on **classification labels** | Secret, Top Secret clearances in government/military |\n| **DAC (discretionary)** | The **owner** of the resource | A file owner shares a folder with a colleague |\n| **ABAC (attribute-based)** | Rules using **attributes** | Allow if department = Finance AND device = managed AND time = business hours |\n| **Rule-based** | Global rules set by an admin | Firewall ACLs |\n\n- **PAM (privileged access management)** protects high-privilege accounts: credentials are **vaulted**, sessions are **monitored and recorded**, and access is granted **just-in-time** for a limited period.\n- **Separation of duties** splits sensitive tasks so no single person can complete them alone.\n- Scenario: \"A new hire needs the same access as everyone else on the help desk\" → add them to the help-desk **role** (RBAC), rather than granting permissions one by one.\n\n## Geofencing\n\n**Geofencing** uses a device's **location** to allow or deny access. Location comes from **GPS** on mobile devices or **IP geolocation** for network logins.\n\n- Block logins from countries where the company doesn't operate.\n- Let a mobile app or company device work only inside the office or campus boundary.\n- Trigger extra checks (MFA) when a login comes from an unusual region.\n- **Impossible travel** detection flags two logins from distant places **too close together in time** to be physically possible, e.g. London at 09:00 and Sydney at 09:20. It usually means stolen credentials.\n- Limitation: VPNs and proxies can hide real location, so geofencing is one signal, not a complete control.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Authentication vs authorization:** authentication = **who you are**; authorization = **what you may do**. Logging in is authentication; being blocked from a folder is authorization.\n- **RADIUS vs TACACS+:** RADIUS = **UDP**, user **network access**, encrypts **only the password**, combines authN/authZ. TACACS+ = **TCP 49**, **device administration**, encrypts the **whole payload**, separates AAA with per-command authorization.\n- **Hashing vs encryption:** encryption is **reversible with a key** (confidentiality); hashing is **one-way** (integrity).\n- **Symmetric vs asymmetric:** one shared key and fast vs a public/private key pair and slow but solves key exchange.\n- **SAML vs OAuth:** SAML = **authentication/SSO** with XML assertions; OAuth 2.0 = **delegated authorization**; OIDC adds identity to OAuth.\n- **TOTP vs HOTP:** TOTP changes with **time**; HOTP changes with a **counter**.\n- **CRL vs OCSP:** CRL = a **downloaded list**; OCSP = a **real-time query** about one certificate.\n- **Two passwords vs MFA:** two things you know is **single-factor**. MFA needs different categories.\n- **RBAC vs DAC vs MAC:** roles vs owner decides vs classification labels.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **AES:** the standard symmetric cipher for bulk data.\n- **Certificate authority (CA):** trusted entity that signs certificates.\n- **CRL / OCSP:** ways to check whether a certificate is revoked.\n- **HSM:** tamper-resistant hardware for generating and storing keys.\n- **IdP / SP:** identity provider (authenticates) and service provider (the app) in SAML.\n- **Kerberos:** ticket-based authentication in Active Directory, using a KDC.\n- **Least privilege:** only the access a job requires.\n- **MFA:** authentication using two or more different factor categories.\n- **Perfect forward secrecy:** ephemeral session keys protect past sessions if a long-term key leaks.\n- **Self-signed certificate:** a certificate not issued by a trusted CA; not trusted by default.\n- **Supplicant / authenticator / authentication server:** the three 802.1X roles.\n- **TOTP:** a one-time code derived from a shared secret and the current time.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Encrypts the whole payload, per-command authorization for admins\" → TACACS+. \"Wi-Fi or VPN users, UDP 1812/1813\" → RADIUS. \"Log in once, use many web apps, XML assertions\" → SSO with SAML. \"Smart card + PIN\" → MFA; \"password + PIN\" → not MFA. \"Stolen laptop\" → encryption at rest; \"sniffed traffic\" → encryption in transit.","4|Physical security":"# Physical security\n\nPhysical access defeats most logical controls: someone standing at a switch can plug in a rogue device, connect to the console port and reset passwords, or simply carry the equipment away. N10-009 focuses on two physical controls, **cameras** and **locks**, but you also need to know the supporting measures that stop tailgating, deter intruders, and protect what's on screens and desks.\n\n## Why physical security matters\n\n- **Console access = control.** Most switches and routers allow **password recovery** from the console port during boot. Anyone who can touch the device can often take it over.\n- **Rogue devices:** an attacker with access to a wiring closet can add an unauthorized access point or a small sniffer on a spare port.\n- **Theft and damage:** laptops, drives, and backup media walk out the door; equipment can be unplugged or sabotaged.\n- **Layering:** physical security is the outermost layer of **defense in depth**: perimeter → building → room → rack → device.\n\nControls are often described by what they do:\n\n| Control type | Purpose | Physical examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Deterrent** | Discourage the attempt | Warning signs, visible cameras, lighting, fences |\n| **Preventive** | Stop the attempt | Locks, bollards, access control vestibules, locked racks |\n| **Detective** | Notice and record that it happened | Cameras (recording), alarms, motion sensors, door contacts, tamper seals |\n\nSome controls fit more than one type: a visible camera both **deters** and **detects**.\n\n## Cameras and surveillance\n\nVideo surveillance (CCTV or IP cameras) has two jobs:\n- **Deter:** people behave differently when they can see a camera.\n- **Detect and record:** footage shows **who** entered, **when**, and **what** they did, which supports investigations and evidence.\n\n| Feature | What it does | When to use it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PTZ (pan-tilt-zoom)** | An operator can **remotely steer and zoom** the camera | Large areas such as parking lots or lobbies; follow an incident live |\n| **Fixed** | Points at one spot | Doorways, specific racks |\n| **Infrared (IR) / night vision** | Records in **low light or darkness** | Outdoor areas, unlit rooms after hours |\n| **Motion detection** | Records or alerts when movement occurs | Saves storage; alerts on after-hours activity |\n| **Wide-angle** | Covers a broad field of view | Rooms and corridors |\n\n**Placement:** cover **entrances and exits, server rooms, data center aisles, and rack rows** with **no blind spots**. A camera that sees the door but not the racks can't show who touched a switch.\n\n**Protect the system itself:** store recordings securely, set a **retention period** long enough for investigations, sync camera clocks (NTP) so timestamps are trustworthy, and put IP cameras on their own segment so they aren't an easy way into the network.\n\nScenario: \"Equipment went missing from the server room overnight, and nobody knows who was there\" → the room needs **cameras** (detective) and **logged badge access**.\n\n## Locks and door access\n\n| Lock type | How it opens | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Traditional keyed lock** | Physical key | Simple, no power needed | Keys can be copied; a lost key means **re-keying**; no record of who entered |\n| **Cipher lock** | **Code on a keypad** (mechanical or electronic) | No key to lose | Codes get **shared or observed** (shoulder surfing); rotate codes regularly |\n| **Badge / proximity card reader** | **Something you have**: RFID or smart card | **Every entry is logged**; a lost badge is **disabled instantly**; access can be limited by area and time | Badges can be lent or cloned |\n| **Biometric lock** | Fingerprint, face, iris, palm | Tied to the **person**, can't be lent | Cost; false accepts and false rejects |\n| **Multifactor door access** | Two different factors: **badge + PIN**, or badge + biometric | A stolen badge alone isn't enough | Slower entry |\n\nWhy badge systems win in most offices: when an employee leaves or loses a badge, an admin **revokes it centrally** in seconds, and the **audit trail** shows every door it opened. With metal keys you'd have to change the locks and you'd never know who used them.\n\nScenario: \"We need to know exactly who entered the wiring closet and be able to cut off a lost credential immediately\" → **badge reader** with central management. \"Even if a badge is stolen, it shouldn't open the data center\" → **multifactor** (badge + PIN or biometric).\n\n## Securing equipment: racks, closets, and devices\n\n- **Locked network closets / IDFs and MDFs:** lockable doors with **access logging** prevent tampering, rogue devices, and console access.\n- **Locking racks and cabinets:** in shared spaces such as **colocation** data centers, rack locks stop other tenants from reaching your devices and console ports.\n- **Cable locks:** attach a **laptop** (via its security slot) to a desk or other immovable object to prevent quick theft.\n- **Tamper-evident seals or tape:** don't stop access, but **show whether a device or case was opened** without authorization.\n- **Disable or protect unused ports and consoles:** shut unused switch ports, set console passwords, and log out of console sessions.\n\nScenario: \"Your company rents half a rack in a shared data center\" → use a **locking cabinet** for your equipment.\n\n## Preventing tailgating\n\n**Tailgating** (also called **piggybacking**) is when an unauthorized person follows an authorized person through a door before it closes. A badge reader alone can't stop it, because the door is already open.\n\n- **Access control vestibule** (formerly called a **mantrap**): a small space between **two interlocking doors**. Only one door can be open at a time, and the second door won't open until the person is verified, so a follower gets **trapped** between them.\n- **Turnstiles** allow one person per credential.\n- **Security guards** check badges and challenge strangers.\n- **Visitor logs and escorts:** record who is on-site, issue visitor badges, and keep visitors **supervised** in sensitive areas.\n- **Security awareness training:** teach staff not to hold doors open for people they don't know and to challenge anyone without a visible badge.\n\n## Perimeter, detection, and deterrence\n\n| Control | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Bollards** | Short, sturdy posts that **stop vehicles** from ramming entrances |\n| **Fences and gates** | Define the perimeter and slow intruders |\n| **Lighting** | **Deters** and helps cameras see |\n| **Warning signs** | **Deter** (\"Authorized personnel only\", \"CCTV in operation\") |\n| **Alarm systems** | **Door contacts** and **motion sensors** trigger an alert when a door opens or movement occurs after hours |\n| **Security guards** | Can deter, detect, and respond; can make judgment calls |\n\nScenario: \"Alert us if the server room door opens at 2 a.m.\" → **alarm system with door contact sensors**. \"Prevent a car from being driven through the lobby glass\" → **bollards**.\n\n## Protecting information in the workspace\n\nPhysical security also covers what people can **see** or **pick up**.\n- **Privacy screens (filters):** narrow the viewing angle of a monitor to prevent **shoulder surfing** in open offices or public places.\n- **Clean desk policy:** no sensitive documents, sticky-note passwords, or unlocked devices left out when away. Lock screens when stepping away.\n- **Secure disposal:** shred paper; wipe or destroy drives before disposal.\n- **Faraday cage:** a conductive enclosure that **blocks electromagnetic signals** from entering or leaving, used to protect sensitive equipment or stop wireless eavesdropping.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Badge reader vs cipher lock:** badge = something you **have**, logged per person and revocable; cipher lock = a **shared code**, not tied to one person.\n- **Access control vestibule vs badge reader:** a badge reader controls **who unlocks the door**; a vestibule stops **someone following them through**.\n- **Deterrent vs detective:** signs and lighting **discourage**; cameras recording and alarms **detect**. A visible camera does both.\n- **Tamper-evident seal vs lock:** a lock **prevents** opening; a seal only **shows** that it was opened.\n- **Bollards vs fences:** bollards stop **vehicles**; fences stop and slow **people**.\n- **PTZ vs infrared:** PTZ = **movable and zoomable**; infrared = **sees in the dark**.\n- **Privacy screen vs clean desk:** privacy screen protects what's **on the monitor**; clean desk protects what's **on the desk**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Access control vestibule:** two interlocking doors that prevent tailgating (formerly mantrap).\n- **Biometric lock:** opens with a fingerprint, face, or iris scan.\n- **Bollard:** a post that stops vehicles.\n- **Cipher lock:** opened with a keypad code.\n- **Faraday cage:** an enclosure that blocks electromagnetic signals.\n- **PTZ camera:** pan-tilt-zoom camera controlled remotely.\n- **Tailgating:** following an authorized person through a secured door.\n- **Tamper-evident seal:** shows whether equipment was opened.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Stop someone following an employee through a door\" → access control vestibule. \"Revocable entry with an audit trail\" → badge reader. \"Badge + PIN at the door\" → multifactor physical access. \"Stop vehicles\" → bollards. Cameras both **deter** and **detect**.","4|Deception technologies":"# Deception technologies\n\nDeception technologies plant **fake targets** that no legitimate user should ever touch. Because real users have no reason to interact with them, any interaction is a strong, **low-false-positive** sign of an intruder, and watching the intruder shows defenders their tools and methods. N10-009 names **honeypots** and **honeynets**; you should also know honeyfiles, honeytokens, DNS sinkholes, and tarpits.\n\n## Why deception works\n\nMost detection tools look for **known bad** behavior: an IDS signature, a malware hash, a known malicious IP. An attacker using a new technique or legitimate admin tools can slip past them.\n\nDeception flips the logic: instead of recognizing the attack, you create something that **should never be touched**.\n- **No legitimate purpose = no normal traffic.** A decoy server isn't in DNS for users, isn't in any application, and isn't advertised. If someone connects to it, they're scanning or exploring.\n- **High-fidelity alerts:** very few false positives, so the security team trusts and acts on them.\n- **Detects unknown (zero-day) techniques:** it doesn't matter what tool the attacker uses; touching the decoy *is* the alert.\n- **Intelligence:** watching what the attacker does on the decoy reveals their tools, techniques, and goals.\n- **Wastes attacker time** that would otherwise be spent on real systems.\n\nDeception **complements** firewalls, IDS/IPS, and endpoint protection. It doesn't replace them, and it doesn't **prevent** attacks on real systems.\n\n## Honeypots\n\nA **honeypot** is a **single decoy system**, such as a fake file server, database, web server, or an exposed SSH or RDP service, set up to attract attackers and record what they do.\n\nHoneypots differ in how much of a real system they offer:\n\n| Interaction level | What it is | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Low-interaction** | **Emulates** a few services and canned responses (e.g. a fake SSH login prompt) | Easy to deploy, **low risk**, little maintenance | Gathers **limited intelligence**; skilled attackers may spot it |\n| **High-interaction** | A **real operating system** and services the attacker can fully use | **Rich intelligence**: commands, tools, malware samples | **Higher risk**: a compromised real system could be used to attack others; needs careful isolation and monitoring |\n\n- A good honeypot looks **believable**: realistic hostname (e.g. `FIN-DB02`), plausible services, maybe some fake data.\n- Scenario: \"The security team wants to see which exploits attackers try against SSH, with minimal risk\" → a **low-interaction** honeypot. \"Researchers want to capture the full toolkit an intruder installs\" → a **high-interaction** honeypot, carefully isolated.\n\n## Honeynets\n\nA **honeynet** is a **network of honeypots**: several decoy systems (servers, workstations, maybe network devices) connected together to simulate a **realistic environment**.\n\n- Lets defenders watch **multi-step behavior**: initial access, **lateral movement** between hosts, privilege escalation, and data staging.\n- More convincing than a lone honeypot, so attackers stay longer.\n- Typically sits behind a **controlled gateway** (sometimes called a honeywall) that captures all traffic and limits what can leave, so the honeynet can't be used to attack the outside world.\n- Costs more to build and maintain.\n\nRule of thumb: **one decoy → honeypot; many decoys forming a fake network → honeynet.**\n\n## Honeyfiles and honeytokens\n\nDeception doesn't have to be a whole system. Small pieces of fake data work too.\n\n| Technique | What it is | What triggers the alert | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Honeyfile** | A **fake document** with an attractive name | The file is **opened** or copied | `passwords.xlsx` or `2025-salaries.docx` on a file share |\n| **Honeytoken** | **Fake data** planted where an attacker would find it | The data is **used** | A **bogus admin credential**, a fake **API key**, a fake **database record** or customer, a fake email address |\n\n- If a honeytoken credential ever tries to log in, someone **stole it** from wherever it was planted (a config file, a password manager, a database dump).\n- A fake customer record with a unique email address: if that address ever receives spam or appears in a breach dump, the database was **exfiltrated**.\n- A honeyfile is really a type of honeytoken in file form.\n\n## DNS sinkholes and tarpits\n\n**DNS sinkhole:** the DNS server returns a **controlled address** for **known-malicious or decoy domain names** instead of the real one.\n- Infected machines trying to reach a malware command-and-control domain are sent to a harmless internal server, which **blocks** the connection and **reveals which hosts are infected** (they're the ones querying it).\n- **Fake DNS records** can also point attackers toward decoy systems to lure them into monitored honeypots.\n\n**Tarpit:** a service that **deliberately slows down malicious connections**, such as port scanners, worms, or spam bots. It accepts the connection and then responds extremely slowly, **wasting the attacker's time and resources**.\n\nScenario: \"Several workstations keep trying to resolve a known botnet domain\" → a **DNS sinkhole** both stops the traffic and identifies the infected hosts.\n\n## Placement and isolation\n\nWhere you put a decoy decides what it can catch:\n\n| Placement | Detects |\n|---|---|\n| **Internet-facing** (e.g. in a screened subnet / DMZ) | **External** scanning and attack trends; generates lots of noise because the internet is constantly scanned |\n| **Internal segments, alongside real servers** | Attackers **already inside** performing **lateral movement**, and malicious insiders; very few false alarms |\n\n- **Isolation is essential.** If a honeypot isn't segmented, an attacker who compromises it can use it as a **launch point** into real systems. Put decoys in their own segment, restrict outbound traffic, and monitor them closely.\n- Don't let a decoy hold **real data** or real credentials.\n- Make sure decoy alerts reach the **SIEM** or security team, or nobody will notice the attacker.\n\nScenario: \"We already have edge IDS; we want to know if an attacker gets past it and starts exploring\" → put honeypots on **internal** segments.\n\n## Legal and operational considerations\n\n- Deception is for **observation and alerting**. Never use a honeypot to **\"hack back\"** or attack the attacker's systems; that is illegal in most places.\n- **Monitoring and data capture have legal implications** (privacy, wiretapping and data-protection laws). Consult **legal and policy** teams before deploying, and document the purpose.\n- Decoys need **maintenance**: patch what should be patched, keep fake data believable, and tune alerts.\n- Don't create real vulnerabilities by accident: a high-interaction honeypot that attacks other organizations could create liability.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Honeypot vs honeynet:** honeypot = **one** decoy system; honeynet = a **network** of decoys.\n- **Honeyfile vs honeytoken:** honeyfile = a fake **document** that alerts when **opened**; honeytoken = fake **data** (credential, key, record) that alerts when **used**.\n- **Low vs high interaction:** low = **emulated** services, safe, less intel; high = **real** system, rich intel, more risk.\n- **DNS sinkhole vs tarpit:** a sinkhole **redirects** name lookups to a controlled address; a tarpit **slows down** connections.\n- **Deception vs IDS:** an IDS recognizes **known patterns** on real traffic; deception alerts on **any contact** with a fake target.\n- **Deception vs prevention:** a honeypot **detects and observes**; it doesn't block attacks on real systems.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Honeypot:** a single decoy system that attracts attackers.\n- **Honeynet:** a network of honeypots simulating a realistic environment.\n- **Honeyfile:** a bait document that alerts when opened.\n- **Honeytoken:** fake data, such as a bogus credential, that alerts when used.\n- **DNS sinkhole:** DNS that returns a controlled address for malicious or decoy names.\n- **Tarpit:** a service that deliberately slows malicious connections.\n- **Lateral movement:** an attacker moving from one internal system to another.\n\n> **Exam tip:** One decoy → honeypot. Many decoys forming a fake network → honeynet. Fake credential that triggers an alert → honeytoken. Fake \"passwords.xlsx\" → honeyfile. Catch attackers already inside → internal honeypots. Biggest risk → poor isolation lets attackers pivot to real systems.","4|Security terminology":"# Security terminology\n\nSecurity questions are full of words that sound alike: vulnerability, threat, exploit, risk. They mean different things, and the exam often tests whether you can tell them apart in a scenario. This lesson defines the core terms, the **CIA triad**, how vulnerabilities are tracked and scored, and how organizations assess and respond to risk.\n\n## Vulnerability, threat, and exploit\n\n| Term | Definition | Ask yourself | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Vulnerability** | A **weakness** that could be exploited | \"What's wrong with *our* system?\" | Unpatched firmware, **default password**, open unused port, misconfigured firewall rule, unencrypted protocol (Telnet) |\n| **Threat** | Anything that **could cause harm** by exploiting a vulnerability | \"Who or what could hurt us?\" | Hacker, malware, **insider**, competitor, flood, fire, power outage |\n| **Exploit** | The **code or technique** that **takes advantage** of a vulnerability | \"How would they do it?\" | A script that abuses a buffer overflow, a login attempt with the vendor's default password |\n\n- A **threat actor** is the person or group behind a threat: cybercriminals, nation-states, hacktivists, script kiddies, and **insiders**.\n- **Insider threat:** a current or former employee, contractor, or partner who **misuses legitimate access**, maliciously (a disgruntled employee) or accidentally (someone who clicks a phishing link). Insiders are dangerous because they're already past perimeter controls.\n- **Threats aren't only people.** Natural disasters and equipment failures are threats too.\n\nHow they fit together: a **threat** uses an **exploit** against a **vulnerability**. Example: an attacker (threat) runs a public script (exploit) against a VPN appliance with missing patches (vulnerability).\n\nYou **control vulnerabilities** (patch, harden, configure). You usually **can't control threats** (you can't stop hackers existing), which is why most security work focuses on removing vulnerabilities and reducing impact.\n\n## Risk\n\n**Risk** is the **likelihood** that a threat exploits a vulnerability, combined with the **impact** if it does.\n\nRisk ≈ **likelihood × impact**\n\n| Situation | Likelihood | Impact | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Internet-facing server with a known, actively exploited flaw holding customer data | High | High | **High**: fix first |\n| Same flaw on an isolated lab PC with no data | Medium | Low | Lower |\n| A meteor strike on the data center | Very low | Very high | Low overall |\n\n- No vulnerability → no risk from that threat. No threat → no risk from that vulnerability. Risk needs both.\n- **Controls reduce likelihood** (patching, firewalls, MFA) **or impact** (backups, redundancy, encryption, segmentation).\n- **Residual risk** is the risk that **remains after controls** are applied. It's never zero.\n- **Risk appetite/tolerance:** how much risk the organization is willing to accept.\n\n## The CIA triad\n\nThe **CIA triad** is the three goals of information security. Every attack harms at least one of them, and every control protects at least one.\n\n| Principle | Means | Violated by | Protected by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Confidentiality** | Only **authorized people can see** the data | Eavesdropping, sniffing, data theft, shoulder surfing | **Encryption**, access control, MFA |\n| **Integrity** | Data is **accurate and unaltered**, changed only by authorized people | **Tampering** in transit or storage, on-path attacks altering data | **Hashing**, **digital signatures**, change control, file integrity monitoring |\n| **Availability** | Systems and data are **usable when needed** | **DoS/DDoS**, ransomware locking files, power or hardware failure, deleted data | **Redundancy** (links, power, servers), **backups**, UPS, DDoS protection, load balancing |\n\nScenarios:\n- \"An attacker reads the payroll file\" → **confidentiality**.\n- \"An attacker changes transaction amounts in transit\" → **integrity**.\n- \"A DDoS takes the website offline\" → **availability**.\n- Ransomware can hit several: files are unusable (**availability**), and if they are also stolen and leaked, **confidentiality**.\n\n## Non-repudiation and authenticity\n\nTwo related goals often appear alongside CIA:\n- **Authenticity:** proof that data or a user is **genuinely who or what it claims** to be.\n- **Non-repudiation:** assurance that someone **can't deny** having performed an action, such as sending a message or approving a change.\n\n**How it's achieved:** **digital signatures** (only the holder of the private key could have signed) and **audit logs** tied to individual accounts.\n- Shared accounts (everyone logs in as \"admin\") destroy non-repudiation: you can't prove which person made a change.\n- A symmetric shared key doesn't give non-repudiation either, because either party could have created the message.\n\nScenario: \"A manager claims they never approved a firewall change\" → **digitally signed approvals** and **per-user audit logs** provide non-repudiation.\n\n## Zero-days, CVE, and CVSS\n\n| Term | What it is | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Zero-day** | A vulnerability **unknown to the vendor**, or with **no patch available yet** | Signatures and patches don't exist yet, so defenders rely on layered controls and behavior-based detection |\n| **CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures)** | A **public identifier** for a known vulnerability, e.g. `CVE-2024-12345` | Everyone (vendors, scanners, admins) refers to the same flaw by the same ID |\n| **CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System)** | A **severity score from 0.0 to 10.0** | Helps **prioritize** patching |\n\nCVSS ratings:\n\n| Score | Rating |\n|---|---|\n| 0.0 | None |\n| 0.1–3.9 | Low |\n| 4.0–6.9 | Medium |\n| 7.0–8.9 | High |\n| 9.0–10.0 | Critical |\n\n- CVSS measures **severity of the flaw**, not your actual **risk**. A critical CVE on a device that's isolated may matter less than a high CVE on an internet-facing server. Combine the score with your own exposure and asset value.\n- Scenario: \"A scanner reports 200 findings; where do we start?\" → sort by **CVSS** and exposure, and patch critical, internet-facing systems first.\n\n## Attack surface and defense in depth\n\n**Attack surface** is **every point where an attacker could try to get in or get data out**: open ports, running services, user accounts, web apps, wireless networks, remote access, physical entrances, and even employees (phishing).\n- **Shrink it** by **hardening**: disable unused services, close unused ports, remove default and unused accounts, shut unused switch ports, and remove unneeded software.\n- Every new service, device, or cloud resource **adds** to the attack surface.\n\n**Defense in depth** means **multiple layers of controls**, so if one fails, another still stops or detects the attacker.\n\n| Layer | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Physical** | Locks, badges, cameras |\n| **Network perimeter** | Firewalls, IPS, screened subnet |\n| **Internal network** | Segmentation, VLANs, NAC/802.1X |\n| **Host** | Patching, endpoint protection, host firewall |\n| **Application** | Secure coding, input validation, WAF |\n| **Data** | Encryption, access control, backups |\n| **People and policy** | Training, least privilege, change management |\n\nScenario: \"The firewall was misconfigured, but the attacker was still stopped by MFA and segmentation\" → **defense in depth** working.\n\n## Assessing risk: risk assessments, scans, and pen tests\n\n- **Risk assessment:** identify **assets** (what you're protecting), the **threats** and **vulnerabilities** they face, estimate likelihood and impact, then **prioritize** risks for treatment. Results are often kept in a **risk register**.\n- **Vulnerability scan:** an **automated** tool checks systems for **known weaknesses** (missing patches, weak configurations, open ports) and reports **potential** issues. It doesn't break in. Run it regularly.\n   - **Credentialed** scans log in for a deeper, more accurate view; **non-credentialed** scans see what an outside attacker sees.\n   - Scans can produce **false positives** that need verification.\n- **Penetration test:** a skilled tester **actively exploits** weaknesses to **prove real impact**, simulating an attacker. Requires **written authorization** and agreed **rules of engagement** (scope, timing, what's off-limits).\n\n| Aspect | Vulnerability scan | Penetration test |\n|---|---|---|\n| Method | Automated, finds and reports | Manual and automated, **exploits** |\n| Answers | \"What weaknesses might exist?\" | \"What could an attacker actually do?\" |\n| Frequency | Often (weekly, monthly) | Periodically (e.g. yearly) |\n| Risk to systems | Low | Higher; needs authorization |\n\n## Risk responses\n\nAfter assessing a risk, the organization picks a response:\n\n| Response | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mitigate** (reduce) | Add **controls** to lower likelihood or impact | Patch, add a firewall rule, enable MFA |\n| **Transfer** (share) | Shift the **financial impact** to someone else | **Cyber insurance**, outsourcing to a provider under contract |\n| **Avoid** | **Stop the risky activity** entirely | Decide not to launch a risky service; retire an insecure legacy system |\n| **Accept** | **Formally acknowledge** the risk and take no further action | Accept the **residual risk** when the cost of more controls exceeds the benefit |\n\n- Acceptance should be a **documented decision** by someone with authority, not just ignoring the risk.\n- Transfer moves the **cost**, not the responsibility: if customer data leaks, the company's reputation still suffers.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Vulnerability vs threat:** the vulnerability is the **weakness**; the threat is **who or what** could use it.\n- **Threat vs exploit:** the threat is the **actor or event**; the exploit is the **tool or technique**.\n- **Risk vs vulnerability:** a vulnerability is a fact about a system; risk adds **likelihood and impact**.\n- **Integrity vs confidentiality:** reading data harms **confidentiality**; changing it harms **integrity**.\n- **CVE vs CVSS:** CVE is the **ID**; CVSS is the **severity score**.\n- **Vulnerability scan vs pen test:** a scan **finds possible** weaknesses; a pen test **exploits** them to prove impact.\n- **Transfer vs avoid:** transfer **keeps the activity** but shifts the cost (insurance); avoid **stops the activity**.\n- **Residual risk vs accepted risk:** residual risk is what **remains after controls**; accepting it is the **decision** to live with it.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Attack surface:** all points where an attacker could get in or get data out.\n- **CIA triad:** confidentiality, integrity, availability.\n- **CVE:** Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures, a public ID for a known flaw.\n- **CVSS:** a 0–10 severity score for vulnerabilities.\n- **Defense in depth:** layered security controls.\n- **Exploit:** code or technique that takes advantage of a vulnerability.\n- **Insider threat:** someone with legitimate access who misuses it.\n- **Non-repudiation:** inability to deny having performed an action.\n- **Residual risk:** risk remaining after controls.\n- **Threat:** anything that could cause harm by exploiting a vulnerability.\n- **Vulnerability:** a weakness that could be exploited.\n- **Zero-day:** a flaw unknown to the vendor or without a patch.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Weakness = vulnerability. Bad actor or event = threat. The attack tool = exploit. Likelihood × impact = risk. DDoS hits **availability**; tampering hits **integrity**; eavesdropping hits **confidentiality**. Insurance = transfer; stop doing it = avoid.","4|Audits & compliance":"# Audits & compliance\n\nLaws, regulations, and industry standards set **minimum** security and privacy requirements that an organization must meet. **Compliance** means meeting those requirements, and an **audit** is how you prove it with evidence. For Network+, focus on **PCI DSS**, **GDPR**, and **data locality**, plus the everyday network practices that make an audit go smoothly.\n\n## Compliance, regulations, and audits\n\n- A **regulation** or **law** is mandatory (e.g. GDPR, HIPAA). Breaking it can mean fines or legal action.\n- An **industry standard** is required by contract or by an industry body (e.g. **PCI DSS**, required by the card brands for anyone who accepts cards).\n- An internal **policy** is the organization's own rule set, written to satisfy those outside requirements.\n- A **compliance audit** is an **independent review** that checks, using **evidence**, whether required controls exist and actually work. Audits can be **internal** (your own team, for self-checking) or **external** (an independent auditor or assessor, often required).\n\n**Why \"compliant\" is not the same as \"secure\":** regulations describe a **minimum baseline** at a point in time. An organization can pass an audit and still be breached by a threat the standard doesn't cover, or by a control that drifted after the audit. Treat compliance as the floor, not the ceiling.\n\nNon-compliance has real consequences: **fines**, loss of the right to process card payments, lawsuits, mandatory breach disclosures, and reputational damage.\n\n## PCI DSS\n\nThe **Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard** applies to **any organization that stores, processes, or transmits credit/debit card data**, from a huge retailer to a small shop with one card terminal. It is an **industry standard** enforced by the card brands, not a government law.\n\nThe systems that handle card data form the **cardholder data environment (CDE)**. Network-related requirements include:\n- **Install and maintain network security controls** (firewalls, router ACLs) that protect the CDE and deny all traffic not explicitly needed.\n- **Secure configurations:** change **vendor default passwords** and remove unnecessary services.\n- **Protect stored card data,** and **never store sensitive authentication data** (full magnetic-stripe track data, the **CVV/CVC** code, PINs) after authorization.\n- **Encrypt cardholder data with strong cryptography** when it crosses **open or public networks** (internet, wireless, cellular).\n- **Restrict access** on a need-to-know basis, with unique IDs and MFA for access into the CDE.\n- **Log and monitor** all access to card data and network resources. Keep audit logs for at least **one year**, with the most recent **three months immediately available**.\n- **Test regularly:** quarterly **vulnerability scans** and periodic **penetration tests**.\n\n**Segmentation and scope:** any system that can connect to the CDE is \"in scope\" for PCI DSS. Isolating the CDE on its own segment, with firewalls allowing only required flows, **reduces the number of systems in scope**, which cuts cost and audit effort. Segmentation isn't strictly required, but without it the **whole flat network is in scope**.\n\n> Scenario: a store's card terminals sit on the same flat VLAN as office PCs and guest Wi-Fi. Every one of those devices is now in PCI scope. Moving the terminals to an isolated CDE segment shrinks the audit dramatically.\n\n## GDPR\n\nThe **General Data Protection Regulation** is a European Union **law** that protects the **personal data and privacy of individuals in the EU**. It applies to **any organization, anywhere in the world**, that offers goods or services to people in the EU or monitors their behavior. A US company with EU customers must comply.\n\n**Personal data** is any information that identifies a person directly or indirectly: names, email addresses, IP addresses, location data, device IDs.\n\nKey points:\n- **Lawful basis** for processing (such as consent or a contract), and only collecting what's needed (**data minimization**).\n- **Individual (data subject) rights:** access to their data, correction, objection, portability, and the **right to erasure** (\"right to be forgotten\"), meaning they can ask for their data to be deleted.\n- **Breach notification:** a qualifying personal data breach must generally be reported to the **supervisory authority within 72 hours** of becoming aware of it. Affected individuals must be told without undue delay if the risk to them is high.\n- **Data protection officer (DPO):** required for some organizations (public bodies, large-scale monitoring or sensitive data processing). The DPO **oversees data protection strategy and compliance** and is the contact point for regulators.\n- **Privacy by design:** build protection (encryption, access control, pseudonymization) into systems from the start.\n- **Penalties** can reach up to **4% of global annual turnover** or €20 million, whichever is higher.\n- **International transfers** of personal data outside the EU are restricted unless adequate protection is in place.\n\n## Data locality (data residency)\n\n**Data locality** (also called **data residency** or data sovereignty) means **laws or contracts require data to be stored and processed within a specific country or region**. Examples: a government requiring its citizens' health records to stay in-country, or EU personal data that can't be freely sent abroad.\n\nWhy it matters to a network team:\n- **Cloud services replicate data globally by default.** You must choose **cloud regions inside the required jurisdiction** and **restrict replication, backups, and failover** to those regions.\n- **Backups and DR sites** count too. A disaster recovery copy in another country can break the rule.\n- **CDNs, SaaS tools, and logging services** may store copies of data elsewhere, so check their data location options.\n- Data in a country is subject to **that country's laws** (including government access), which is why regulators care.\n\n> Scenario: a company must keep customer data in Germany, but its cloud storage is set to geo-redundant replication across continents. Fix: use a region in Germany (or the EU, if allowed) and limit replication to paired regions inside the boundary.\n\n## Other regulations you may see\n\nThe Network+ objectives name PCI DSS and GDPR, but questions may use these as distractors, so know what each covers:\n\n| Regulation | Covers |\n|---|---|\n| **PCI DSS** | Payment **card** data (industry standard) |\n| **GDPR** | Personal data of people in the **EU** |\n| **HIPAA** | US **patient health information** (PHI) |\n| **SOX** (Sarbanes-Oxley) | Accuracy of **financial reporting** by US public companies |\n| **GLBA** | Customer data at US **financial institutions** |\n| **FERPA** | US **student education records** |\n| **CCPA** | Personal data of **California** residents |\n\n## Audit practices and evidence\n\nAuditors don't take your word for it; they want **evidence**. Good network operations generate that evidence naturally:\n- **Documented policies and procedures** (acceptable use, password, change management).\n- **Change records** showing every change was requested, approved, and tested.\n- **Configuration baselines** and proof that devices match them.\n- **Logs** of access and security events, kept for the required **retention period**, with accurate timestamps from **NTP** so events can be correlated.\n- **Access reviews** showing accounts and permissions are checked and stale ones removed.\n- **Network diagrams and data flow diagrams** showing where regulated data travels.\n\n**Firewall rule reviews:** rules accumulate over years. Periodic **internal audits of firewall rules** remove **unused, overly permissive (e.g. \"any any\"), or undocumented** rules. PCI DSS requires these reviews regularly.\n\n**Log retention:** regulations require keeping logs for a set time so incidents can be investigated and audits completed. Too short and you can't investigate; logs must also be protected from tampering.\n\n**Data classification:** labeling data by sensitivity (e.g. **public, internal, confidential, restricted**) tells everyone which protection and handling rules apply. You can't protect card data or personal data properly until you know where it is and how sensitive it is.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Compliant vs secure:** compliance proves you meet a **minimum** at audit time; security is ongoing. You can be compliant and still breached.\n- **PCI DSS vs GDPR:** PCI DSS = **card data**, an **industry standard**. GDPR = **EU personal data**, a **law**.\n- **GDPR vs data locality:** GDPR is about **how personal data is handled and people's rights**; data locality is about **where data physically lives**.\n- **GDPR applies where the data subject is, not where the company is:** a non-EU company serving EU residents must comply.\n- **HIPAA vs GDPR:** HIPAA = **US health** information; GDPR = **EU personal** data of any kind.\n- **72 hours vs one year:** 72 hours = GDPR **breach notification** deadline; one year = PCI DSS **log retention** (three months immediately available).\n- **Segmentation doesn't make you compliant:** it **reduces PCI scope**; the in-scope systems still need every control.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Compliance:** meeting the requirements of laws, regulations, and standards.\n- **Compliance audit:** an independent, evidence-based review of controls.\n- **PCI DSS:** Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard.\n- **CDE:** cardholder data environment, the systems that handle card data.\n- **Scope:** the systems a standard's requirements apply to.\n- **GDPR:** EU General Data Protection Regulation.\n- **Right to erasure:** the GDPR right to have personal data deleted.\n- **DPO:** data protection officer.\n- **Data locality / data residency:** keeping data within a required geographic or legal boundary.\n- **Data classification:** labeling data by sensitivity to drive protection rules.\n- **Log retention:** keeping logs for a required period.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Credit cards → **PCI DSS** (segment the CDE to reduce scope, encrypt over public networks, keep logs one year). EU personal data → **GDPR** (72-hour breach notice, right to erasure, DPO). \"Data must stay in-country\" → **data locality**: pick cloud regions inside the jurisdiction and restrict replication.","4|Network segmentation":"# Network segmentation\n\nSegmentation splits one network into separate **zones** (VLANs and subnets) and controls what may pass between them with **firewalls and ACLs**. Its main security job is to **limit lateral movement**: if one device is compromised, the attacker or malware can't freely reach everything else. Network+ expects you to know which kinds of devices get their own segment and why: **IoT, IIoT, SCADA/ICS/OT, guest, and BYOD**.\n\n## Why segment the network\n\nOn a **flat network**, every device can talk to every other device. One infected laptop, smart TV, or visitor's phone can scan and attack the whole organization. Segmentation fixes that:\n- **Limits lateral movement:** an attacker who compromises a camera on the IoT segment can't reach the finance servers.\n- **Contains malware outbreaks:** ransomware spreading on one segment is blocked at the firewall boundary.\n- **Reduces compliance scope:** isolating the PCI cardholder data environment means fewer systems are audited.\n- **Applies the right policy to each group:** strict rules for untrusted devices, broader access for managed corporate PCs.\n- **Improves performance and troubleshooting:** smaller broadcast domains and clearer traffic flows.\n\nThis fits the **zero trust** mindset: **assume breach** and design so that a breach in one place can't spread.\n\n## How segmentation is enforced\n\nA VLAN by itself only separates traffic at Layer 2. **The security comes from the rules at the Layer 3 boundary** where segments meet.\n- **VLANs and subnets:** each group gets its own VLAN and IP subnet.\n- **Firewalls or ACLs between segments:** the router, Layer 3 switch, or firewall that routes between VLANs enforces what's allowed.\n- **Default deny:** block everything between segments, then **allow only the specific flows required** (e.g. \"cameras may send video to the NVR on TCP 554, nothing else\").\n- **Micro-segmentation:** very fine-grained policies down to **individual workloads, VMs, or applications**, usually enforced in software (hypervisor or host firewalls) in data centers and clouds. Two web servers in the same subnet can be prevented from talking to each other.\n- **Physical separation / air gap:** for the most critical systems, no network connection at all.\n\n> Scenario: IoT devices are on their own VLAN, but the core switch routes between all VLANs with no ACLs. The VLAN provides no real protection; add firewall rules on the Layer 3 boundary.\n\n## IoT and IIoT\n\n**IoT (Internet of Things)** devices are network-connected \"things\": smart TVs, IP cameras, printers, thermostats, door locks, building HVAC controllers, smart speakers, and medical devices such as infusion pumps.\n\nWhy they need their own segment:\n- Often shipped with **default credentials** and **weak or no security**.\n- **Rarely patched**, and vendors may stop releasing updates.\n- Can't run endpoint security software.\n- Common targets for **botnets** (compromised cameras have powered huge DDoS attacks).\n\nBest practice: put them on a **dedicated IoT VLAN** with **default-deny rules** that allow only the flows each device needs (e.g. to its management server or cloud service), and block access from IoT into the corporate network. **Medical IoT** in hospitals gets the same treatment with strict access controls.\n\n**IIoT (Industrial Internet of Things)** is IoT in **industrial settings**: connected sensors, meters, and actuators in factories, oil and gas, utilities, and logistics. IIoT devices often feed data from the plant floor to analytics systems, so they bridge into OT environments and need the same careful isolation.\n\n## OT, ICS, and SCADA\n\nThese terms are related and the exam may ask you to tell them apart:\n\n| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OT (operational technology)** | The broad category: hardware and software that **monitor and control physical processes and equipment** | Factory machines, building systems, power equipment |\n| **ICS (industrial control systems)** | Systems that **control industrial processes**. Includes **SCADA, DCS, and PLCs** | A manufacturing line's control system |\n| **SCADA** | **Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition:** centrally monitors and controls equipment spread over **wide geographic areas** | Power grids, water treatment, pipelines |\n| **DCS** | **Distributed control system:** controls a process within one site | A chemical plant or refinery |\n| **PLC** | **Programmable logic controller:** a rugged industrial computer that directly controls machinery | Controlling a conveyor belt or valve |\n| **HMI** | **Human-machine interface:** the screen operators use to view and control the process | A control room dashboard |\n\n**IT vs OT priorities:** in IT, **confidentiality** usually comes first. In OT, **availability and safety** come first, because stopping a process or sending a wrong command could **damage equipment, harm people, or cut power or water**. That's why OT systems are patched cautiously and often run for decades.\n\n## Protecting OT and ICS networks\n\nOT/ICS networks are isolated from corporate IT because:\n- They use **legacy devices and protocols** (e.g. **Modbus, DNP3**) designed with **no authentication or encryption**. Anyone who can reach a PLC can often send it commands.\n- Devices can't easily be patched or run security software.\n- A compromise has **physical** consequences, not just data loss.\n\nKey protections:\n- **Industrial DMZ:** a buffer zone between IT and OT. IT systems talk only to servers in the DMZ (such as a data historian replica), and OT systems talk only to the DMZ, so there are **no direct connections between IT and OT**.\n- **Jump host (jump box):** engineers coming from IT must log in to a hardened, **monitored and audited** jump host first, then reach OT systems from there. Use MFA on it.\n- **Default-deny firewalls** allowing only specific industrial protocols between specific hosts.\n- **Air gap:** the most critical systems may be **physically isolated** from all other networks, including the internet. Remember an air gap is not perfect: **USB drives and laptops** carried in can still introduce malware.\n- **Monitoring** with OT-aware IDS that understand industrial protocols.\n\n## Guest networks\n\nVisitors need internet access but should **never reach internal systems**.\n- Separate **SSID** mapped to a separate **guest VLAN/subnet**.\n- Firewall rules allowing **internet access only**; deny all traffic to internal networks.\n- **Client isolation** so guests can't attack each other on the same Wi-Fi.\n- Often a **captive portal** for acceptance of terms, plus bandwidth limits and time-limited access.\n\n> Scenario: a visitor on guest Wi-Fi can ping the internal file server. The guest VLAN is missing deny rules toward internal subnets.\n\n## BYOD\n\n**BYOD (bring your own device)** means employees use **personal phones, tablets, and laptops** for work. The organization doesn't own or fully manage them, so it can't guarantee they're patched or malware-free.\n\nControls:\n- Place BYOD devices on a **separate segment** with limited access (e.g. email and specific web apps, not the whole internal network).\n- **NAC (network access control)** checks identity and device posture before granting access, and can assign the BYOD VLAN automatically.\n- **MDM (mobile device management)** enforces policies on the device: screen lock, encryption, and the ability to **remotely wipe corporate data**. Containerization keeps work data separate from personal data.\n- A clear **BYOD policy** users agree to.\n\nRelated models: **CYOD** (choose your own device from an approved list) and **COPE** (corporate-owned, personally enabled) give the company more control.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **IoT vs IIoT:** IoT = **consumer and office** smart devices; IIoT = **industrial** sensors and devices.\n- **OT vs ICS vs SCADA:** OT is the **umbrella** for systems controlling physical processes; ICS is **industrial control** within OT; SCADA is a type of ICS that spans **wide geographic areas**.\n- **SCADA vs DCS:** SCADA = **wide area** (grid, pipeline); DCS = **one site** (plant).\n- **IT vs OT priorities:** IT usually prioritizes **confidentiality**; OT prioritizes **availability and safety**.\n- **Guest vs BYOD:** guest = **visitors, internet only**; BYOD = **employees' personal devices** needing some corporate access, controlled with NAC/MDM.\n- **VLAN vs segmentation policy:** a VLAN alone doesn't block traffic; **firewall/ACL rules** between segments do.\n- **Segmentation vs micro-segmentation:** segmentation = **zones of many devices**; micro-segmentation = policy per **individual workload**.\n- **Industrial DMZ vs screened subnet:** both are buffer zones; the industrial DMZ sits between **IT and OT**, the screened subnet sits between the **internet and the internal network**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Lateral movement:** an attacker moving from one compromised system to others.\n- **IoT:** Internet of Things.\n- **IIoT:** Industrial Internet of Things.\n- **OT:** operational technology.\n- **ICS:** industrial control system.\n- **SCADA:** Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition.\n- **PLC:** programmable logic controller.\n- **Industrial DMZ:** buffer zone that prevents direct IT-to-OT connections.\n- **Jump host:** a hardened, monitored system used as the controlled entry point into a protected segment.\n- **Air gap:** physical isolation from other networks.\n- **BYOD:** bring your own device.\n- **MDM:** mobile device management.\n- **Micro-segmentation:** fine-grained, per-workload isolation.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Smart devices, cameras, HVAC\" → separate **IoT** segment with default-deny rules. \"Visitors\" → **guest** network, internet only. \"Personal phones\" → **BYOD** segment with **NAC/MDM**. \"Power grid / water plant controls\" → **SCADA/ICS/OT**, isolated with an industrial DMZ and jump host, where **availability and safety** come first.","4|Types of attacks":"# Types of attacks\n\nTo defend a network you need to recognize how it's attacked. Network+ expects you to identify an attack from its **symptoms** and match it to its **defense**: denial of service, Layer 2 attacks (VLAN hopping, MAC flooding, ARP poisoning), DNS attacks, rogue devices and evil twins, on-path attacks, social engineering, and malware.\n\n## Denial of service\n\nA **denial-of-service (DoS)** attack tries to make a service **unavailable** to legitimate users by exhausting its bandwidth, CPU, memory, or connection tables. It attacks **availability**, the \"A\" in the CIA triad.\n- **DoS:** comes from **one source**. Easier to block (filter that one IP).\n- **DDoS (distributed DoS):** comes from **many sources at once**, usually a **botnet**: thousands of compromised computers and IoT devices controlled remotely by an attacker through **command-and-control (C2)** servers. Much harder to block, because the traffic comes from everywhere and looks partly legitimate.\n\nCommon types:\n- **Volumetric:** floods the link with more traffic than it can carry (e.g. UDP floods).\n- **SYN flood (protocol attack):** sends huge numbers of TCP **SYN** packets but **never completes the three-way handshake**. The server's table fills with **half-open connections** and it can't accept real ones. Mitigation: **SYN cookies**, rate limiting, firewalls.\n- **Amplification / reflection:** the attacker sends **small requests with the victim's spoofed source IP** to open servers (**DNS**, **NTP**, **memcached**). The servers send **much larger responses to the victim**. A 60-byte query can trigger a response many times larger.\n- **Application layer (Layer 7):** floods of legitimate-looking HTTP requests that exhaust a web server.\n\nDefenses: ISP or cloud-based **DDoS scrubbing services**, CDNs, rate limiting, firewalls/IPS, and closing open resolvers so your servers can't be used for reflection.\n\n> Scenario: a web server suddenly can't accept new connections; its connection table shows thousands of half-open sessions from random IPs. That's a SYN flood.\n\n## VLAN hopping\n\n**VLAN hopping** lets an attacker on one VLAN send traffic into another VLAN they shouldn't reach, bypassing the separation VLANs provide. There are two methods:\n\n| Method | How it works | Defense |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Switch spoofing** | The attacker's device pretends to be a switch and uses **DTP** (Dynamic Trunking Protocol) to **negotiate a trunk**. A trunk carries all VLANs, so the attacker reaches every VLAN | **Disable DTP** and **hard-code user ports as access ports**; trunk only where needed |\n| **Double tagging** | The attacker, on the **native VLAN**, sends a frame with **two 802.1Q tags**. The first switch strips the outer tag (native VLAN traffic is untagged on trunks) and forwards it; the next switch reads the **inner tag** and delivers it into the target VLAN | Set the **native VLAN to an unused VLAN**, **tag the native VLAN**, and keep user ports out of it |\n\nDouble tagging is **one-way only**: the attacker can send into the victim VLAN but replies don't come back, so it's mostly used for attacks like DoS.\n\n## MAC flooding and STP manipulation\n\n**MAC flooding:** a switch learns which MAC address is on which port and stores it in its **MAC address (CAM) table**, which has limited space. The attacker sends thousands of frames with **fake source MACs** until the table is full. The switch can no longer learn real addresses, so it **floods frames out all ports like a hub**, and the attacker can **sniff traffic** meant for others.\n- Defense: **port security**, which **limits the number of MAC addresses allowed per port** and shuts down or restricts the port on a violation.\n\n**STP manipulation:** Spanning Tree Protocol elects a **root bridge** using BPDUs. An attacker connects a device that sends **superior BPDUs** (a lower bridge priority) and becomes the **root bridge**. Traffic then flows through the attacker's device (letting them sniff it), or the topology keeps recalculating and causes outages.\n- Defenses: **BPDU guard** on access ports (shuts the port if a BPDU arrives where no switch should be) and **root guard** on ports that should never lead to the root bridge.\n\n## ARP and DNS poisoning\n\n### ARP poisoning and ARP spoofing\n\n**ARP** maps an IP address to a MAC address on the local network. It has **no authentication**: hosts accept ARP replies even if they never asked.\n- **ARP spoofing:** the attacker sends **forged ARP replies** claiming \"the gateway's IP is at **my** MAC\".\n- **ARP poisoning:** the result: victims' **ARP caches now hold the false mapping**, so they send their traffic to the attacker. (The terms are often used interchangeably; spoofing is the forging, poisoning is the corrupted cache.)\n\nOnce the attacker's MAC is linked to the gateway's IP, the attacker is **on-path**: they can read, change, or drop the traffic, then forward it so nobody notices.\n- Defense: **Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI)**, which checks ARP packets against the **DHCP snooping binding table** and drops ones that don't match. Static ARP entries work for a few critical hosts.\n\n### DNS poisoning and DNS spoofing\n\nDNS turns names into IP addresses, so corrupting it sends users to the wrong place even when they type the correct name.\n- **DNS cache poisoning:** the attacker **injects false records into a DNS resolver's cache**, so every user of that resolver gets the malicious IP for, say, the bank's website.\n- **DNS spoofing:** more generally, **forging DNS responses** to a victim (e.g. answering faster than the real server, or from a rogue DNS server handed out by rogue DHCP).\n- **Hosts file modification:** malware edits the local **hosts file**, which is checked before DNS, to redirect a site. Redirecting users to fake sites this way is often called **pharming**.\n- Defenses: **DNSSEC** (digitally signs DNS records so forged answers fail validation), patched and locked-down resolvers, DNS filtering, and protecting the hosts file.\n\n> Scenario: users type the correct bank URL but land on a lookalike site, and only users of one resolver are affected. Suspect DNS cache poisoning on that resolver.\n\n## Rogue devices and evil twins\n\nA **rogue device** is any **unauthorized** device attached to the network: an AP, switch, router, or server someone plugged in without approval, whether malicious or just convenient.\n\n**Rogue DHCP server:** answers clients' DHCP requests and hands out a **malicious default gateway or DNS server**, putting the attacker on-path or redirecting users. Sometimes it's an accident (a home router plugged in backwards), causing clients to get wrong addresses.\n- **DHCP starvation:** the attacker first requests every address in the real server's pool with spoofed MACs, **exhausting the pool** so legitimate clients can't get addresses (and the rogue server answers instead).\n- Defense: **DHCP snooping** (only **trusted ports** facing real DHCP servers may send offers) and port security to stop starvation.\n\n**Rogue access point:** an **unauthorized AP connected to the wired network**, creating a back door that bypasses perimeter security. Defenses: **NAC/802.1X on switch ports**, **wireless IDS/IPS (WIDS/WIPS)** scanning for unknown APs, and regular wireless surveys.\n\n**Evil twin:** a malicious AP that **imitates a legitimate SSID** (same network name, often a stronger signal) to lure users into connecting, so the attacker can **capture credentials and traffic**. Unlike a rogue AP, it usually isn't connected to your network at all.\n- Attackers often send spoofed **deauthentication frames** to kick users off the real AP so they reconnect to the evil twin. Defense: **802.11w Protected Management Frames (PMF)**, which WPA3 requires.\n- Defenses: **802.1X/EAP with server certificate validation** (clients refuse an AP that can't prove its identity), WIPS, and user training (beware of free open Wi-Fi with a familiar name).\n\n## On-path attacks\n\nAn **on-path attack** (formerly **man-in-the-middle**) places the attacker **secretly between two parties** so they can **intercept, read, and alter** the communication while both sides think they're talking directly.\n\nWays an attacker gets on-path:\n- **ARP poisoning** on the local network.\n- **Rogue DHCP** handing out the attacker as the gateway.\n- **DNS spoofing** sending the victim to the attacker's server.\n- **Evil twin / rogue AP** carrying the victim's wireless traffic.\n\nWhat they can do: steal credentials and session cookies, inject malware into downloads, or **downgrade** connections to unencrypted versions.\n\nDefense: **encryption with proper certificate validation**, such as **TLS/HTTPS**, VPNs, and SSH. Even if the attacker is in the path, they can't read or alter encrypted traffic, and a fake certificate triggers a warning, which users **must not click through**. HSTS helps stop HTTPS-to-HTTP downgrades.\n\n## Social engineering\n\n**Social engineering** manipulates **people** rather than technology into giving up information or access. It exploits trust, urgency, fear, authority, and helpfulness.\n\n| Attack | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Phishing** | Fraudulent emails or messages that trick users into revealing information or running malware |\n| **Spear phishing** | Phishing **targeted at a specific person or group** using personalized details |\n| **Whaling** | Spear phishing aimed at **senior executives** |\n| **Vishing** | Phishing by **voice** call (e.g. fake \"IT support\" asking for a password) |\n| **Smishing** | Phishing by **SMS** text message |\n| **Dumpster diving** | Searching **trash** for documents, notes, or old drives with useful information |\n| **Shoulder surfing** | **Watching** someone type a password or read sensitive data |\n| **Tailgating** | Following an authorized person through a secure door **without their knowledge** |\n| **Piggybacking** | Following an authorized person through **with their consent** (they hold the door) |\n| **Pretexting** | Inventing a **believable scenario** (\"I'm the auditor, I need...\") to extract information |\n| **Baiting** | Leaving **infected USB drives** or offers for victims to pick up and use |\n| **Watering hole** | Compromising a **website the target group frequently visits** |\n\nDefenses: **security awareness training**, phishing simulations, verification procedures (call back on a known number), email filtering, shredding, privacy screens, and physical controls such as **access control vestibules (mantraps)**, badges, and guards.\n\n## Malware and password attacks\n\n**Malware** is malicious software. Types to recognize:\n- **Virus:** attaches to a file and spreads when the file is run.\n- **Worm:** **self-replicates across the network** without user action, exploiting vulnerabilities. Segmentation and patching limit it.\n- **Trojan:** disguised as legitimate software.\n- **Ransomware:** **encrypts data and demands payment** for the key. Defenses: **offline/immutable backups**, patching, segmentation, and least privilege.\n- **Spyware / keyloggers:** secretly collect activity and keystrokes.\n- **Rootkit:** hides deep in the OS to keep privileged access.\n- **Botnet agent:** turns the device into a remotely controlled bot for DDoS or spam.\n\n**Password attacks:**\n- **Brute force:** tries **many combinations** against an account until one works. Stopped by **account lockout**, long passwords, and MFA.\n- **Dictionary attack:** tries common words and leaked passwords.\n- **Password spraying:** tries **a few common passwords against many accounts**, staying under each account's lockout threshold. Stopped by **MFA**, banned-password lists, and monitoring for many failed logins across accounts.\n- **Credential stuffing:** reusing username/password pairs leaked from other sites.\n\n**Spoofing:** **IP spoofing** forges the **source IP** in packets (used in reflection attacks and to bypass IP-based rules). **MAC spoofing** changes a device's MAC to impersonate another device (used to bypass MAC filtering or MAB).\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **DoS vs DDoS:** one source vs **many** (botnet).\n- **SYN flood vs amplification:** SYN flood exhausts **half-open connections**; amplification uses **third-party servers** to send big responses to a **spoofed** victim.\n- **Switch spoofing vs double tagging:** switch spoofing **negotiates a trunk via DTP**; double tagging uses **two 802.1Q tags** and the **native VLAN**.\n- **MAC flooding vs ARP poisoning:** MAC flooding fills the **switch's CAM table** (defense: port security); ARP poisoning corrupts **hosts' ARP caches** (defense: DAI).\n- **ARP poisoning vs DNS poisoning:** ARP maps **IP to MAC** on the LAN; DNS maps **names to IPs**.\n- **Rogue AP vs evil twin:** a rogue AP is **unauthorized and plugged into your network**; an evil twin **impersonates your SSID** to lure users.\n- **Rogue DHCP vs DHCP starvation:** rogue DHCP **hands out bad settings**; starvation **exhausts the real pool**.\n- **Tailgating vs piggybacking:** tailgating is **without** the employee's knowledge; piggybacking is **with** consent.\n- **Brute force vs password spraying:** brute force = **many passwords, one account**; spraying = **few passwords, many accounts**.\n- **Phishing variants:** spear = targeted; whaling = executives; vishing = voice; smishing = SMS.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Botnet:** a network of compromised devices controlled remotely by an attacker.\n- **C2 (command and control):** servers attackers use to direct bots or malware.\n- **SYN flood:** a DoS using half-open TCP connections.\n- **Amplification attack:** small spoofed requests causing large responses to a victim.\n- **VLAN hopping:** reaching another VLAN via switch spoofing or double tagging.\n- **CAM table:** the switch's MAC address table.\n- **DAI:** Dynamic ARP Inspection.\n- **DNSSEC:** DNS Security Extensions, which sign DNS records.\n- **Rogue device:** any unauthorized device on the network.\n- **Evil twin:** a fake AP impersonating a legitimate SSID.\n- **On-path attack:** intercepting traffic between two parties (formerly man-in-the-middle).\n- **Pharming:** redirecting users from a legitimate site to a fake one.\n- **Social engineering:** manipulating people to gain information or access.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Match each attack to its defense: MAC flooding → **port security**; ARP spoofing → **DAI**; rogue DHCP → **DHCP snooping**; switch spoofing → **disable DTP**; double tagging → **change/tag the native VLAN**; rogue STP root → **BPDU/root guard**; DNS poisoning → **DNSSEC**; evil twin → **802.1X with certificate validation and WIPS**; on-path → **TLS with certificate validation**.","4|Security features & defense":"# Security features & defense\n\nThis topic covers the controls you configure to stop the attacks in the previous lesson: **hardening devices**, controlling **who and what may connect** (NAC, 802.1X, port security, MAC filtering), **filtering traffic** with ACLs and URL/content filters, organizing the network into **trusted and untrusted zones** with a **screened subnet**, and **managing keys**. Good defense is layered: no single control stops everything.\n\n## Device hardening\n\n**Hardening** means **reducing a device's attack surface**: removing anything an attacker could use and securing what's left. Apply it to every switch, router, firewall, AP, and server.\n\n- **Change default passwords and accounts.** Default credentials are published online and are the first thing attackers try. Use strong unique passwords, **central AAA** (RADIUS/TACACS+) for admin logins, and **MFA** where supported.\n- **Disable unused ports.** Shut down (administratively disable) unused switch ports and place them in an unused **parking/black-hole VLAN**, so nobody can plug in an unauthorized device.\n- **Disable unused services.** Turn off anything not needed: the HTTP server, Telnet, CDP/LLDP on edge ports facing untrusted networks, and legacy \"small servers\". Every running service is a possible vulnerability.\n- **Use secure protocols:** **SSH** instead of Telnet, **HTTPS** instead of HTTP, **SFTP/SCP** instead of FTP/TFTP, and **SNMPv3** (with authPriv for authentication and encryption) instead of SNMPv1/v2c community strings.\n- **Patch firmware** regularly and **verify firmware signatures/hashes** so only authentic, untampered vendor images run.\n- **Restrict management access:** management ACLs so only admin subnets can reach SSH/HTTPS/SNMP, or a separate out-of-band management network.\n- **Login banner:** a legal warning that unauthorized access is prohibited and activity may be monitored.\n- **Authenticate routing protocols** (e.g. OSPF with SHA) so rogue neighbors can't inject false routes.\n- **Log** to a central syslog/SIEM with NTP time, and keep **IPS signatures** updated so new threats are detected.\n\n> Scenario: an auditor finds that a branch switch still accepts Telnet logins with the vendor's default \"admin\" password. Two hardening failures: insecure protocol and default credentials.\n\n## Network access control (NAC)\n\n**NAC** checks **who and what** is connecting **before** granting network access, then applies policy. It's how you stop rogue devices and unhealthy machines at the door.\n\n**802.1X** is the standard for port-based authentication, on both wired and wireless networks. Three roles:\n- **Supplicant:** the client device requesting access.\n- **Authenticator:** the switch or AP, which blocks the port until authentication succeeds.\n- **Authentication server:** usually **RADIUS**, which checks credentials or certificates.\n\nUsing **RADIUS attributes**, the server can tell the switch to place each user in a **dynamic VLAN** or apply an ACL based on role (e.g. staff VLAN vs contractor VLAN).\n\n**Posture assessment** checks device health: antivirus running and current, OS patched, firewall enabled, disk encrypted. A device that **fails** is placed in a **quarantine/remediation VLAN** where it can only reach update servers until it's fixed.\n\n**Agent-based vs agentless:**\n- **Agent-based (persistent or dissolvable):** software on the device reports detailed posture. Best for managed corporate PCs.\n- **Agentless:** identifies devices using **network scans, DHCP fingerprinting**, and other passive data. Suits IoT, printers, and guest devices that can't run an agent.\n\n**MAC authentication bypass (MAB):** for devices that **can't do 802.1X** (printers, cameras, some phones), the switch sends the device's **MAC address** to RADIUS as its identity. Convenient, but weaker, because MACs can be **spoofed**.\n\n## Port security and MAC filtering\n\n**Port security** is a switch feature that controls **which and how many MAC addresses** may use an access port.\n- **Limit MACs per port** (e.g. 1 or 2 for a PC plus an IP phone). This defeats **MAC flooding**.\n- **Sticky learning:** the switch learns the first MAC(s) it sees and saves them as allowed.\n- **Violation modes:** **shutdown** (port goes **err-disabled**, the default and most secure), **restrict** (drop and log), **protect** (drop silently).\n\n**MAC filtering** allows or blocks devices by MAC address, on switches or wireless APs. It's easy to set up but **weak on its own**, because MAC addresses are visible on the network and **easily spoofed**. Treat it as one layer, never the only control.\n\n**Other switch-level protections** that block Layer 2 attacks:\n- **DHCP snooping:** only **trusted ports** (those facing legitimate DHCP servers) may send DHCP offers and acks, which stops rogue DHCP. It also builds a **binding table** of IP, MAC, port, and VLAN.\n- **Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI):** checks ARP packets against the DHCP snooping binding table, which stops ARP spoofing.\n- **BPDU guard and root guard:** protect spanning tree from rogue switches.\n- **Storm control:** limits broadcast/multicast floods.\n\n## Access control lists (ACLs)\n\nAn **ACL** is an **ordered list of permit and deny rules** applied to an interface (inbound or outbound) that filters traffic by fields such as source/destination IP, protocol, and port.\n\nHow it's processed:\n1. Rules are checked **top-down**.\n2. The **first matching rule wins** and processing stops.\n3. If nothing matches, the **implicit deny** at the end **blocks** the traffic, even though you can't see it.\n\n**Order matters:** put **specific rules before general ones**. If \"deny 10.1.1.0/24\" sits above \"permit host 10.1.1.50\", the host is denied because the broader rule matches first.\n\n> Scenario: an ACL contains only \"deny host 10.1.1.9\". Now nothing passes at all. Why? The implicit deny blocks everything not permitted; you need a permit rule too.\n\n**Standard vs extended:**\n\n| Type | Matches on | Placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Standard ACL** | **Source IP only** | **Near the destination**, so it doesn't block the source from reaching other places |\n| **Extended ACL** | Source and destination IP, protocol, and port | **Near the source**, so unwanted traffic is dropped before it crosses the network |\n\n**Wildcard masks** (Cisco) are inverted subnet masks: **0.0.0.0** matches one exact host; **0.0.0.255** matches a /24.\n\n**Firewall rules** follow the same logic. The most secure approach for inbound internet traffic is **default deny**, explicitly allowing only required services. Review rules periodically and remove unused or overly broad ones so risk doesn't build up.\n\n## URL and content filtering\n\n**URL filtering** blocks or allows websites by **category** (e.g. gambling, malware), **reputation score**, or **specific URL**. It's usually done by a proxy, next-generation firewall, or DNS filtering service.\n\n**Content filtering** goes further and **inspects the content** of web traffic, email, and files to block **malware, prohibited material, or sensitive data leaving** the network.\n- **Data loss prevention (DLP)** is content filtering focused on **outbound** data: it detects things like **credit card or national ID numbers** and blocks or alerts.\n- Inspecting HTTPS content requires **TLS inspection** (decrypt, inspect, re-encrypt) with a trusted certificate on clients.\n\nRelated filtering:\n- **Web application firewall (WAF):** protects **web applications** from application-layer attacks such as **SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS)**.\n- **Egress filtering:** controls **outbound** traffic to block malware callbacks to C2 servers, data exfiltration, and packets with spoofed source addresses leaving your network.\n\n## Trusted zones, untrusted zones, and the screened subnet\n\nFirewalls divide the network into **zones** with different trust levels:\n- **Trusted zone:** the **internal network**, with a higher level of trust and protection.\n- **Untrusted zone:** the **internet** and anything you don't control.\n- Traffic from untrusted to trusted is **denied by default**; only specific, required traffic is allowed. Outbound from trusted is usually allowed more freely (but still filtered).\n\n**Screened subnet** (formerly called the **DMZ**, demilitarized zone): a separate zone for **public-facing servers** such as **web servers, mail relays, public DNS, and reverse proxies**. Internet users can reach these servers, but the servers can only reach the internal network in tightly limited ways. If a public server is compromised, the attacker is still outside the trusted zone.\n\nCommon designs:\n- **Three-legged firewall:** one firewall with **three interfaces/zones**: internet (untrusted), screened subnet, and internal (trusted).\n- **Dual firewall:** an outer firewall between the internet and the screened subnet, and an inner firewall between the screened subnet and the internal network, often from different vendors.\n\n> Scenario: the company web server is on the internal LAN with a port forward from the internet. If it's hacked, the attacker is inside. Move it to the screened subnet.\n\n## Key management\n\nEncryption is only as strong as the protection of its **keys**. **Key management** covers the whole lifecycle:\n- **Generate** strong keys with good randomness.\n- **Store** them securely: a **hardware security module (HSM)**, TPM, or a secrets **vault**, never in plain text in scripts or configs.\n- **Distribute** them securely to the systems that need them.\n- **Rotate** keys and certificates periodically, which **limits the damage if a key is compromised** and how much data one key protects.\n- **Revoke** compromised keys and certificates (CRL or OCSP for certificates).\n- **Destroy** retired keys securely.\n- **Track expiry and automate renewal**, because an expired certificate causes outages (VPNs, websites, and 802.1X fail).\n\nNetwork examples: SSH host keys, TLS certificates on web servers and VPN gateways, 802.1X server certificates, Wi-Fi pre-shared keys, and SNMPv3/routing authentication keys.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **NAC vs port security:** NAC checks **identity and health** (802.1X, posture) and assigns policy; port security just limits **which/how many MACs** use a port.\n- **802.1X vs MAB:** 802.1X authenticates with **credentials or certificates**; MAB uses only the **MAC address** for devices that can't do 802.1X.\n- **MAC filtering vs real authentication:** MAC filtering is **easily spoofed**; don't rely on it alone.\n- **Standard vs extended ACL placement:** standard (source only) → **near the destination**; extended → **near the source**.\n- **Explicit vs implicit deny:** explicit is a rule you write; **implicit deny** is the invisible final rule blocking everything else.\n- **URL filtering vs content filtering:** URL filtering decides by **site/category**; content filtering **inspects what's inside** traffic and files.\n- **WAF vs network firewall:** a WAF understands **HTTP and web app attacks**; a network firewall filters by IP, port, and protocol (or app identity, for NGFWs).\n- **Ingress vs egress filtering:** ingress controls traffic **coming in**; egress controls traffic **going out**.\n- **Screened subnet vs internal network:** public servers belong in the **screened subnet**, never on the trusted LAN.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Hardening:** reducing a device's attack surface.\n- **Attack surface:** all the ways a system can be attacked.\n- **NAC:** network access control.\n- **802.1X:** port-based network access control using supplicant, authenticator, and authentication server.\n- **Posture assessment:** checking device health before granting access.\n- **Quarantine VLAN:** a restricted VLAN for non-compliant devices.\n- **MAB:** MAC authentication bypass.\n- **Port security:** limiting MAC addresses on a switch port.\n- **Implicit deny:** the hidden final rule that blocks unmatched traffic.\n- **DLP:** data loss prevention.\n- **WAF:** web application firewall.\n- **Screened subnet:** a zone for public-facing servers (formerly DMZ).\n- **HSM:** hardware security module for storing keys.\n- **Key rotation:** replacing keys periodically.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Check device health before granting access\" → **NAC posture assessment**; failures go to a **quarantine VLAN**. \"Printer can't do 802.1X\" → **MAB**. \"Too many MACs on a port\" → **port security**. \"Traffic not matched by any rule\" → **implicit deny**. \"Public web server location\" → **screened subnet**. \"Stop card numbers leaving\" → **DLP**.","5|Troubleshooting methodology":"# Troubleshooting methodology\n\nCompTIA's troubleshooting methodology is a **7-step process** that keeps you from guessing, making things worse, or \"fixing\" the wrong thing. The exam loves \"what should the technician do **next**?\" questions, so you need to know the **order** of the steps and **what belongs in each one**.\n\n## Why follow a methodology\n\nWithout a method, technicians tend to jump straight to a fix (\"just replace the switch\"), change several things at once, and never write down what happened. That wastes money, causes new outages, and means the same problem is solved from scratch next time.\n\nThe 7 steps, in order:\n\n1. **Identify the problem**\n2. **Establish a theory of probable cause**\n3. **Test the theory to determine the cause**\n4. **Establish a plan of action to resolve the problem and identify potential effects**\n5. **Implement the solution or escalate as necessary**\n6. **Verify full system functionality and, if applicable, implement preventive measures**\n7. **Document findings, actions, outcomes, and lessons learned**\n\n> **Memory tip:** **I**dentify, **E**stablish, **T**est, **P**lan, **I**mplement, **V**erify, **D**ocument. Try \"**I** **E**at **T**acos, **P**lease **I**nvite **V**ery **D**elightful friends.\"\n\nNotice the shape: steps 1–3 find the **cause**, steps 4–5 **fix** it safely, and steps 6–7 **confirm and record** the result. Documentation is **always last**, and you never implement a fix before you've identified the problem and tested a theory.\n\n## Step 1: Identify the problem\n\nYou can't fix what you don't understand. This step is all about **gathering information** before forming any opinions.\n\n- **Gather information:** read the ticket, check **logs**, **monitoring dashboards**, and alerts. Logs and monitoring let you learn a lot **without bothering users**.\n- **Question users:** ask open questions. \"What exactly fails? Since when? Does it happen all the time? Can you show me the error?\"\n- **Identify symptoms:** \"the internet is down\" might really mean one website fails, or only email fails. Pin down the actual symptom.\n- **Determine if anything has changed:** recent **patches, configuration changes, moved equipment, new devices**, or a user's own actions. Check **change logs**. Recent changes are one of the most common causes of new problems.\n- **Duplicate the problem, if possible:** reproducing it **confirms the symptoms** and gives you a way to **prove a fix works** later.\n- **Approach multiple problems individually:** if you find three unrelated issues, treat them as **three separate problems**. Mixing them makes it impossible to know which fix solved what.\n\n**Determine the scope:** ask whether **other users** are affected.\n- **One user** → likely a local cause (their PC, cable, port, settings).\n- **Many users or a whole site** → likely shared infrastructure (a switch, DHCP, DNS, WAN link, firewall).\n\nScenario: a user says \"the internet is down.\" The best first move is to ask which sites or applications fail, since when, and whether anything changed, not to reboot the router.\n\n## Step 2: Establish a theory of probable cause\n\nNow you form a hypothesis: \"I think the cause is X.\"\n\n- **Question the obvious:** check the simple things first: **power, cables, link lights**, whether the device is on, whether the user is on the right Wi-Fi. Many \"complex\" problems are an unplugged cable.\n- **Consider multiple approaches:** work through the OSI model **top-to-bottom** or **bottom-to-top**, or use **divide and conquer** (see Troubleshooting approaches below).\n- **Use references:** **vendor documentation**, **knowledge bases**, and **logs** help with unusual error messages.\n\nA theory is still only a guess. You haven't changed anything yet; you've decided **what to test**.\n\n## Step 3: Test the theory to determine the cause\n\nYou test the theory to prove or disprove it, for example by swapping a suspected bad patch cable, pinging the gateway, or checking the port's VLAN.\n\n- **If the theory is confirmed:** determine the **next steps to resolve the problem** (which leads to step 4, the plan).\n- **If the theory is not confirmed:** **establish a new theory or escalate**. These are the only two options. Don't keep guessing randomly.\n\n**Change one thing at a time** while testing. If you change the cable, the port, and the VLAN together and it starts working, you don't know which one was the cause.\n\nScenario: a technician suspects a bad patch cable, swaps it, and the problem goes away. That swap was **testing the theory** (step 3). It also happens to resolve the issue, but you still continue through verification and documentation.\n\n## Steps 4 and 5: Plan, then implement or escalate\n\n**Step 4: Establish a plan of action to resolve the problem and identify potential effects.**\nBefore touching production, think about what your fix could break.\n- Follow **change management** (get approval for changes that affect others).\n- Schedule a **maintenance window** if the fix causes downtime, and **notify users** of planned downtime.\n- Prepare a **rollback plan** in case the fix fails.\n- Identify **potential effects**: will rebooting this switch drop the whole floor? Will the firmware upgrade reset settings?\n\n**Step 5: Implement the solution or escalate as necessary.**\n- Carry out the plan.\n- **Escalate** when the fix is beyond your **knowledge, authority, or scope**. Example: the fix needs a firmware upgrade on a core switch you don't have permission to change, so you escalate to the network engineering team (or the vendor, or the ISP).\n\nEscalating is not failure. It's the correct step when you can't or shouldn't make the change yourself.\n\n## Steps 6 and 7: Verify, then document\n\n**Step 6: Verify full system functionality and, if applicable, implement preventive measures.**\n- **Verify full functionality**, not just the original symptom. Check the **user's real workflow** (email, file shares, the business app), because the fix might have solved one part and broken another.\n- Confirm the fix **didn't break anything else**.\n- **Preventive measures** stop the problem recurring: add **monitoring** or alerts, add **redundancy**, keep **spare parts** (e.g. spare SFPs), apply patches, or **train users**.\n\n**Step 7: Document findings, actions, outcomes, and lessons learned.**\n- Record the **symptoms, root cause, steps taken, the fix, and lessons learned**.\n- Update the **ticket**, the **knowledge base**, and any affected network documentation (e.g. a changed VLAN assignment).\n- Good documentation lets the next technician solve the same problem in minutes.\n\nScenario: a technician changes a user's port VLAN and the user can now connect. Before closing the ticket, they should **verify full functionality** and **document the change and outcome**.\n\n## Troubleshooting approaches\n\nThese are ways to **form and test theories** (step 2 and 3).\n\n| Approach | How it works | When to use it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Bottom-to-top** (Layer 1 → 7) | Start with cables and link lights, then addressing, then applications | Total loss of connectivity; a physical cause is likely |\n| **Top-to-bottom** (Layer 7 → 1) | Start at the **Application layer** and work down | One application fails but the network otherwise seems fine |\n| **Divide and conquer** | Start in the **middle** (usually Layer 3, e.g. ping) and move **up or down** based on the result | You have no strong hunch; it halves the search quickly |\n| **Follow the path** | Trace traffic **hop by hop** from source to destination | Multi-hop problems, e.g. one remote site unreachable |\n| **Spot the difference** (comparison) | Compare a **working** system with a **failing** one | One PC fails while an identical one works |\n\nExample of divide and conquer: a ping to the server succeeds, so Layers 1–3 work; you move **up** and check the port, service, or application. If the ping fails, you move **down** and check addressing, the link, and cabling.\n\n## Good habits and common mistakes\n\n**Good habits:**\n- **Change one thing at a time** so you know what fixed (or broke) it.\n- **Ask \"what changed?\"** early: change logs, updates, moves, new devices.\n- **Determine scope** first: one user versus many changes your whole theory.\n- **Intermittent problems:** if you can't reproduce it, **gather data over time** with logs and monitoring, and ask users to **note exact times** it happens.\n- Keep the user informed and set expectations.\n\n**Common mistakes the exam describes:**\n- **Skipping steps:** a user reports slow file transfers and the technician immediately replaces the switch. They skipped **identifying the problem** and **testing a theory**.\n- **Implementing without a plan:** a disruptive change during business hours with no rollback plan.\n- **Closing the ticket at \"it works now\"** without verifying full functionality or documenting.\n- **Fixing several problems with one big change**, instead of approaching them individually.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Test the theory vs implement the solution:** testing **proves the cause** (step 3); implementing is the **planned fix** (step 5). A quick swap to confirm a suspicion is a test.\n- **Establish a plan vs implement:** the **rollback plan, change approval, maintenance window, and impact analysis** belong to **step 4**, before implementation.\n- **Theory not confirmed vs theory confirmed:** not confirmed → **new theory or escalate**; confirmed → **plan of action**.\n- **Verify vs document:** verify (step 6) = **check it works and prevent recurrence**; document (step 7) = **write it all down**. Document is always last.\n- **Top-to-bottom vs bottom-to-top:** top-to-bottom **starts at the Application layer**; bottom-to-top **starts at the Physical layer** (cables).\n- **Divide and conquer vs follow the path:** divide and conquer **starts in the middle of the OSI model**; follow the path **traces the route through devices**.\n- **Escalate vs give up:** escalation is a **legitimate step** when the problem exceeds your knowledge, authority, or scope.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Probable cause:** your best theory of what's causing the problem.\n- **Question the obvious:** check simple causes (power, cables, link lights) first.\n- **Scope:** how many users, devices, or sites are affected.\n- **Duplicate (reproduce) the problem:** make it happen again to confirm symptoms and later test the fix.\n- **Escalation:** handing the problem to someone with more knowledge, authority, or access.\n- **Rollback plan:** how to undo a change if it fails.\n- **Preventive measures:** actions that stop the problem recurring, such as monitoring or redundancy.\n- **Divide and conquer:** start in the middle of the OSI model and move up or down.\n- **Spot the difference:** compare a working system with a failing one.\n- **Lessons learned:** what to do differently next time, recorded in documentation.\n\n> **Exam tip:** After a theory is **confirmed** → **establish a plan of action**. Theory **not confirmed** → **new theory or escalate**. After implementing → **verify full functionality** (and add preventive measures). The final step is always **document**.","5|Cabling & physical interface issues":"# Cabling & physical interface issues\n\nMost network problems start at **Layer 1**: the wrong cable, a bad termination, a dirty fiber, an overloaded PoE switch, or a mismatched optic. This lesson covers the cable, interface, and hardware problems N10-009 tests, the symptoms each one produces, and how to read **interface counters** and **port status** to find them.\n\n## Incorrect cable type\n\nEvery cable has limits on **speed, distance, and environment**. Using the wrong one gives errors, slow links, or no link at all.\n\n**Copper categories (twisted pair, 100 m max channel unless noted):**\n\n| Category | Typical max speed | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cat 5** | 100 Mbps | Obsolete; not for gigabit |\n| **Cat 5e** | 1 Gbps at 100 m | Common desktop cabling; **not rated for 10GBASE-T** |\n| **Cat 6** | 1 Gbps at 100 m; **10 Gbps up to about 55 m** | Better crosstalk performance |\n| **Cat 6a** | **10 Gbps at 100 m** | The usual choice for 10GBASE-T |\n| **Cat 7** | 10 Gbps at 100 m | Fully shielded; not a TIA standard |\n| **Cat 8** | **25/40 Gbps up to 30 m** | Data center, short switch-to-server runs |\n\nScenario: an 80 m 10GBASE-T run built with Cat 5e shows errors → **incorrect cable type**; it needs Cat 6a.\n\n**Fiber: single-mode vs multimode:**\n- **Multimode (MMF):** wide core, **short distances** (hundreds of meters), cheaper **SR** optics (850 nm).\n- **Single-mode (SMF):** narrow core, **long distances** (kilometers), **LR/ER** optics (1310/1550 nm).\n- **Mixing single-mode and multimode** in one link causes huge loss, and the link usually fails. Fiber type and optic type must match.\n\n**Shielding: STP vs UTP:**\n- **UTP** is fine in offices. Near **motors, elevators, heavy machinery, or fluorescent lighting**, use **STP** (properly grounded) or fiber to resist **EMI**.\n\n**Other wrong-cable problems:**\n- **Jacket rating:** plenum-rated cable used **outdoors** isn't built for UV, moisture, or burial and degrades. Outdoor runs need outdoor or direct-burial cable.\n- **Rollover (console) cable** used as a patch cable → no Ethernet link, because the pinout is fully reversed.\n- **Crossover vs straight-through:** on modern switches **Auto-MDIX** fixes this automatically. On old devices without Auto-MDIX, the wrong one gives no link.\n\n## Signal degradation\n\nSignal degradation means the signal arrives **too weak or too noisy** to be read reliably. Symptoms are **CRC/FCS errors, retransmissions, slow speeds**, and intermittent drops.\n\n| Problem | What it is | Common causes | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Attenuation** | Signal **loses strength over distance** | Copper run **over 100 m**, poor-quality or damaged cable, too many connectors, long tight coils | Shorten the run, add a switch, use fiber |\n| **Crosstalk** | Signal from one pair **bleeds into another** | **Untwisting too much** (more than about 13 mm / half an inch) at terminations, split pairs, low-grade cable | Re-terminate properly; use a higher category |\n| **Interference (EMI/RFI)** | Outside electrical noise corrupts the signal | Motors, elevators, fluorescent lights, power cables running alongside data | Reroute away from the source; use STP or fiber |\n\n**Crosstalk types:** **NEXT** (near-end crosstalk) is measured at the transmitting end; **FEXT** is at the far end. Certifiers test both.\n\n**Return loss:** signal **reflected back** toward the sender by **impedance changes**, often from mixing cable types or bad connectors.\n\nScenario: FCS errors spike **only when the elevator motor runs** → **EMI**. Reroute the cable away from the motor, or use shielded cable or fiber.\n\n## Termination and wiring faults\n\n**Improper termination** is one of the most common Layer 1 problems: a bad crimp, a loose punchdown, or pairs on the wrong pins.\n\n| Fault | What happened | Symptom |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Open** | A conductor has **no continuity** end to end (broken wire, bad crimp) | No link, or link at lower speed |\n| **Short** | Two conductors **touch** when they shouldn't | No link or errors |\n| **Reversed pair** | The two wires **within one pair** are swapped (e.g. pins 1 and 2) | Wire map fault |\n| **Crossed pair** | A **whole pair** is on the wrong pins at one end (e.g. 1/2 swapped with 3/6) | Accidental crossover |\n| **Split pair** | Pins are right, but wires from **two different pairs** are twisted together | **Passes continuity**, but **severe crosstalk** |\n| **Loose punchdown** | A conductor isn't seated in the patch panel or jack | **Intermittent** connectivity |\n\n**Mixed standards:** T568A on one end and T568B on the other makes a **crossover** cable. Fine with Auto-MDIX; no link on an old device that needs straight-through.\n\n**Bad pair, fallback speed:** gigabit uses **all four pairs**, 100 Mbps uses two. A link that negotiates only **100 Mbps instead of 1 Gbps** usually has **one damaged or badly terminated pair**.\n\n**Fiber faults:**\n- **TX/RX transposed (reversed polarity):** a new fiber link **stays down** even though both optics are good. **Swap the two connectors** (e.g. the LC pair) at one end.\n- **Dirty or damaged connectors:** light is present but **RX power is low**. **Inspect with a fiber scope**, then clean.\n- **Bend radius exceeded:** tight bends cause **macrobend loss** or break the fiber.\n- **Polish mismatch:** connecting **UPC to APC** causes high insertion loss and back reflection.\n\n**Testing tools for cabling:**\n\n| Tool | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| **Cable tester (wire map)** | Continuity and pinout: opens, shorts, reversed and crossed pairs |\n| **Cable certifier** | Whether the run **meets a category** (e.g. Cat 6a): NEXT, return loss, length. **Catches split pairs** |\n| **TDR** | **Distance** to an open, short, or impedance change in copper |\n| **OTDR** | Distance to breaks, bends, splices, and bad connectors in **fiber** |\n| **Fiber inspection scope** | Dirt or damage on a connector end face |\n| **Optical power meter / light meter** | How much light arrives, to compare against the loss budget |\n\nA cable that **passes continuity** but **fails certification** almost always has **poor termination** (too much untwisting, cheap connectors).\n\n## Interface counters\n\nInterface counters tell you **what kind** of problem a link has. What matters is counters that are **increasing**, not old totals (clear them and watch).\n\n| Counter | What it means | Usual cause |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CRC / FCS errors** | Frames arrived **corrupted** (checksum failed) | **Layer 1**: bad cable, EMI, dirty fiber, or **duplex mismatch** |\n| **Runts** | Frames **smaller than 64 bytes** | Collisions, duplex mismatch, faulty NIC |\n| **Giants** | Frames **larger than the max size** (1518 bytes, or the MTU) | **MTU / jumbo frame mismatch** |\n| **Input errors** | Total of receive errors | CRC, runts, giants, alignment errors, **overruns** |\n| **Output drops / discards** | Egress queue full, packets thrown away | **Congestion** |\n| **Input queue drops** | Device couldn't process incoming packets fast enough | **High CPU** or traffic bursts |\n| **Late collisions** | Collisions after the first 64 bytes | **Duplex mismatch** or a cable **too long** |\n| **Broadcast/multicast counters** spiking | Huge volumes of broadcasts | A **switching loop** / broadcast storm |\n\n**Duplex mismatch:** one side is full duplex and the other half duplex (often one side hard-coded, the other auto). The **half-duplex side** logs **late collisions**; the **full-duplex side** logs **CRC errors and runts**. Throughput collapses but the link stays up. Fix: set **both ends the same**, ideally **both auto-negotiate**. CDP/LLDP may log \"duplex mismatch discovered.\"\n\n**Speed mismatch:** if speeds don't match, there's usually **no link at all**. A 10G link that only comes up at 1G often has a **1G SFP** in one end or a port **hard-set to 1G**.\n\nScenario: a storage port's **giants** counter climbs → **jumbo frames** arriving on a port without a larger MTU.\n\n## Port status\n\nPort status shows **where** the problem sits.\n\n| Status | Meaning | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Up/up** | Layer 1 and Layer 2 working | Look elsewhere (Layer 3+) |\n| **Down/down** | **Physical** problem: unplugged, bad cable, far end powered off | Check cable, jack patching, the far device |\n| **Up/down** (line protocol down) | Layer 1 OK but **Layer 2 problem**: encapsulation or keepalive mismatch | Check Layer 2 settings on both ends |\n| **Administratively down** | Someone **shut it down** with `shutdown` | Confirm it should be active, then `no shutdown` |\n| **Err-disabled** | The switch **automatically disabled** it after a violation | Fix the cause, then `shut` / `no shut` (or errdisable recovery) |\n| **Suspended** | A **link aggregation (LACP/EtherChannel) member** whose settings don't match the bundle, or the far end isn't negotiating | Match speed, duplex, VLANs, and LACP settings on all members |\n\n**Common err-disabled triggers:**\n- **Port security violation** (`psecure-violation`): an unknown MAC, or too many MACs, e.g. someone plugged in a hub or small switch.\n- **BPDU guard:** a switch was plugged into a PortFast edge port.\n- **Link flapping:** the link went up and down too many times (bad cable, optic, or NIC).\n\n**Link flapping** itself (up/down/up/down) points to a faulty cable, transceiver, or NIC, or a speed/duplex negotiation problem.\n\n**UDLD (Unidirectional Link Detection):** on fiber, one strand can fail so one side sees the link up and the other doesn't. UDLD detects this and disables the port, preventing loops.\n\nScenario: a user moves offices and the new desk jack shows no link → check that the jack is **patched to an active switch port**, and that the port isn't administratively down.\n\n## Power over Ethernet issues\n\n**PoE** sends power over the data cable to APs, phones, and cameras. Two things go wrong: the port can't supply **enough power for the device**, or the switch runs out of **total power**.\n\n| Standard | Name | Max per port (at the switch) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **802.3af** | PoE | **15.4 W** |\n| **802.3at** | PoE+ | **30 W** |\n| **802.3bt** Type 3 | PoE++ | **60 W** |\n| **802.3bt** Type 4 | PoE++ | **90 W** (100 W in some specs) |\n\n**Incorrect standard:** the device needs more than the port's class provides, e.g. a **PoE+ AP on an 802.3af port**. Symptoms: the device **won't power on**, **keeps rebooting**, or runs with features disabled (fewer radios).\n\n**Power budget exceeded:** each switch has a **total PoE budget**. Example: a 370 W switch with ten 30 W APs uses 300 W. Adding five more needs 450 W total, so **some ports get no power**. Symptom: **new** devices won't power on after more were added, while the old ones keep working.\n\n**Other PoE problems:**\n- A **faulty powered device** drawing more than its class allows → the switch repeatedly negotiates power, then shuts it off.\n- An IP phone works but the **PC behind its passthrough port** has no link → check the phone's PC port and the cable between phone and PC.\n\n**Check it:** `show power inline` shows per-port draw and the remaining budget. Fixes: move devices to another switch, add a **PoE injector**, upgrade to a higher-budget switch or power supply, or set port priorities.\n\n## Transceiver and signal strength issues\n\n**Transceiver mismatch:** both ends of a link must use **compatible optics**.\n- **Wavelength/fiber type:** **SR on one end and LR on the other**, or **1000BASE-SX vs 1000BASE-LX**, won't work reliably; they use different wavelengths and fiber types.\n- **Speed:** a **1G SFP** in one end of a 10G SFP+ link → link at 1G or no link.\n- **Unsupported module:** the switch logs \"**unsupported transceiver**\" and keeps the port down; some vendors reject third-party optics.\n- **DAC cables:** a direct attach copper cable may not work after a switch replacement if it's not supported, the wrong speed, or too long.\n\n**Optical signal strength (DOM):** **Digital optical monitoring** reports TX/RX power and temperature (e.g. `show interfaces transceiver`). Compare **RX power** with the receiver's sensitivity range.\n- **Too weak** (e.g. RX −30 dBm when the sensitivity is −14 dBm): a **break**, **dirty connector**, excessive bends, or the **wrong fiber type**.\n- **Slowly falling** over weeks with rising errors: a **failing optic** or gradually **contaminated or damaged fiber**.\n- **Too strong:** long-reach optics on a **very short** run **saturate the receiver**. Add an **optical attenuator**.\n- **Loss budget:** if measured loss is higher than calculated, inspect and clean connectors, check splices and bends, and test with an **OTDR**.\n\n**Wireless signal strength:**\n- Measure **RSSI** and **SNR** at the client's location. Weaker than about **−70 dBm** RSSI, or SNR under about **25 dB**, gives low data rates, retries, and disconnects at the edge of coverage.\n- Hardware causes: **detached, damaged, or wrong antennas**, or wrong antenna **orientation**, can give poor signal even near the AP.\n- Fix by adjusting AP **placement, power, or antennas**.\n\nScenario: after a storm, several ports in a building show errors and one is dead → possible **power surge or lightning damage** to ports or cabling.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Attenuation vs crosstalk:** attenuation = signal **gets weaker with distance**; crosstalk = signal **leaks between pairs** (usually from untwisting).\n- **Crosstalk vs EMI:** crosstalk comes from **inside the cable**; EMI comes from **outside sources** like motors and lights.\n- **Reversed pair vs crossed pair vs split pair:** reversed = two wires swapped **within one pair**; crossed = **whole pairs** on the wrong pins; split = pins right but **twists mixed**, which only a **certifier** catches.\n- **Cable tester vs certifier:** a tester checks **continuity and pinout**; a certifier proves the run **meets a category's performance**.\n- **TDR vs OTDR:** TDR is for **copper**; OTDR is for **fiber**.\n- **Runts vs giants:** runts are **too small** (under 64 bytes, collisions); giants are **too big** (MTU mismatch).\n- **CRC errors vs output drops:** CRC = **corruption** (Layer 1); output drops = **congestion** (queues full).\n- **Down/down vs administratively down vs err-disabled:** down/down = **physical fault**; admin down = **someone shut it**; err-disabled = **the switch shut it** after a violation.\n- **PoE budget exceeded vs incorrect standard:** budget = the **whole switch** is out of power; incorrect standard = **one port's class** is too low for the device.\n- **RX too weak vs too strong:** weak = dirt, breaks, wrong fiber; strong = **receiver saturation**, add an attenuator.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Attenuation:** loss of signal strength over distance.\n- **Crosstalk (NEXT/FEXT):** signal bleeding between pairs, measured at the near or far end.\n- **EMI:** electromagnetic interference from outside sources.\n- **Split pair:** a wiring fault that passes continuity but causes heavy crosstalk.\n- **TX/RX transposed:** fiber strands swapped so transmit doesn't meet receive.\n- **CRC/FCS error:** a frame whose checksum failed, meaning it was corrupted.\n- **Runt / giant:** a frame below 64 bytes / above the maximum size.\n- **Err-disabled:** a port automatically shut down by the switch after an error or violation.\n- **Suspended:** an aggregation member port held out of the bundle because of a mismatch.\n- **PoE budget:** total power a switch can supply to all PoE ports.\n- **DOM:** digital optical monitoring of transceiver power and temperature.\n- **Auto-MDIX:** automatic correction for straight-through vs crossover cabling.\n- **UDLD:** detects a fiber link that works in only one direction.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"CRC errors rising\" → Layer 1 (cable, EMI, dirty fiber) or duplex. \"Late collisions\" → duplex mismatch. \"Giants\" → MTU mismatch. \"Output drops\" → congestion. \"New fiber link down, optics good\" → TX/RX transposed. \"New PoE devices won't power on\" → PoE budget exceeded. \"Port err-disabled after a hub was plugged in\" → port security or BPDU guard.","5|Network services issues":"# Network services issues\n\nWhen the cabling is fine but traffic still doesn't get where it should, the cause is usually in **switching, routing, or addressing**: a spanning tree problem, a port in the wrong VLAN, an ACL, a bad route, an exhausted DHCP pool, or a host with the wrong gateway, IP, or mask. This lesson teaches how each one behaves so you can recognize it from the symptoms.\n\n## Spanning tree problems\n\n**Spanning Tree Protocol (STP, 802.1D)** and **Rapid STP (RSTP, 802.1w)** stop Layer 2 loops when switches have redundant links. They do it by electing a **root bridge** and **blocking** some ports.\n\n**Network loops:** without STP (or with it disabled), two cables between switches create a loop. Ethernet frames have **no TTL**, so broadcasts circle forever.\n- Symptoms: a **broadcast storm**, links at **100% utilization**, **MAC address flapping** (the same MAC seen on different ports), a pegged switch CPU, and the whole segment grinding to a halt.\n- Logs showing \"MAC flapping\" when a particular port is connected → the device on that port is creating a loop or bridging two segments.\n\n**Root bridge selection:** the switch with the **lowest bridge ID** becomes root. Bridge ID = **priority** (default **32768**) + **MAC address**; on a tie, the **lowest MAC** wins, which is often the **oldest** switch.\n- Problem: a **new or old access switch becomes root** (e.g. someone adds a switch with a lower priority). Traffic then takes **suboptimal paths** through it, causing congestion, and a topology change briefly disrupts the network.\n- Fix and prevention: **deliberately set the priority** on the core/distribution switch (e.g. 4096), and enable **root guard** on ports facing access switches so they can't become root.\n\n**Port roles:**\n\n| Role | Meaning | Forwards data? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Root port** | The **best path toward the root bridge**; one per non-root switch | Yes |\n| **Designated port** | The forwarding port **for each segment**; all ports on the root bridge are designated | Yes |\n| **Alternate / backup port** | A redundant path kept in reserve | **No (blocking/discarding)** |\n\n**Port states:**\n\n| 802.1D state | What happens | RSTP equivalent |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Blocking** | Receives BPDUs only; **no data passes** | Discarding |\n| **Listening** | Building the topology; no data | Discarding |\n| **Learning** | Learns MAC addresses; no data yet | Learning |\n| **Forwarding** | Normal operation | Forwarding |\n| **Disabled** | Administratively down | Discarding |\n\n**Slow startup:** classic STP takes **30–50 seconds** to move a new port to forwarding. PCs booting after a power outage may get **DHCP timeouts** or APIPA. Fix: **PortFast** (with **BPDU guard**) on end-user access ports, or run **RSTP**.\n\nA user's port stuck in **blocking** passes **no data** at all.\n\n## Incorrect VLAN assignment\n\n**Port in the wrong VLAN:** the user lands in the wrong subnet. They get a DHCP address from the **wrong range** and can't reach the resources they need (or can reach things they shouldn't). After fixing the port's VLAN, **renew the DHCP lease** on the client.\n\n**VLAN missing or not allowed on a trunk:** a new VLAN was created on the core, but hosts on an access switch in that VLAN can only talk to each other. Check that the VLAN **exists on every switch** and is in the trunk's **allowed VLAN list**.\n\n**Native VLAN mismatch:** the untagged VLAN differs on each end of a trunk. Traffic **leaks between VLANs**, and the switches log **native VLAN mismatch** errors (via CDP).\n\n**Other VLAN-related problems:**\n- **Voice VLAN:** a VoIP phone gets an address from the **data** VLAN → the voice VLAN isn't configured on the port or isn't advertised via **CDP/LLDP**.\n- **SVI down/down:** a Layer 3 switch's VLAN interface stays down when **no active ports** are in that VLAN, or the VLAN doesn't exist.\n- **Router-on-a-stick:** VLAN 10 routes but VLAN 20 doesn't → check the VLAN 20 **subinterface's encapsulation tag and IP**, and that VLAN 20 is **allowed on the trunk**.\n- **802.1X:** a laptop lands in the **guest VLAN** → authentication **failed or timed out**, so the switch used the fallback VLAN.\n\n## ACL problems\n\n**Access control lists** filter traffic on routers, Layer 3 switches, and firewalls. They're read **top to bottom**, and the **first match wins**.\n\n- **Implicit deny:** every ACL ends with an invisible **deny all**. A new ACL that only has deny lines, or that forgets to permit needed traffic, **blocks everything**. Symptom: all traffic through the interface stops right after the ACL is applied.\n- **Rule order:** a broad rule above a specific one shadows it. `deny ip any any` as the first line means **nothing below it is ever reached**.\n- **Wrong wildcard mask or subnet:** a rule meant to block **one host** blocks the **whole subnet** (or the reverse).\n- **Wrong interface or direction:** applying an ACL inbound instead of outbound, or on the wrong interface, filters the wrong traffic.\n- **Blocking ICMP:** ping and traceroute fail even though real traffic works, which can **mislead troubleshooting**.\n- **Firewall default deny:** a new firewall drops expected traffic and logs show it hitting the **default deny** rule → add or correct an **allow rule above** it.\n\nScenario: routing between two VLANs is correct, but traffic between them fails → suspect an **ACL or firewall rule**.\n\n## Route selection and routing tables\n\nA router forwards each packet using its **routing table**. When several routes match, it chooses in this order:\n\n1. **Longest prefix match:** the **most specific** route wins (a /24 beats a /16 beats the /0 default route).\n2. **Administrative distance (AD):** if prefixes are equal length, the most **trusted source** wins: connected 0, static 1, eBGP 20, OSPF 110, RIP 120.\n3. **Metric:** within the same routing protocol, the lowest metric (cost, hop count) wins.\n\n**Default route** (0.0.0.0/0, the **gateway of last resort**) catches everything with no more specific match. Branch routers usually send internet traffic this way.\n\n| Problem | Symptom |\n|---|---|\n| **Missing route** and no default route | Packets **dropped**; \"destination unreachable\" |\n| **Wrong or missing default route** | **Internal traffic works**, but internet or remote traffic fails |\n| **Routing loop** (e.g. two static routes pointing at each other) | Traceroute shows the **same hops repeating** until the **TTL expires** |\n| **Missing return route** | A can reach B, but B has **no path back** (data center can't reach a remote subnet) |\n| **Asymmetric routing** | Return traffic takes a different path, **bypassing the stateful firewall** that saw the outbound session, so it's dropped |\n| **Static route with the wrong mask** | Part of the intended network takes the wrong path or fails |\n| **Wrong more-specific route** redistributed | **Longest prefix match** pulls traffic to the wrong place (route hijacking) |\n| **Neighbor flapping** (OSPF and others) | Routes appear and disappear; caused by mismatched timers, area, MTU, authentication, or an unstable link |\n\n**Other routing-related problems:**\n- **Overlapping subnets:** a VPN user's home network uses the same range as the office (e.g. both 192.168.1.0/24), so corporate resources are unreachable.\n- **NAT/PAT table exhausted:** **new** outbound connections fail while **existing** ones keep working.\n- **Missing inbound NAT/port forward** after a firewall replacement: users reach the internet, but outsiders can't reach the public web server.\n- **Traceroute shows `* * *`** after a certain hop: a missing route on the next router, or a firewall blocking the traffic or ICMP.\n\n## Address pool exhaustion\n\nA DHCP **scope** has a limited number of addresses. When every address is leased, **new** clients get nothing.\n\n- **Symptom:** **new** devices get **APIPA (169.254.x.x)** addresses, while **existing** devices keep working (they already have leases).\n- **Common cause:** **long lease times** in a busy area (guest Wi-Fi, conference rooms), so departed devices hold addresses for days.\n- **Fixes:** **shorten lease times**, **expand the scope** (or use a larger subnet), and **clear stale leases**.\n\n**Related DHCP problems:**\n- **Missing DHCP relay:** one VLAN gets no addresses while other VLANs work. The DHCP server is on another subnet and the VLAN's router interface lacks an **IP helper / DHCP relay**.\n- **Rogue DHCP server:** someone plugs in a home router. Clients get addresses from an **unexpected subnet** with the rogue's gateway and lose connectivity intermittently. Prevent with **DHCP snooping**.\n- **Two uncoordinated DHCP servers** on one subnet → conflicting or duplicate assignments.\n- **Wrong scope options:** a bad **option 3 (router)** breaks off-subnet access; a bad **option 6 (DNS)** breaks name resolution.\n- **DHCPDECLINE** messages in server logs → clients found the offered address **already in use** (a conflict).\n\n**Checking it:** `ipconfig /all` (Windows) shows the lease times and DHCP server. A **packet capture** showing Discover/Offer, or the **server logs**, proves whether the server answers.\n\n## Incorrect default gateway\n\nThe **default gateway** is the router a host sends all **off-subnet** traffic to.\n\n- **Symptom:** the host reaches **local** hosts but **nothing on other subnets** or the internet.\n- **Gateway not on the host's subnet:** e.g. a host at 192.168.1.50/24 with gateway 192.168.2.1. The host can't ARP for it, so remote traffic fails.\n- **Gateway interface shut down** or failed: same symptom; ARP for the gateway fails.\n- **DHCP handing out the wrong gateway** (option 3): every client in the scope can reach local hosts only.\n- **Multiple default gateways** on a server with two NICs: unpredictable routing and intermittent connectivity. Use **one** default gateway and static routes for other networks.\n\n**Testing it:** ping the gateway. If it fails while other local hosts answer, check the gateway address, the gateway's interface, and the ARP table.\n\n**DNS vs gateway:** if the host can **ping remote servers by IP** but **names don't resolve**, the gateway is fine; the problem is **DNS** (wrong DNS server, a bad hosts file entry, or stale **cached records** that haven't reached their TTL).\n\n## Incorrect IP address and duplicate IPs\n\n**Wrong IP address (wrong network):** a static IP from the wrong subnet means the host **can't communicate with anything** (except perhaps itself), because nothing local is on its network and it can't reach its gateway.\n\n**Duplicate IP address:** two devices use the same IP.\n- **Symptoms:** \"**IP address conflict**\" warnings and **intermittent** connectivity for both devices, as switches and hosts update ARP entries back and forth.\n- **Most common cause:** a device (often a new **printer** or server) given a **static IP inside the DHCP range**, which DHCP then also leases to someone else.\n- **Find it:** ping the IP, then `arp -a` to see which **MAC** answers; look up the MAC in the switch's MAC table to find the port.\n- **Prevent it:** **exclude** static ranges from the DHCP scope, or use **DHCP reservations**. DHCP servers can also **ping before offering** (conflict detection).\n\n**Stale ARP:** after a server's NIC is replaced, traffic to it fails until the router's **old ARP entry** times out or is cleared.\n\n**\"Destination host unreachable\" from your own IP:** the host **couldn't ARP** for the destination or its gateway, which is a local reachability problem.\n\n## Incorrect subnet mask\n\nThe subnet mask tells the host **which addresses are local** (delivered directly via ARP) and which are **remote** (sent to the gateway).\n\n- **Mask too short (too big a network):** e.g. 10.1.1.20 with **255.255.0.0** when the VLAN uses 255.255.255.0. The host thinks every 10.1.x.x address is **local**, ARPs for them directly, and **fails to reach** them because they're actually behind the gateway.\n- **Mask too long (too small a network):** the host thinks some **local** hosts are **remote** and sends their traffic to the gateway. It may work inefficiently or fail.\n- **Gateway with a different mask** than the hosts (e.g. hosts /24, router /16): routing and reachability errors for some destinations.\n\nThe result is **partial reachability**: some destinations work and some don't, which makes the mask easy to overlook.\n\n**Putting host settings together:**\n\n| Setting wrong | Typical symptom |\n|---|---|\n| **Default gateway** | Local hosts OK; **nothing off-subnet** |\n| **Subnet mask** | **Some** remote or local hosts unreachable; partial reachability |\n| **IP address** (wrong network) | **Nothing** reachable |\n| **Duplicate IP** | **Conflict** warnings; **intermittent** connectivity |\n| **DNS server** | Works **by IP**, fails **by name** |\n| **No DHCP response** | **APIPA** 169.254.x.x address |\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Pool exhaustion vs DHCP unreachable:** exhaustion = **new** clients get APIPA but **existing** ones are fine; unreachable server or missing relay = **everyone** on that VLAN fails to get an address.\n- **Rogue DHCP vs pool exhaustion:** rogue = clients get a **valid-looking address from the wrong subnet**; exhaustion = clients get **169.254.x.x**.\n- **Wrong gateway vs wrong DNS:** gateway = **can't leave the subnet** at all; DNS = **IP works, names don't**.\n- **Wrong subnet mask vs wrong IP:** mask = **partial** reachability; wrong-network IP = **nothing** works.\n- **Duplicate IP vs wrong IP:** duplicate = **intermittent** with **conflict warnings**; wrong IP = **consistently** broken.\n- **Routing loop vs missing route:** loop = traceroute **repeats the same hops** until TTL expires; missing route = packets **dropped** with \"unreachable.\"\n- **Missing return route vs asymmetric routing:** missing return = **no path back** at all; asymmetric = a path back exists but **bypasses the stateful firewall**.\n- **Root port vs designated port:** root port = **toward the root bridge** (one per switch); designated = **forwarding port for a segment**.\n- **Implicit deny vs rule order:** implicit deny = traffic **not matched** by any permit is dropped; rule order = an **earlier** rule matched first.\n- **Longest prefix match vs administrative distance:** prefix length is checked **first**; AD only breaks ties between equally specific routes.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Root bridge:** the STP reference switch with the lowest bridge ID.\n- **Bridge ID:** priority (default 32768) plus MAC address.\n- **Root guard:** stops a port's neighbor from becoming root bridge.\n- **PortFast:** puts an edge port straight into forwarding.\n- **Native VLAN:** the untagged VLAN on an 802.1Q trunk; must match on both ends.\n- **Implicit deny:** the invisible deny-all at the end of every ACL.\n- **Longest prefix match:** the most specific matching route is used.\n- **Administrative distance:** trustworthiness of a route source; lower wins.\n- **Gateway of last resort:** the default route (0.0.0.0/0).\n- **APIPA:** 169.254.x.x self-assigned address when DHCP fails.\n- **DHCP relay (IP helper):** forwards DHCP broadcasts to a server on another subnet.\n- **DHCP snooping:** blocks rogue DHCP servers on untrusted ports.\n- **DHCP reservation:** a fixed IP always given to a specific MAC.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Reach local, not remote\" → default gateway. \"Works by IP, not by name\" → DNS. \"New devices get 169.254.x.x, existing ones fine\" → DHCP scope exhausted. \"One VLAN gets no DHCP\" → missing relay. \"Same hops repeat in traceroute\" → routing loop. \"All traffic stopped after a new ACL\" → implicit deny. \"Broadcast storm and MAC flapping\" → STP loop.","5|Performance issues":"# Performance issues\n\nA network can be \"up\" and still be unusable. Performance troubleshooting is about measuring **how well** traffic flows: how much gets through, how long it takes, how consistent that delay is, and how much is lost. You'll match each symptom (\"calls sound robotic\", \"slow every morning\", \"Wi-Fi drops in the warehouse\") to the metric behind it and the fix that actually helps.\n\n## Bandwidth, throughput, and capacity\n\nThese three words get used loosely, but the exam treats them precisely.\n\n| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Bandwidth** | The **theoretical maximum** rate a link can carry | A \"1 Gbps\" Ethernet port |\n| **Throughput** | The rate **actually achieved** end to end, after overhead, errors, and retransmissions | A file copy that runs at 740 Mbps on that 1 Gbps link |\n| **Capacity** | How much load a link or device can handle **before performance degrades**, including CPU, memory, and session limits | A firewall rated for 2 Gbps, but only 600 Mbps with IPS and TLS inspection on |\n\n**Throughput is always lower than bandwidth** because of protocol headers, acknowledgments, and contention. A big gap with **no errors and low utilization** points somewhere else: a slow server or disk, a small TCP window over a long path, or an endpoint bottleneck.\n\n**Measure, don't guess:**\n- **Speed tester / iperf:** generate traffic between two points and report real throughput (iperf also reports jitter and loss with UDP). Use iperf to verify a new link really delivers its rated speed.\n- **Utilization graphs (SNMP) and flow data (NetFlow/sFlow):** show how busy a link is over time and **which hosts (\"top talkers\")** are using it.\n- **Interface status:** a gigabit link that **negotiated 100 Mbps** (bad pair, wrong cable category, or a hard-set speed) caps throughput at a tenth of what you expect.\n\n**Baselines** matter: you only know 70% utilization is abnormal if you know normal is 20%.\n\n## Congestion, contention, and bottlenecks\n\n- **Congestion:** **demand exceeds capacity**, so packets wait in queues and, when queues fill, get **dropped**.\n- **Contention:** many senders **competing for the same shared resource**: an uplink, a WAN circuit, or a Wi-Fi channel's airtime. Contention is *why* congestion happens on shared media.\n- **Bottleneck:** the **lowest-capacity point** in a path. The whole path can go no faster than its narrowest part. Classic spots: a **10G link feeding a 1G link**, a busy **uplink** shared by 48 access ports, a **WAN circuit**, or an undersized **firewall**.\n\n**Symptoms of congestion:** utilization near **100%**, rising latency, **output drops / discards** on interface counters, and slowness that follows a **time pattern** (every day at 9 a.m., every afternoon during backups).\n\n**Common culprits:**\n- **Bandwidth hogs:** one host streaming, downloading, or infected with malware. Flow analysis identifies the top talker.\n- **Scheduled jobs:** backups or updates saturating the WAN during business hours.\n- **Broadcast overhead:** a huge flat network where every host processes every broadcast. Segment it into smaller VLANs.\n- **Switching loops:** broadcast storms can saturate links and CPUs in seconds (STP prevents this).\n- **Overloaded devices:** a firewall or router with high CPU or session counts adds latency and drops connections.\n\n**Fixes, from cheapest to most expensive:**\n1. **Move or schedule** the heavy traffic (run backups off-hours).\n2. **QoS:** **classify and mark** traffic (DSCP at Layer 3, 802.1p/CoS at Layer 2), then give voice a **priority queue** (LLQ) and protect business apps.\n3. **Traffic shaping** (buffers excess traffic and sends it at a set rate, smoothing bursts) or **policing** (drops or re-marks traffic above a rate).\n4. **Upgrade the bottleneck** or add links (e.g. link aggregation).\n\n**QoS only matters when there's congestion.** On an idle link, every packet is sent immediately anyway, so QoS changes little. QoS also **doesn't create bandwidth**; it decides who waits when there isn't enough. Confirm QoS is working by checking **queue statistics** and measuring voice latency, jitter, and loss.\n\n## Latency\n\n**Latency** is the time a packet takes to get from source to destination (often measured as **round-trip time**, RTT, with ping). Total delay is the sum of four parts:\n\n| Delay type | Cause | Reduced by |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Propagation** | Distance; signals travel at a fixed fraction of light speed | Shorter path or closer server (not more bandwidth) |\n| **Serialization** | Time to put the bits on the wire | Faster links (matters on slow links) |\n| **Processing** | Device inspecting and forwarding the packet | Faster or less-loaded devices |\n| **Queuing** | Waiting in buffers behind other packets | Less congestion, QoS |\n\n**Key ideas:**\n- **More bandwidth doesn't fix propagation delay.** A geostationary satellite link adds roughly **500–600 ms round trip** no matter how fast it is. (Low-earth-orbit satellite services have much lower latency.)\n- **Bufferbloat:** oversized buffers on a congested device hold packets for a long time instead of dropping them, so latency balloons during heavy uploads or downloads.\n- **VPN hairpinning:** traffic that's sent to a distant VPN concentrator and back out to the internet takes a long detour.\n- **Overloaded devices:** high CPU on a router or firewall adds processing delay.\n- **TCP over long distances:** a single TCP flow can only have one **window** of unacknowledged data in flight per round trip, so **throughput ≤ window size ÷ RTT**. A 64 KB window over 120 ms RTT is only about 4.4 Mbps, even on an idle 1 Gbps link. Fix with **TCP window scaling**, parallel streams, or **WAN optimization**.\n\n**VoIP targets:** one-way latency **150 ms or less** (ITU-T G.114). Beyond that, people talk over each other.\n\n**Troubleshooting:** latency only at certain times → **correlate monitoring data** (utilization, errors, routing changes) with those times. One hop in a traceroute jumping to 300 ms while later hops are back to 20 ms usually means that router just **deprioritizes replies to ICMP/TTL-expired** messages, not a real problem.\n\n## Jitter\n\n**Jitter** is the **variation in delay** between packets. If packets arrive 20 ms, 90 ms, 25 ms, 140 ms apart, the jitter is high even if the average latency looks acceptable.\n\n- **Why it hurts real-time traffic:** voice and video need packets at a **steady rate**. Phones use a **jitter buffer** to smooth out small variations, but late packets beyond the buffer are discarded, causing **choppy, robotic, or clipped audio** and **frozen or pixelated video**.\n- **Doesn't hurt bulk data much:** a file download just reassembles packets and doesn't care about timing, which is why \"**VoIP is robotic but downloads are fine**\" is the classic jitter scenario.\n- **Causes:** congestion and **variable queuing**, no QoS, busy Wi-Fi (contention for airtime), and consumer broadband with bursty cross-traffic.\n- **Target:** under about **30 ms**.\n- **Fixes:** **QoS** with a priority queue for voice, reduce congestion, prefer wired over Wi-Fi for desk phones, and use Wi-Fi QoS (WMM).\n- **Measure it:** **iperf** in UDP mode, VoIP monitoring tools, or call-quality reports (MOS scores).\n\nScenario: video calls are fine in the office but choppy from home, and the home speed test is high. A speed test measures raw throughput, not consistency, so the likely culprit is **jitter or packet loss** on the home connection or Wi-Fi.\n\n## Packet loss\n\n**Packet loss** is packets that **never arrive**. Even a few percent is very noticeable.\n\n**Effects:**\n- **Real-time media** (voice, video): gaps and dropouts, because there's no time to resend. Target under about **1%**.\n- **TCP:** lost segments must be **retransmitted** and TCP **slows its sending rate**, so throughput collapses.\n\n**Causes and where to look:**\n\n| Cause | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| **Congestion** | Output drops/discards, high utilization |\n| **Bad cable, connector, or optic** | **CRC errors** and input errors on the interface |\n| **Duplex mismatch** | Late collisions on the half-duplex side, CRC errors on the other |\n| **Overloaded device** | High CPU, dropped sessions |\n| **Wireless interference or collisions** | High **retry rate**, low SNR, high channel utilization |\n| **MTU mismatch with ICMP blocked** | Small packets work, large ones silently vanish |\n\n**MTU black hole:** if a link in the path has a smaller MTU and ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" messages are blocked, **path MTU discovery** fails. Small packets (pings, logins) work but large transfers **hang**. Fix by allowing that ICMP type or clamping the **TCP MSS**.\n\n**Tools:**\n- **ping** (continuous, e.g. `ping -t` on Windows) shows loss to one destination.\n- **pathping** (Windows) and **mtr** (Linux) combine traceroute with **per-hop loss statistics**, so you can see *where* loss starts.\n- **iperf** measures throughput, jitter, and loss between two hosts.\n- **Interface counters** (`show interfaces`) show whether drops are errors or congestion.\n\n## Wireless interference and channel overlap\n\nWi-Fi is a **shared, half-duplex** medium: only one device on a channel can transmit at a time, so anything that uses the channel's airtime slows everyone.\n\n| Problem | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Co-channel interference (CCI)** | Nearby APs on the **same** channel must take turns | Channel plan, lower transmit power, narrower channels |\n| **Adjacent-channel interference (ACI)** | **Overlapping** channels, e.g. 3 and 5 in 2.4 GHz | Use only **1, 6, 11** in 2.4 GHz |\n| **Wide channels in 2.4 GHz** | A 40 MHz channel covers most of the band | Use **20 MHz** in 2.4 GHz |\n| **Non-Wi-Fi interference** | Microwaves, Bluetooth, cordless phones, baby monitors (2.4 GHz); **radar on DFS channels** (5 GHz) | Find it with a **spectrum analyzer**; move to 5/6 GHz |\n| **Hidden node** | Two clients can hear the AP but **not each other**, so they transmit at once and collide at the AP | Adjust AP placement, **RTS/CTS** |\n| **Overcrowding (high client density)** | Too many clients per AP in a meeting room or lecture hall | More APs, 5/6 GHz, band steering, airtime fairness |\n\n**Why channel overlap matters:** in 2.4 GHz, channels are only 5 MHz apart but about 20 MHz wide, so only **three don't overlap**. 5 GHz and 6 GHz have many more channels, which is why dense deployments use them with **20 or 40 MHz** widths instead of 80/160 MHz.\n\n**DFS:** some 5 GHz channels are shared with **radar**. When an AP detects radar it must **leave the channel**, briefly disconnecting clients.\n\n**Metrics a Wi-Fi tool shows:**\n- **Channel utilization:** how busy the channel is from all sources. High values (e.g. over 50%) mean contention.\n- **Retry rate:** the percentage of frames that had to be **resent**. High retries mean interference, collisions, or weak signal.\n\nScenario: the site survey shows good coverage everywhere, but throughput collapses during big meetings → **overcrowding/contention**, not coverage. A strong signal doesn't guarantee clean airtime.\n\n## Signal degradation and coverage\n\n**Signal degradation** is the weakening of RF as it travels and passes through or bounces off things.\n\n| Effect | What happens | Typical culprit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Attenuation / free-space path loss** | Signal weakens with distance | Clients far from the AP |\n| **Absorption** | Material soaks up RF | Concrete, brick, **water** (people, fish tanks), and 2.4 GHz less affected than 5/6 GHz |\n| **Reflection** | Signal bounces off a surface | **Metal** shelving, mirrors, elevators |\n| **Multipath** | Reflected copies arrive at **different times** and distort each other (older standards) | Warehouses, metal-rich spaces |\n| **Refraction / scattering** | Signal bends or breaks up | Glass, uneven surfaces |\n\n**Measuring signal:**\n- **RSSI** is received signal strength in **dBm** (negative numbers; closer to 0 is stronger). About **−67 dBm** is a common target for voice and video; around **−70 dBm** is acceptable for data; below **−80 dBm** is poor.\n- **SNR (signal-to-noise ratio)** = signal − noise, in **dB**. Signal −65 dBm with noise −90 dBm → **25 dB**. Aim for at least about **20–25 dB**. SNR describes **quality**, not just strength.\n\n**Insufficient coverage:** dead spots and weak edges cause **low data rates** (clients rate-shift down), retries, and drops. Fix with a **site survey** and **heat map**, adding or moving APs, using appropriate antennas, and designing for the band the clients actually use (5/6 GHz cells are smaller).\n\n**Legacy and low data rates:** slow clients (old 802.11b/g) and low **basic data rates** (1, 2, 5.5, 11 Mbps) take much longer to send each frame, eating airtime. **Disabling low data rates** shrinks cells, frees airtime, and pushes clients to roam sooner.\n\n**Transmit power:** cranking **every AP to maximum power** in a dense office increases co-channel interference and creates **sticky clients**, because clients can hear far-away APs but the AP can't hear the weaker client well.\n\n## Client disassociation and roaming\n\n**Client disassociation issues:** clients keep dropping off the Wi-Fi.\n- **Weak signal or interference:** the client loses the AP at the cell edge.\n- **Authentication problems:** 802.1X/RADIUS timeouts, expired certificates, or a wrong pre-shared key cause repeated disconnects.\n- **Mismatched settings:** security mode or band settings a client doesn't support (e.g. WPA3-only with older clients).\n- **AP load balancing or band steering** that's too aggressive kicks clients off.\n- **DFS radar events** force the AP to change channels.\n- **Deauthentication attacks:** forged deauth frames from an attacker (802.11w **protected management frames** helps).\n- **Power-saving drivers** on the client put the NIC to sleep.\n\n**Roaming** is a client moving from one AP to another without dropping the session. The **client** decides when to roam.\n\n| Roaming problem | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sticky client** | Client holds on to a distant AP | Lower AP power, raise minimum data rates, **802.11k/v** to guide clients |\n| **Calls drop while walking** | Full re-authentication at each AP takes too long | **802.11r fast BSS transition** (and 802.11k/v) |\n| **Session or IP changes after roaming** | APs place the SSID in **different VLANs/subnets** | Same SSID mapped to the same VLAN (or a controller that handles it) |\n| **Client won't roam at all** | APs use **different SSIDs or security settings** | Identical SSID, security, and settings on every AP |\n| **Gaps between cells** | Not enough overlap | Design about **15–20% cell overlap** |\n\n**802.11k** gives clients a list of neighbor APs, **802.11v** lets the network suggest a better AP, and **802.11r** speeds up the security handshake during the move.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Bandwidth vs throughput:** bandwidth is the **rated maximum**; throughput is what you **actually get**.\n- **Latency vs jitter:** latency is **how long** a packet takes; jitter is **how much that delay varies**. Robotic voice with fine downloads → jitter.\n- **Congestion vs bottleneck:** congestion is the **condition** (too much demand); the bottleneck is the **location** where it happens (the narrowest link).\n- **Shaping vs policing:** shaping **buffers and delays** excess traffic; policing **drops or re-marks** it.\n- **Co-channel vs adjacent-channel interference:** co-channel = **same** channel, APs take turns; adjacent = **overlapping** channels, frames get corrupted.\n- **RSSI vs SNR:** RSSI is **raw strength**; SNR is strength **compared to the noise floor**, the better quality measure.\n- **Wi-Fi analyzer vs spectrum analyzer:** a Wi-Fi analyzer sees **802.11 networks**; a spectrum analyzer sees **all RF energy**, including microwaves.\n- **Coverage problem vs capacity problem:** weak signal in an area → coverage; good signal but slow when crowded → capacity/contention.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Bottleneck:** the lowest-capacity point in a path.\n- **Contention:** multiple senders competing for the same link or airtime.\n- **Bufferbloat:** excessive buffering that causes high latency under load.\n- **Jitter buffer:** a buffer in phones that smooths out variation in packet arrival.\n- **QoS:** classifying, marking, and prioritizing traffic during congestion.\n- **LLQ:** low-latency queuing, a strict priority queue for voice.\n- **Retry rate:** the percentage of wireless frames retransmitted.\n- **Channel utilization:** how busy a wireless channel is.\n- **DFS:** dynamic frequency selection; 5 GHz channels shared with radar.\n- **Sticky client:** a client that won't roam to a closer AP.\n- **802.11k/v/r:** neighbor reports, network-assisted transitions, and fast roaming.\n- **PMTUD black hole:** large packets silently dropped because ICMP is blocked.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Robotic VoIP but downloads fine\" → jitter, fix with QoS. \"Slow every day at 9 a.m.\" → congestion at peak; check utilization. \"Microwave kills Wi-Fi at lunch\" → 2.4 GHz interference; use a spectrum analyzer. \"Fast link but slow transfer over a long distance\" → latency and the TCP window. \"Calls drop when walking between APs\" → roaming; use 802.11r.","5|Tools & protocols":"# Tools & protocols\n\nTroubleshooting goes faster when you reach for the right tool first. This lesson covers the software tools (packet capture, command-line utilities, scanners, discovery protocols), the device `show` commands, and the hardware tools CompTIA expects you to match to a symptom.\n\n## Protocol analyzers and packet capture\n\nA **protocol analyzer** (packet analyzer) such as **Wireshark** or **tcpdump** captures packets and **decodes every header**, so you can see exactly what was sent, what came back, and where a conversation fails. It's the tool of last resort when other tests disagree, and the tool of proof when you need evidence.\n\n**Getting the traffic:** a switch only sends a port the traffic meant for it, so to capture someone else's traffic you need:\n- **Port mirroring (SPAN):** the switch copies traffic from one or more ports or VLANs to the port where your analyzer sits. Cheap and flexible, but the switch can drop copies when busy.\n- **Network TAP:** a hardware device placed **inline** on a link that copies **every** frame (including errored ones) to a monitor port. More reliable, but you have to break the link briefly to install it.\n\n**Wireshark display filters** (applied after capture to hide what you don't need): `dns`, `http`, `tcp.port == 443`, `ip.addr == 10.1.1.5`, `tcp.analysis.retransmission`.\n\n**tcpdump** is a command-line capture tool (Linux/macOS). It uses **capture filters** (BPF syntax) that decide what gets recorded: `tcpdump -i eth0 host 10.0.0.5 and port 53`. Use `-w capture.pcap` to **write a file** for later analysis in Wireshark.\n\n**What to look for in a capture:**\n\n| Capture shows | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **SYN, SYN, SYN** with no reply | Host unreachable or a **firewall silently dropping** traffic |\n| **SYN answered by RST** | Host is up but **nothing is listening** on that port (or a firewall rejecting) |\n| Many **retransmissions** / duplicate ACKs | **Packet loss** somewhere in the path |\n| DNS query with no response | DNS server unreachable or down |\n| DHCP Discover with no Offer | DHCP server or relay problem |\n| TLS handshake fails after Client Hello | Certificate or protocol/cipher mismatch |\n\nCapturing traffic can expose passwords and personal data, so do it **only with authorization** and protect capture files.\n\n## Connectivity and path testing\n\n**ping** sends **ICMP echo requests** and reports replies and round-trip time. It answers \"can I reach it, and how fast?\"\n- Windows options: `-t` (continuous until stopped), `-n` (count), `-l` (packet size), `-f` (**don't fragment**). `ping -f -l 1472 host` tests whether a 1500-byte packet fits (1472 data + 28 bytes of headers).\n- Linux equivalent MTU test: `ping -M do -s 1472 host`. Linux pings continuously by default; `-c` sets a count.\n- No reply doesn't always mean down: many firewalls **block ICMP**.\n\n**traceroute** (Linux/macOS) and **tracert** (Windows) list **every router hop** to a destination by sending probes with **TTL 1, 2, 3...**. Each router that decrements TTL to zero sends back an ICMP \"time exceeded,\" revealing itself.\n- **tracert (Windows) uses ICMP echo**; **traceroute (Linux) uses UDP** by default (with `-I` for ICMP or `-T` for TCP).\n- `* * *` means a hop didn't reply, which may just be a filter. Where the trace **stops** is where to look.\n- A single slow middle hop with fast hops after it is usually just that router deprioritizing replies.\n\n**Combined tools:**\n- **pathping** (Windows) and **mtr** (Linux): traceroute plus **per-hop loss and latency statistics** over time.\n- **tracepath** (Linux): traces the path and **discovers the path MTU** without needing root.\n\n**Testing a specific port:** ping only proves ICMP works. To check that a service port is open, use **Test-NetConnection server -Port 443** (PowerShell), **nc -zv server 443** (netcat), or `telnet server 443` in a pinch.\n\n## Host configuration commands\n\n| Task | Windows | Linux |\n|---|---|---|\n| Show IP, mask, gateway, DNS, MAC | `ipconfig /all` | `ip addr` (older: `ifconfig`) |\n| Renew DHCP lease | `ipconfig /release` then `/renew` | `dhclient` (or the network manager) |\n| View / clear DNS cache | `ipconfig /displaydns`, `/flushdns` | `resolvectl` (systemd) |\n| IP-to-MAC table | `arp -a` | `ip neigh` (or `arp -n`) |\n| Connections and listening ports | `netstat -ano` | `ss -tulnp` (older: `netstat`) |\n| Routing table, default gateway | `route print` | `ip route` |\n| Computer's name | `hostname` | `hostname` |\n\n**What each helps you find:**\n- **ipconfig / ip addr:** a **169.254.x.x (APIPA)** address means DHCP failed; a wrong mask or gateway explains \"can reach local hosts but not remote ones.\"\n- **arp -a:** two IPs showing the **same MAC** can reveal **ARP spoofing**; a duplicate IP shows a MAC you don't expect. An entry for the gateway proves Layer 2 reachability to it.\n- **netstat -ano:** `-a` all connections, `-n` numeric addresses, `-o` the **owning process ID**, so you can spot unexpected listeners or malware connections. GUI alternatives: **Resource Monitor** or **TCPView**.\n- **route print / ip route:** a missing or wrong **default route** explains traffic that never leaves the subnet.\n- **Other Windows tools:** **nbtstat** shows NetBIOS over TCP/IP name tables (legacy name resolution); **netsh** configures network settings and the firewall (e.g. `netsh wlan show interfaces`, `netsh advfirewall`).\n\n## DNS lookup tools\n\nWhen \"it works by IP but not by name,\" the problem is name resolution, and these tools test DNS directly.\n\n- **nslookup** (Windows, Linux, macOS): `nslookup www.example.com` uses your configured DNS server; `nslookup www.example.com 8.8.8.8` queries a **specific server**, which tells you whether your internal DNS or the record itself is the problem. Use `set type=mx` (or other types) to query specific records.\n- **dig** (Linux/macOS): more detailed output, including **TTLs, flags, and the answering server**. `dig example.com MX`, `dig @8.8.8.8 example.com`, and `dig +trace example.com` to follow the delegation from the root down.\n\n**Typical uses:**\n- Confirm a hostname **resolves to the expected IP** after a change.\n- Compare answers from two servers to spot **stale records** or propagation delays (check the TTL).\n- An **NXDOMAIN** answer means the name doesn't exist on that server; a **timeout** means the server isn't responding.\n\n## Discovery, scanning, and speed tools\n\n- **nmap:** discovers live hosts, **open ports**, running services and versions, and operating systems. The default privileged scan is the **SYN (\"half-open\") scan, `-sS`**: it sends a SYN, notes the reply, and never completes the handshake. `-sT` is a full connect scan, `-sU` scans UDP, `-sn` is a ping sweep with no port scan, `-sV` detects service versions. **Only scan networks you're authorized to scan.**\n- **LLDP and CDP:** Layer 2 **discovery protocols** where devices advertise their name, model, port, IP, and capabilities to directly connected neighbors. **LLDP is vendor-neutral** (IEEE 802.1AB); **CDP is Cisco-proprietary**. Use them to map \"which switch port is this AP plugged into?\" or to find a mis-patched link.\n- **Speed tester:** a web or app-based test of throughput (and usually latency) to an internet server. Good for checking an ISP's delivered speed, but it doesn't test your internal links or measure consistency well.\n- **iperf:** runs a **client and server on two endpoints** you control and measures maximum TCP or UDP **throughput**, plus **jitter and loss** in UDP mode. Best for verifying a specific internal link (e.g. a new 10 Gbps link).\n\nScenario: users can't reach a new web server on port 443. ping works. `Test-NetConnection -Port 443` fails, and nmap shows 443 as **filtered** → a firewall is blocking it. If nmap shows it **closed**, the host is reachable but the service isn't running.\n\n## Network device commands\n\nCompTIA lists these Cisco-style commands; other vendors have equivalents.\n\n| Command | Shows | Use it when |\n|---|---|---|\n| `show interfaces` (`show interface`) | Status, speed/duplex, **counters**: CRC, input errors, collisions, drops | Suspecting a bad cable, duplex mismatch, or congestion |\n| `show ip interface brief` | Each interface's IP and **up/down** status at a glance | Quick health check |\n| `show interfaces status` | Speed, duplex, VLAN, **err-disabled** ports | Port won't come up |\n| `show mac address-table` | Which **MAC was learned on which port** and VLAN | Finding where a device is plugged in |\n| `show vlan` (`show vlan brief`) | VLANs and their **assigned access ports** | User in the wrong VLAN |\n| `show interfaces trunk` | Trunk ports, **allowed and native VLANs** | VLAN traffic not crossing a link |\n| `show ip route` (show route) | The **routing table** | Remote network unreachable |\n| `show arp` (`show ip arp`) | The device's **IP-to-MAC cache** | Duplicate IPs, gateway issues |\n| `show running-config` (show config) | The **active configuration** | Verifying settings or a recent change |\n| `show power inline` (show power) | **PoE** budget and power per port | Phone or AP not powering up |\n| `show cdp neighbors` / `show lldp neighbors` | Directly connected devices, model, port | Mapping the topology |\n| `show spanning-tree` | Root bridge, port roles and states | Loops or blocked ports |\n| `show logging` | Messages in the log buffer | Finding what happened and when |\n| `show ip dhcp binding` | Leased IPs and the MACs they went to | DHCP scope exhaustion or conflicts |\n| `show ip dhcp snooping binding` | MAC, IP, VLAN, and port bindings learned by snooping | DHCP snooping, DAI, and IP source guard issues |\n\n## Hardware tools\n\n| Tool | What it does | Typical scenario |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cable tester** | Checks **wire map**: opens, shorts, reversed, crossed, and split pairs | A newly crimped cable doesn't work |\n| **Cable certifier** | Proves a run meets its **category spec** (length, attenuation, NEXT) | Documenting a new install, or proving a run is within the 100 m limit |\n| **TDR** | Finds the **distance to a fault** on copper | Where is the break in this cable? |\n| **OTDR** | Finds the distance to breaks, bends, and bad splices on **fiber** | Locating a fiber break inside a wall or conduit |\n| **Visual fault locator (VFL)** | Shines a **bright red laser** into fiber; light leaks at breaks and sharp bends | Finding a break or tracing a fiber in a short run or patch panel |\n| **Optical power meter / light meter** | Measures received light level (dBm) and loss | Fiber link up but erroring; is the signal too weak? |\n| **Fiber inspection scope** | Magnifies the connector end face | Check for dirt before connecting |\n| **Toner and probe** | Puts a tone on a wire; the inductive probe finds it at the other end | Which cable in the bundle or which patch panel port? |\n| **Network TAP** | Inline copy of all traffic to an analyzer | Capturing traffic reliably |\n| **Loopback plug** | Feeds a port's transmit back to its receive | Proving a NIC or switch port works |\n| **Multimeter / PoE tester** | Measures voltage; a PoE tester shows whether and how much PoE is present | AP won't power up |\n| **Punchdown tool** | Terminates wires into **110 blocks**, keystone jacks, and patch panels | Re-terminating a damaged jack |\n| **Crimper** | Attaches RJ45 plugs to cable | Making a patch cable |\n| **Fusion splicer** | **Melts two fiber strands together** for a permanent, low-loss joint | Repairing a cut fiber |\n\n## Wi-Fi analyzers and spectrum analyzers\n\n- **Wi-Fi analyzer:** decodes **802.11** frames and shows **SSIDs, BSSIDs, channels, channel width, RSSI and SNR**, security settings, and channel utilization. Use it for **site surveys, heat maps, channel planning**, and spotting **rogue APs**.\n- **Spectrum analyzer:** shows **raw RF energy** across a band, whether or not it's Wi-Fi. It's the only way to see **non-Wi-Fi interference** such as microwaves, Bluetooth, cordless phones, video senders, or jammers.\n\nA Wi-Fi analyzer can tell you **which network** is on channel 6 but not that a microwave is flooding it. A spectrum analyzer shows the microwave's energy but can't tell you SSIDs.\n\n## Common mix-ups\n\n- **Cable tester vs cable certifier:** a tester checks **wiring/continuity**; a certifier proves the run **meets the category's performance spec**.\n- **TDR vs OTDR:** TDR = **copper**; OTDR = **fiber**. Both report **distance to a fault**.\n- **OTDR vs visual fault locator:** an OTDR measures distance over long runs; a VFL is a **red light** you see leaking at a break, good for short runs.\n- **Toner vs cable tester:** a toner **finds** a cable; a tester **checks** it.\n- **SPAN vs TAP:** SPAN is a **switch feature** that copies traffic (may drop under load); a TAP is **inline hardware** that copies everything.\n- **Display filter vs capture filter:** display filters **hide** already-captured packets (Wireshark); capture filters decide what's **recorded** at all (tcpdump/BPF).\n- **tracert vs traceroute:** Windows tracert uses **ICMP**; Linux traceroute uses **UDP** by default.\n- **netstat vs nbtstat:** netstat shows **connections and ports**; nbtstat shows **NetBIOS names**.\n- **CDP vs LLDP:** CDP is **Cisco-only**; LLDP is the **open standard**.\n- **Wi-Fi analyzer vs spectrum analyzer:** Wi-Fi analyzer sees **networks**; spectrum analyzer sees **all RF energy**.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Protocol analyzer:** software that captures and decodes packets (Wireshark, tcpdump).\n- **SPAN:** switch port mirroring.\n- **TAP:** inline hardware that copies all traffic on a link.\n- **TTL:** the hop counter that traceroute increments to reveal each router.\n- **APIPA:** a 169.254.x.x address a host assigns itself when DHCP fails.\n- **NXDOMAIN:** a DNS response meaning the name doesn't exist.\n- **SYN scan:** nmap's half-open scan that never completes the handshake.\n- **OTDR:** optical time-domain reflectometer.\n- **VFL:** visual fault locator, a red laser for finding fiber breaks.\n- **Wire map:** a test of which pin connects to which on a cable.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Ping works by IP, not name\" → nslookup/dig. \"Find which cable in the bundle\" → toner and probe. \"Locate a fiber break\" → OTDR (or VFL for short runs). \"Microwave interference\" → spectrum analyzer. \"Which port is that MAC on?\" → `show mac address-table`. \"Phone won't power on\" → `show power inline` or a PoE tester."};
